package starlarkfrontend

import (
	"fmt"
	binaryapi "github.com/tinyrange/trex/binary"
	"strings"
)

// Each native entry point needs an explicit contract. Do not replace missing
// descriptions with boilerplate: generation must fail until the API is documented.
var nativeStarlarkDescriptions = map[string]string{
	"windows.api_set_namespace":          "Decodes a raw v6 API-set namespace (.apiset section bytes or a file slice), limited to 16 MiB. Accepts exactly one positional/data or file argument. Also supports the immutable inspection mapping keys version, flags, hash_factor and contracts (with ordered alias/host dictionaries), and a contracts attribute. Returns version, flags, hash_factor, entries and resolve(contract, importer=\"\"). Entries preserve flags, UTF-16 hashed_length and ordered importer/host records. resolve uses the validated hashed prefix excluding the final revision, then selects an exact importer override or value-zero fallback; it does not guess nearest versions. Names are bounded ASCII UTF-16 module basenames; unresolved or empty hosts return None. Rejects unsupported versions, malformed offsets/counts/names, duplicate contracts/importers and ambiguous or inconsistent hash indexes. The result owns its parsed data. Does not load DLLs, invent hosts, initialize modules, implement their exports or confer trust.",
	"windows.cdf_filemap_transition":     "Computes a bounded no-hash FileMap ownership transition without mutating inputs. document has the cdf_filemap schema (empty entries/identities represent a new map). Each node requires name, operation, state, directory_flags, file_flags, before_identity and after_identity; identities are None or lists of namespace/name/value tuples. Names are immediate ASCII leaves; dot denotes the directory. States must be post-preliminary-file-pass native states, not raw manifest declarations. Files compare existing ownership against the new family ignoring version; dot uses the old family. Unmentioned entries survive. Returns document and directory_collisions (retained_identity/requested_identity pairs). A duplicate dot retains the resident owner and is reported, not silently overwritten; transaction-level diagnostic handling remains separate. Unsupported identities and unresolved file-family conflicts fail closed. Empty documents do not authorize deletion and cannot be encoded. Does not select dirty directories, normalize root paths, mutate image files/security, authenticate packages or commit servicing.",
	"windows.cdf_filemap_encode":         "Rebuilds the supported no-hash FileMap dictionary returned by cdf_filemap. Interns UTF-8 strings, prunes unused identities and remaps references, preserves resolved flags/names/attributes, sorts native 32-bit name hashes while preserving equal-hash input order, and emits a checksummed CDF. IDs and physical bytes may differ from input. Uses the same bounds and rejected variants as cdf_filemap; empty maps fail rather than implying deletion. No ownership selection, root-path normalization, image mutation or authentication is performed; callers must supply the complete intended records and correct root selector.",
	"windows.cdf_filemap_name":           "Derives the native CSI FileMaps leaf filename from a canonical absolute image path with slash separators. windows_root defaults to /Windows and must be a non-root canonical image directory. Supports printable ASCII paths of at most 32767 bytes; rejects drive prefixes, backslashes, empty/dot components, trailing separators and Unicode rather than approximating Windows folding. Uses the Windows-relative $$ prefix, native filtering/collapse and 100-character bound, and uppercase CSI hashing when required. Drive root maps to _0000000000000000.cdf-ms. Does not inspect the image, choose ownership or mutate any files.",
	"windows.cdf_filemap_path":           "Computes native FileMap path/root_selector metadata for a canonical ASCII image path and an explicit drive designator such as C:. Windows-root matching uses the native case-insensitive literal prefix, not a path-component boundary. Image root emits an absolute NT path with selector 0; Windows-relative paths use 1 and other volume-relative paths use 2. Shares cdf_filemap_name path bounds and rejects noncanonical paths. Does not mutate a map or image.",
	"windows.cdf_filemap":                "Decodes the observed version-one CSI FileMap no-hash schema from CDF bytes. Returns path, root_selector, identities (ordered lists of namespace/name/value tuples), and entries (name, display_name, flags, identity index). Validates container checksum, schema GUID, table framing, key hashes, string/blob/identity references and duplicate keys. Preserves native flags without authorizing filesystem effects. Unsupported compact tables, hashed records, unknown field bits, non-ASCII lookup keys or other schema variants fail closed. Bound to 64 MiB container/expanded payload, 1048576 records/total attributes, and 1024 attributes per identity. Does not decide ownership transitions, apply updates or authenticate contents.",
	"windows.cdf_decode":                 "Parses version-one PcmH compiled data container bytes. Returns a mutable dictionary with flags, format (16 bytes), root, auxiliary_root, strings, identities, appids, guids, and blobs. The three ID indexes refer to zero-based blobs; GUIDs are inline 16-byte values; blobs are (kind, opaque bytes) tuples. 0xffffffff denotes an absent root. Validates the noncryptographic IHA8 checksum, extents and outer references; does not validate nested-table semantics, decode strings, authenticate contents, or decide FileMap ownership. Input/expanded payload bounded to 64 MiB and each record list to 1048576. No host parsing or writes.",
	"windows.cdf_encode":                 "Serializes the exact nine-field dictionary returned by cdf_decode, preserving blob/index IDs and opaque data. Four-byte aligns payloads and recomputes IHA8; padding, physical ordering and shared extents need not match input. Validates integer ranges, extents and outer references, not nested payload semantics or FileMap servicing policy. Bounds match cdf_decode. Returns bytes without image mutation or host intermediates.",
	"windows.cbs_parent_states":          "Evaluates CBS parent states without modifying an image. packages is a list of dicts with manifest (file or bytes), comparisons (one runtime descriptor per parsed parent block in preorder), and optional standalone_satisfied (default false; caller-resolved package/session bypass). Each descriptor requires major_minor, build and revision operators (EQ/NE/GE/GT/LE/LT) and an explicit ignore_revision boolean. candidates is an effective-intent snapshot of dicts with canonical package identity string in package, intended_state, and explicit baseline boolean from the CBS Baseline attribute. Pending/session overrides must already be resolved. Returns one integer parent state per input package; no-parent is the 0x1000 unspecified sentinel, not installed. Exact/range version lookup, staged-Baseline descending revision relaxation, and nested parent constraints are evaluated separately from owner intents. This does not populate runtime descriptors, infer installed state from MUM presence, compute final CurrentState, supersede packages, or write registry records. Lists are bounded to 65536 entries; manifests to 32 MiB each and 256 MiB total. Invalid inputs raise errors without partial results. No host tools, guest execution or network access.",
	"windows.cbs_manifest":               "Parses one CBS MUM into an immutable reusable value (32 MiB maximum). Accepted wherever CBS parent queries, update indexes, package-owner planning or deployment-source encoding accept a manifest. Reuses parsing only: comparison descriptors, candidates, intended states and aggregate byte bounds are still evaluated per call. Identity-validated update metadata files reuse their owned parse without rereading XML. No global cache, file writes or servicing effects.",
	"windows.cbs_parent_update_scopes":   "Selects available parent packages for CBS update-name queries without modifying an image. packages is a list of dicts with manifest (file or bytes) and comparisons (one explicit runtime descriptor per parent block in preorder). Descriptors require major_minor, build, revision (EQ/NE/GE/GT/LE/LT) and ignore_revision. candidates is a list of canonical package identity strings. Returns one sorted, deduplicated list of matching package identities per input. Exact top-level references use exact lookup even when revision is ignored; ranged top-level references seed related families, then any root or nested rule can accept a candidate. Nested-only families do not seed candidates. Missing packages are skipped. This is not parent-state reduction: no state filter, disposition, standalone bypass or staged-Baseline revision relaxation applies. It does not enumerate updates, reproduce native release-type ordering, populate runtime descriptors, or perform a servicing transaction. Lists are bounded to 131072 inputs and 1048576 total results; manifests to 32 MiB each and 256 MiB total. Errors return no partial results. No host tools, registry writes or network access.",
	"windows.csi_winner_change":          "Analyzes one exact CSI servicing family after installed-mark updates. identity is full canonical CSI bytes; current is the effective winning version or an empty string. installed is complete final installed version membership. changes is a list of dict(version, installed, impactful_install) for affected versions. Returns winner and shadowed strings; both empty means unchanged, an empty winner with a shadowed version means clear the effective winner. Preserves surviving winners unless a strictly higher impactful install replaces them, and falls back to the highest remaining installed member only on removal. Ordinary SxS identities are restricted to their major/minor branch; NonSxS and policy identities span branches. Does not rebuild untouched branch defaults or write state.",
	"windows.csi_composition":            "Calculates a bounded exact CSI composition, following only explicit install dependencies. resolve(identity_bytes) must return the exact decoded component manifest as a file, string, bytes or immutable cbs_manifest value; None or a foreign identity fails. Parsed values can be shared across compositions without bypassing identity, dependency or work-limit checks. Returns records with canonical identity bytes and disposition (1 patch, 2 install). Omitted dependency types, prerequisites and runtime bindings do not introduce owners. Preserves native breadth-first impact propagation and rejects missing install edges, cycles exceeding native depth 128 and work-limit exhaustion. Manifest reads are bounded at 64 MiB; work_limit ranges from 1 to 4194304. Does not decide package applicability, source activation, final installed marks, winners or driver-store effects, and does not write state.",
	"windows.csi_identity_hash":          "Hashes bounded full COMPONENTS identity/appid bytes using the native non-cryptographic CSI identity discriminator, returning an unsigned 64-bit integer. omit_version=True selects the version-independent family hash used by component-family and SOFTWARE winner key suffixes; type, versionScope, architecture and culture remain significant. This differs from CBS update-analyzer family matching. Only the validated ASCII identity alphabet is supported. Does not reconstruct an identity from an abbreviated path, select a winning version, infer activation or write registry state.",
	"windows.csi_winner_family_key":      "Formats a SOFTWARE SideBySide/Winners family key from bounded full CSI identity bytes. Native abbreviation scans both ends; type and versionScope affect the hash but not the readable prefix. Validates ASCII identities, canonical named architectures and 16-hex-digit tokens; rejects unsupported typeName and Unicode rather than guessing. Does not choose winners or write state.",
	"windows.csi_source_plan":            "Plans ordered value-only CBS p!/i!/s! source edits for one deployment. before is the complete source inventory as dicts (name, type, data); changes contains dicts (kind, delete, data), with native kind 1 pinned, 2 installed, or 4 staged. Data must be exact serialized bytes. Existing adds preserve their opaque payload even when the requested secondary differs; deletion requires presence. Returns edits (name, delete, type, data), before and after counts (pinned, installed, staged). Rejects non-CBS sources, mismatched names, malformed data and invalid masks atomically. Bounds: one million entries per list, 64 KiB per payload, 64 MiB total payload. Does not choose operations or apply writes: staged marks require successful staging or the separate native pending-content protocol, not merely a queued stage action. Counts do not authorize backlink deletion or activation.",
	"windows.csi_winner_plan":            "Plans value-only SOFTWARE Winners edits from a full CSI identity, actual current winner, complete baseline membership before, and affected-version dicts (version, installed, impactful_install). Derives final membership and winner choice using native semantics. Returns family, winner, shadowed, edits; each edit has branch, name, delete, type and data. Empty branch means family root; empty name means default. Nondelete data is raw REG_SZ or REG_BINARY. Deletions remove individual values only, never keys or unrelated branch defaults. This is not complete servicing and applies no writes.",
	"windows.csi_deployment_key":         "Encodes complete bounded CSI identity bytes as the native canonical COMPONENTS deployment key, preserving architecture, culture, type and version-scope hash distinctions. Not a WinSxS directory name; malformed or unsupported identities fail instead of approximating.",
	"windows.csi_file_source_key":        "Returns the ordinary CSI FileInfo intern key as (name, culture, architecture, token, type, version_scope, relative_name, version_family). Identity is canonical bytes bounded to 64 KiB; compared fields must be printable ASCII. Relative names may contain backslash-separated nonempty components, but not slash, colon, dot or dot-dot components. Absent versionScope retains major/minor; nonSxS ignores version; other scopes fail. This is not an exact payload identity, content hash or permission to publish a file.",
	"windows.csi_identity":               "Decodes bounded canonical COMPONENTS identity/appid bytes, preserving name, version, language, processor_architecture, public_key_token, type and version_scope. Rejects malformed, unknown or repeated attributes; does not infer identity from a lossy registry key or activate components.",
	"windows.cbs_deployment_source_data": "Serializes a native two-length CBS deployment source payload from an exact package manifest and update name. The manifest may be a file, string or bytes, bounded at 64 MiB. Preserves the declared update spelling. Secondary bytes encode the first applicable updateComponent analyzer flags as decimal: the elevation mask plus bit 0 for multiple assemblyIdentity targets; absent analyzer entries yield empty secondary data. Direct updateComponent declarations precede nested applicability groups, independently of XML interleaving. Selectability does not supply source flags. Unknown elevations and missing or ambiguous updates fail. This formats data only; it does not install, stage, write state or replace existing opaque source payloads.",
	"windows.cbs_deployment_source":      "Decodes a bounded native two-length canonical CSI CBS source payload. Returns package, update, primary, opaque secondary bytes and source_key. Secondary data does not participate in source identity. Validates framing before slicing; inspection does not imply activation or interpret secondary bytes as an elevation.",
	"windows.cbs_component_owners":       "Reduces explicit component-analyzer owner changes for one resolved version. installed and pinned are lists of dict(source=string_or_None, elevation=int); changes adds action=pin, unpin, install or uninstall and requires a string source. None is a native RTM placeholder, distinct from an empty string. Source names are case-sensitive; elevation is part of owner identity. Pass the complete ordered transaction prefix against the original baseline: identical source/elevation/action events are deduplicated in first-seen order, not replayed. Additions preserve existing owners; removal requires the exact source/elevation or consumes one anonymous RTM placeholder, otherwise fails. Returns a record with installed and pinned lists of source/elevation dicts; only installed elevations feed cbs_update_index.with_components. No inventory loading, RTM synthesis, package scheduling, winner selection or registry writes. Bounds: 1048576 combined owners/events, 64 KiB per source and 64 MiB combined source text. Unknown actions/elevations, malformed text, or missing removals fail with no partial result.",
	"windows.cbs_parent_relationships":   "Tests native CBS IsParent relationships against explicit runtime version descriptors. Uses the same manifest/comparisons dicts and canonical candidate-name list as cbs_parent_update_scopes, but every root and nested rule participates, including nested-only families. EQ descriptors honor ignore_revision instead of performing exact lookup. Returns sorted unique matching candidate identities for each package. No state, disposition, standalone or Baseline relaxation is applied. This predicate does not reproduce reverse-index order, populate a watch list, authorize admission, or implement a servicing transaction. Bounds and all-or-error behavior match cbs_parent_update_scopes; no host tools or registry writes.",
	"windows.cbs_update_index":           "Builds a read-only immutable CBS update-state snapshot. packages and editions use the same explicit contexts as cbs_package_owners, but intended_state also accepts resolved absent (0), resolved (0x20) and permanent (0x80) states. Returns a record with state(package, update), yielding raw_state and intended_state (capped by the containing package), and with_states(states), yielding an independent snapshot after replacing the targets in a canonical-package-name to state dict. All dependency memo entries are invalidated in the new snapshot, including transitive detections; old snapshots remain unchanged. Parent scopes, editions, choices, explicitly published targets and parsed manifests are retained. with_updates(package, states) creates a new snapshot with explicit published update targets (update-name to resolved state dict); 0x1000 clears an override. state prefers these targets even when they carry an earlier containing-package cap. applicable(package, update) evaluates metadata for that update instead, while detectors still honor published dependency targets. Neither query publishes its result. with_components(components) replaces the effective component-analyzer snapshot for updateComponent predicates and invalidates derived applicability, preserving published targets. Each entry requires identity (canonical CSI UTF-8 bytes) and owners (effective installed-owner elevation DWORDs, not package states). Purely staged owners must be excluded and transaction overlays resolved by the caller. Missing snapshots fail; an explicit empty snapshot is distinct. Supported elevate values are revision, distribution, build, version and install. Same-family strictly older components supply evidence; architecture, culture, type and versionScope remain distinct. Only nonSxS families discard the entire version; other scopes retain major/minor, preventing activation from a different side-by-side version family. This does not load ownership from a hive, update analyzer execution overlays or choose CSI winners. Component inputs are bounded to 1048576 entries/owner words and 64 MiB identity bytes. This does not model the separate native applicability-result cache or its invalidation policy. Rebuild with cbs_update_index if parent/selection/edition policies change. Pending/unspecified targets, duplicate case-insensitive changes and unknown packages are rejected. package_states() returns an independent canonical-package-name to target dict, including unadmitted inventory. package_state(package) queries one exact target without copying the inventory; missing contexts are errors, not inferred absent states. ordinary_population(roots, store_owners, applicability, populate_mum, work_limit, omitted_references=[], watch=..., allow_absent_references=False, store_loaded_packages=[], lcu_session=False) evaluates only an explicitly selected ordinary population prefix. store_loaded_packages supplies exact contexts resolved from the installed store (native source kind -1); only their ordinary deployment references may ignore a not-found child at nonzero target. It does not permit missing roots/MUM requests or grant this policy to new sources merely sharing a family. lcu_session explicitly models native LCU task flags and requires a Package_for_RollupFix root requested installed or staged; only not-found deployment children become optional. The returned unresolved_references records parent, package identity, update, intended_state and reason for every optional not-found lookup; these records are not admissions or content-installation evidence. Each root/request is a dict(package, owner, state, integration, ceiling), optionally preserve_store_owner, bypass_owners and skip_mum; store owners are dict(package, owner, state, integration). applicability(package, snapshot) must return a resolved state. populate_mum(package, snapshot, action) must return dict(before_updates=[requests], deferred=[requests]); both callbacks must be pure and explicitly resolve missing policy. MUM actions 2/3 require supersedence policy; 4/5/6 require lower-version reinstall policy. Initial unchanged installed items select action 3. A merge uses the previous target after completed MUM processing, otherwise the original state, then clears the processed flag. MUM processing is not once per item; it rechecks reinstall after immediate supersedence recursion. Immediate MUM requests precede update publication; deferred requests follow all roots/children. Updates publish in manifest order before depth-first child admission. Named execution requests replace state and retain first integration; dirty normalizes only superseded to staged. Returns index, executions (package, intended_state, owners, updates, update_starts, action, mum_populated, mum_processed, mum_passes, dirty, dirty_updates, watch_processed), and admissions. Unknown referenced packages fail unless explicitly omitted. allow_absent_references=True permits an unavailable reference only when its containing update target is absent; nonzero targets still fail. This models the native target-zero optional resolve path, not the broader session-flag policy; work_limit is required in 1..4194304 and bounds queued requests and evaluations. Optional watch is dict(initial_update, dependents, satellites), with all three pure callbacks required. initial_update(package, update, target, snapshot) returns dict(state, has_deployments) using the resolved native start state; deployment-free updates are initially dirty even when unchanged. dependents(notifier, snapshot) returns ordered deduplicated requests. satellites(notifier, admitted_package, snapshot) returns requests after each dependent/satellite admission drains; recursion keeps the same notifier. Notifications scan execution insertion order, repeat after admissions, preserve package dirtiness, and clear update dirtiness/mark processed only after children return. Any ordinary merge resets processed, even with unchanged target. Records, dirty-update lists and update_starts are independent callback snapshots. update_starts maps each update name to its first resolved state/has_deployments record; merges and notifications preserve it. It is empty without watch and is not post-stage CSI state. The returned population exposes execution_count without materializing its full mutable executions inspection list. selected_executions(packages, actions=...) intersects up to 65536 nonempty package-name strings case-insensitively with native execution order, returning fresh independent execution records only for matches; duplicates collapse and missing packages are omitted. Optional actions is a list of at most seven integer MUM actions in 0..6; filtering occurs before record materialization, and an explicit empty list selects nothing. Omitting it preserves all matching executions. This view never consults edits to the full inspection list. The returned population can explicitly capture `deployment_plan()`: this validates the complete native deployment projection once and returns admissions, execution_count, selected_executions, executions, deployment_actions and install_marks without retaining the admission index or package manifests. Dropping the original population then permits that graph to be reclaimed. The captured action evidence is private; changing either inspection list cannot change later marks. This is an action plan, not completed servicing. The returned population also provides deployment_actions(changed_only=False), a read-only projection in execution/manifest order: records contain package, update (exact spelling), deployment (CSI identity record), driver, driver_attributes (independent namespace/name/value records from the containing MUM driver declaration, including inf/ranking/bootCritical when declared), stage and action. No driver metadata is inferred from a dual_* identity or an INF-looking component file. Stage is separate from Execute: stage-only sources have stage=True and action=0; action=3 installs and action=4 uninstalls. With changed_only=True only records with stage=True or action!=0 are materialized, in the same order, AFTER validating the entire native action set. Default inspection retains unchanged references; update starts rather than package last-action determine operations. Resolved starts from watch are required for every deployment. Unsupported restaging, removal below staged, pending/resolved-only source states and undrained notifications fail. The method retains the native snapshot independently of mutable inspection records and returns fresh lists. install_marks(inventory) uses that same immutable action snapshot to analyze exact installed-source counts. inventory is a list of dict(identity=canonical CSI bytes, composition=records from csi_composition, installed_sources=native i!CBS_ payload bytes). Supply every resident owner, including unchanged owners, and every changed deployment; changed resident compositions must match their c! backlinks. An unchanged deployment may replace composition with persisted_members, a list of canonical CSI identity bytes from its installed c! backlinks. These contribute all baseline source counts without recomputing manifest graphs or inventing dispositions. Supplying both forms, duplicate members, missing roots of nonempty/installed inventories, or using persisted membership for an install/uninstall action fails. Empty membership is allowed only for an unowned unchanged deployment. Returns component records with identity, initial/final counts, affected and impactful_install. Each installed source contributes a mark, not merely each active deployment; source conflicts and duplicate changes fail. Uninstall closures can set impactful_install even when another source keeps the component installed. Stage-only changes do not alter install marks. Input identity/source data is bounded to 64 MiB, entries to 4194304. This analysis does not persist sources, resolve non-CBS source policy or execute driver operations. Driver records additionally require native driver-store/DOQ operations; this projection neither executes them nor selects winners, materializes files or authorizes registry writes. These callbacks must explicitly resolve native indexes, child rules, pending/session state and satellite requests. Omit watch to stop before notifications; watch fields are then not evidence of notifications. Deferred MUM work during watching fails because transaction queue policy is not supplied. Inputs and retained snapshots are unchanged; any error returns no partial result. This prefix excludes force-state, pending resolution, CBSBLR owner clearing, capability wrappers, target-raise restrictions, feature-controlled staged restaging, automatic watch-index/initial-state resolution, component execution, permanence and final persistence. It is never a fallback for unsupported transaction policy or authorization to write CurrentState. Uses the same manifest/list bounds as cbs_package_owners; no registry writes, host tools or network access. Returns a native immutable cbs_update_index snapshot, bounded to 65,536 packages, with lazily bound methods. package_state_vector(packages) returns an ordered tuple of intended states for up to 65,536 package-name strings. It queries this exact snapshot; publication never reuses another snapshot's state memo. initial_component_states(deployments, packages) joins explicit canonical-deployment value dictionaries (appid and p!/s!/i! source bytes) to immutable component/driver references, returning independent package/update-to-state lists in manifest reference order. Missing bindings are zero; matching installed/staged/pinned sources yield 112/64/32 with that precedence. Identity matching retains architecture, normalized culture, version, versionScope and type; source primary names remain case-sensitive. Duplicate deployment identities/packages, malformed source data and bounded-input violations fail. It does not cap states by package intents, infer transaction completion, change the baseline or share stage/install execution state. Inputs are bounded to 1048576 deployments/sources, 64 KiB per identity/source, 256 MiB aggregate bytes and 1048576 projected references.",
	"windows.cbs_package_owners":         "Plans package-reference owner intents without modifying an image. packages is a list of dicts with manifest (file or bytes), intended_state (0x40 staged, 0x50 superseded or 0x70 installed), optional parents (resolved canonical package identity strings) and choices (update-name to boolean selectable overrides). targets is an explicit list of selected canonical package identities; every target and parent needs a package context. Returns sorted records with package, owner, integration, state and value (integration shifted 16 bits plus state). Local update names and parent-package scopes remain separate; repeated detectors within one applicable block are conjunctive, while separate applicable blocks are alternatives. Failed detect applicability retains the detector state (including absent or superseded), not an implicit staged floor. Local detection uses uncapped update intent; parent detection and emitted owners apply the containing package cap. Unknown conditions, cycles and unresolved metadata raise errors, never partial records. Supports detectNone and scoped detectUpdate with staged/detect dispositions, plus absent selectable defaults for dependency queries and selected content; edition-gated selectability accepts up to two explicitly resolved current/target edition identity names in editions. A nonmatching edition disables the selector, not the child. Missing edition context, absent owner intents requiring removal, capability restrictions and component predicates remain unsupported. Intended state is supplied transaction intent, not a computed CurrentState. This does not select packages, resolve parent versions, infer permanence, supersede packages, verify signatures or complete a servicing transaction. Lists are bounded to 65536 items; manifest input is at most 32 MiB each and 256 MiB total. No host tools, registry writes or network access.",
	"windows.etl_walk":                   "Visits raw records from a finalized sequential uncompressed kernel ETL in file order, NOT timestamp order. Calls visit(record); callback return values are ignored and errors stop traversal. Records contain offset, tick, cpu, header_type, group, opcode, version, pid, tid and owned payload bytes. Also exposes marker, provider_id (16 GUID bytes in Windows byte order), event_id, message_flags, header_flags and activity_id (16 GUID bytes). Modern event extensions are separated from user payload: extensions contains type, linkage, owned data bytes, match_id, pointer_size and stack address values. Stack types 5/6 decode 32/64-bit addresses; unknown types remain opaque. Extension chains are limited to 128 items and malformed bounds fail. Supports bounded WPP TraceMessage framing (marker 0x9000); its optional timestamp/TID/PID fields follow message_flags. Missing timestamps are zero; payload schemas are not inferred. Unavailable PID/TID are 0xffffffff; non-kernel group is 255. Optional group/opcode (-1 or 0..255) and inclusive raw min_tick/max_tick filters select callbacks; max_tick=0 is unbounded. The entire file is still validated and all records count toward max_events (1..4000000); input is bounded to 1 GiB and buffers to 1 MiB. Returns logfile header and loss counters. Callers must check loss, interpret clocks and schemas, account for lifecycle reuse and sort selected records before scheduler attribution. A thread running on the ReadyThread CPU is not proof of the wake source: interrupts may intervene. No symbols, inferred dependency claims, guest execution or network access.",
	"windows.certificate_chain":          "Validates X.509 paths using only explicit roots (at most 64), intermediates (at most 32), unix_time and purpose (code_signing or timestamping). Timestamping additionally requires a critical, exclusive RFC3161 timestamping EKU on the leaf. Each DER/PEM input is bounded to 1 MiB. Returns lists of certificate DER from leaf through root; invalid time, usage or trust paths raise errors. This is not revocation checking, a trusted timestamp decision or Windows provider policy; it uses no host roots, clock or network.",
	"windows.catalog_signature":          "Verifies single-signer RSA/SHA-256 Windows catalog SignedData integrity and recognized Microsoft RFC3161 timestamps. Supports directly carried PKCS#7 CTLs and CMS OCTET STRING encapsulation with distinct signed hash domains. Returns the same signer, certificates, signature, unsigned_attributes, timestamp and digest_algorithm fields as pe_signature. Requires a parseable catalog membership index, bounds input to 64 MiB and certificate count to 32. Does not establish certificate-chain, revocation, publisher or Windows trust-provider policy. No host roots, clock or network are consulted.",
	"windows.serialized_certificate":     "Parses one SystemCertificates registry Blob into certificate and properties records. Bounds input to 1 MiB and 256 unique properties; requires encoding 1, complete lengths and exactly one certificate property 32. Validates X.509 DER and optional SHA-1 property 3. Preserves all other properties as integer-to-bytes entries without interpreting their trust policy. This is not a trust decision.",
	"windows.appx_signature":             "Verifies single-signer RSA/SHA-256 APPX/MSIX package integrity, all AXPC/AXCD/AXCT/AXBM and optional AXCI digest domains, and recognized RFC3161 timestamp integrity. Returns the same signature metadata as pe_signature. Supports bounded single-disk, comment-free ZIP/ZIP64 packages with a final signature entry; rejects unsupported layouts and bundles. Input limit 64 MiB, signature 4 MiB, footprint 16 MiB, 4096 entries. Does not establish publisher, chain, expiry or revocation trust. No host tools, roots, clock or network.",
	"windows.script_signature":           "Verifies SHA-256 Authenticode PowerShell or CDXML script integrity and recognized RFC3161 timestamp integrity. format is powershell or xml. Accepts strict UTF-8 or BOM-marked UTF-16LE, CRLF signature framing, and no trailing unsigned data. Input limit 16 MiB. Returns pe_signature-compatible metadata, not a certificate-chain or publisher trust decision. No host roots, clock or network.",
	"windows.pe_signature":               "Verifies embedded single-signer RSA/SHA-256 Authenticode image integrity using bounded native PE and PKCS#7 parsing. Returns signer DER, embedded certificate DER list, signature bytes, raw unsigned attributes, digest_algorithm, timestamp and secondary_signature_count. The count describes advertised nested signatures, not verified secondary signatures. A recognized Microsoft RFC3161 SHA-256 timestamp is checked for signature integrity and binding to the primary signature; timestamp contains unix seconds, signer DER and certificates, or None when absent. Input is limited to 256 MiB, certificate table to 4 MiB and certificates to 32. Rejects unsigned images, unsupported signature forms and mismatched image/content/signature digests. This is NOT certificate-chain, timestamp-authority trust, expiry, revocation, publisher or Windows trust-policy validation. Other unsigned attributes and CMS v1 attribute certificates are not authenticated or used as trust material. No host roots, clock or network are consulted.",
	"windows.pri_resource_values":        "Returns a parallel list of sole neutral/default string candidates. Each decoded\nentry has `value` (text) and `path` (boolean). Multiple candidates, a sole\nnon-default candidate, or binary data return `None`: retain the resource index\nrather than choosing a locale. Absent resources, unsupported external-file\nlocators, invalid bounds and malformed encodings are errors. Supports bounded\ninternal/data-item values in UTF-16, ASCII and UTF-8. Path values are not opened\nor trusted as filesystem paths; callers must validate package-relative paths.",
	"windows.decode_file_eas":            "Decodes a bounded FILE_FULL_EA_INFORMATION chain (at most 64 KiB) to name/flags/value dictionaries. Validates lengths, alignment, terminators, supported flags and unique case-insensitive names. Values are opaque bytes; this is not a trust decision or WIM EA decoding.",
	"windows.catalog_attributes":         "Parses ordered catalog-level CTL extensions from PKCS#7/CMS catalogs (64 MiB limit). Returns oid, critical, name_value, name, flags and raw value bytes. SPC name/value names are BMPStrings; flags and value bytes are preserved. Unknown extension OIDs remain opaque, duplicates remain distinct, and member/signer attributes are not enumerated. This is neither signature verification nor a trust decision.",
	"windows.catalog":                    "Owns at most 64 MiB of catalog bytes and parses its CTL index once, returning a read-only file. Reapplying catalog to that handle returns the same handle. catalog_index reuses the native index; catalog_signature lazily memoizes cryptographic content/signature/timestamp integrity on these exact bytes, returning independent projections. This is not certificate-chain, revocation, expiry, publisher or timestamp-authority trust. Callers must validate trust against the exact trust store and policy; no trust result is cached.",
	"windows.assembly_declarations":      "Extracts ordered raw assembly declarations in one traversal of a binary.xml_node root. Returns a frozen record with architectures, files, directories, security_definitions, memberships and managed_assembly. Recognizes asm.v1/asm.v3 only and ignores foreign namespace nodes. Preserves duplicates, qualified attributes, absent versus empty values, unknown directory children, link content presence, and security references for caller validation. Bounds extraction to 1,048,576 visited nodes/attributes. This is not macro expansion, path validation, precedence, security resolution, installation policy or authenticity verification.",
	"windows.catalog_entries":            "Returns version (1 or 2) and ordered CTL entries with subject, lookup digest and ordered attributes. SPC name/value attributes expose name, flags and exact value bytes; unknown values retain their DER. Member attributes are never taken from global extensions or signer metadata. name_value optionally selects SPC name/value attributes by lowercase name and omits entries without matches, preserving order and duplicates; the complete catalog is still parsed and validated before filtering. Bounded to 64 MiB; malformed input fails. Parsing is not signature verification, path validation or a trust verdict.",
	"windows.catalog_index":              "Parses signed-catalog CTL lookup entries into `sha1` and `sha256` byte lists. Version 1 uses SPC indirect-data digests; version 2 uses subject identifiers. Unrelated certificate/attribute octet strings are not treated as entries. Input is bounded to 64 MiB and malformed catalogs fail. These are catalog lookup keys, not signature verification or trust decisions. No network access or Windows API is used. An input from windows.catalog reuses its owned parsed index, but every call returns independent mutable digest lists.",
	"windows.etl_scheduler":              "Returns timestamp-sorted scheduler observations for one PID from a finalized\nkernel ETL file. Records contain `seconds` relative to `first_tick`, `kind`,\n`tid`, `peer_tid`, `peer_pid`, `cpu`, `state`, and `reason`. Kinds are\n`process_start`, `process_end`, `thread_start`, `thread_rundown`, `thread_end`,\n`in`, `out`, and `ready`. State/reason describe the outgoing thread on a\ncontext switch; they do not describe the incoming thread on an `in` record.\n\nOwnership follows observed thread lifetimes; process boundaries expose PID\nreuse, and end rundown never invents an earlier lifetime. A ready record's\npeer is the last observed running thread on that CPU, not a proven waker:\ninterrupts/DPCs may be responsible. Zero peer IDs mean idle or unknown.\n\nIncludes `header` loss counters, `first_tick`, `last_tick`,\n`switch_discontinuities`, and `unsupported_thread_events`. Unsupported process\nschemas are not counted by the latter; use `etl_report` for a schema inventory.\nLosses and incomplete ownership prevent reliable interval/causal conclusions.\nNo wait durations or critical path are inferred. Input/event limits match\n`etl_report`; `max_records` must be 1..1000000. Limits and malformed framing\nfail rather than silently truncating. No symbols or network access is implicit.",
	"windows.etl_profile":                "Native 64-bit kernel sampled-IP analysis with the ETL reader's bounded input\nand event limits. Returns image identities, process lifetimes, aggregated\n`points` (process/module indices, RVA, interrupt flag, sample count), loss\nmetadata and explicit unknown counts. Index -1 means unknown; an unmapped\nRVA retains the absolute IP. Counts are self samples, not elapsed time or\ninclusive stacks. Also returns timestamp-sorted `image_events` (tick, pid, opcode, base, path, size, timestamp, checksum) from native load/unload/rundown records for explicit cross-trace address correlation. Callers must compare trace clocks and boot identity, respect process/address reuse, and retain ambiguous mappings. End rundown is not treated as an earlier image load.\nNo symbols or network requests are implicit.\n\n`windows.pe(file).symbolize(pdb, rvas, timestamp, image_size, checksum)` resolves\nan AMD64 RVA list against `windows.pdb(file)`. Requires matching PE identity\nand CodeView GUID/DBI age; returns name/RVA/displacement/match dictionaries\nor None. Nearest public symbols cannot extend past their runtime-function\ninterval or explicit chained unwind owner; unnamed leaves/unlinked fragments remain unresolved.",
	"windows.etl_report":                 "Native offline analysis of finalized sequential, uncompressed kernel ETW `.etl`\nfiles (not EVT/EVTX). Uses the portable file reader; no Windows API, external\nparser, guest conversion tool, symbols or network access. Input is limited to\n1 GiB, 1 MiB per buffer, 4096 schemas, one day of event timestamps, and the\nconfigured event count (1..4000000). Unsupported framing/clock modes and\ntruncation are errors, not partial-success reports.\n\nReturns `header` (clock, CPU count, loss counters), observed tick/time range,\nscheduled/idle/unknown/uncovered CPU-seconds, switch discontinuities, per-process\nlifetimes, command lines, scheduled CPU, matched ready/block thread-seconds,\nwait reasons, disk bytes/counts/summed and maximum response times, DPC/ISR sums,\none-second `timeline`, `schemas` (group/opcode/version/header/count/decoded), and\n`warnings`. Events are time-sorted before lifecycle attribution; PID/TID reuse\nis not collapsed into one final ID map. Unsupported payloads remain in the schema\ninventory rather than being guessed.\n\nTimes are relative to the first observed event, not firmware entry. Process start\nis not a desktop-ready milestone. Scheduled CPU includes interruptions; DPC/ISR\nsums can overlap each other and scheduled time. Ready/block/I/O sums across\nconcurrent work are **not** critical-path wall time. Boundary gaps, unknown\nthreads and losses are explicit. Reports include sensitive process arguments.\nSee `windows/etl/FORMAT.md` for format coverage and permissive reference licenses.",
	"windows.cli_inspect":                "Reads CLI metadata and selected managed method bodies without executing them. Accepts a portable file or bytes, at most 32 MiB. Returns runtime_version (metadata version string without trailing NUL padding), cor_flags (raw COR header flags), entry_point (raw COR header entry field), rows (token, columns in ECMA-335 table order), strings (user-string token to text), and methods (token, name, instructions). String/blob columns are decoded; coded references and table indexes become tokens, except null remains zero; list-end sentinels are preserved as token-shaped indexes. Other columns retain their integer value, including GUID heap indexes. method is a case-sensitive method-name substring; empty selects all IL bodies. Each instruction has offset relative to IL code, numeric opcode, raw operand bytes and token (zero for non-token operands). Bounds tables/heaps/bodies, limits rows to 100000, decoded row heaps to 64 MiB and instructions to 1000000, and rejects unknown opcodes. Does not verify IL stack/control flow, resolve external assemblies, decode signatures or exception clauses, evaluate feature gates, or claim observed runtime effects.",
	"archive.irix_product":               "Reads IRIX pd001 product descriptors into products, images and subsystems with versions, flags, build-selection expressions, replacement ranges and grouped prerequisites. Preserves alternate descriptions and extended attributes. Checks field bounds, entry limits and zero block padding; it neither executes expressions nor installs software.",
	"archive.irix_plan":                  "Plans explicitly selected subsystem patterns (or declared defaults when selected is empty), choosing the newest supplied version unless versions pins an exact subsystem. Reports unsatisfied prerequisite alternatives, explicit machine facts, ambiguous destinations, missing payloads and unexecuted preop/postop/exitop hooks. indexes uses product@version keys; images uses product.image@version keys. installed maps subsystem names to versions. existing is the caller's complete current regular-file map, and previous maps paths to prior BSD checksums for config(update/suggest/noupdate) decisions. Returns ordered selections, declarative borrowed-file/node/link operations, removed subsystem names, hooks and issues. resolved only means no known planning issue: payload checksums are validated when read, and this is not installation or boot proof. Unknown policy remains an explicit issue; scripts are never executed.",
	"binary.irix_executable":             "Inspects MIPS ELF32/ELF64 and ECOFF32 files natively, returning byte order, o32/n32/n64 ABI and ISA flags, entry/GP, borrowed sections and segments, symbols, relocations, dynamic tags and needed libraries. MIPS64 relocations preserve all three operation types and the special-symbol byte; MIPS option/ABI-flag records expose compiler metadata and the GP value. ECOFF symbolic tables retain local/external names and storage classes. Tables, counts, names, ranges and references are bounded and checked. It does not apply relocations, load executable code, interpret all debug records or emulate IRIX syscalls.",
	"filesystem.mtree":                   "Reads a bounded BSD mtree metadata manifest natively, including /set and /unset defaults, directory descent and parent navigation, full ./ paths, line continuations, comments and octal or common escaped characters. Returns path/attributes records with metadata values preserved as strings, including Apple extensions. Bounds input, logical lines, records, expanded attributes and paths; rejects duplicates, malformed escapes and parent traversal. Does not read the described files, follow links or mutate a filesystem.",
	"vmm.arm64_platform":                 "Composes ARM64 hardware independently of CPU/load state. create(ctx) runs once per VM and returns a dictionary with devices (ordered MMIO/native device values) and optional hvc(immediate,function)->bool. Each factory must create fresh mutable state. Earlier devices win overlapping address ranges; unknown MMIO and unhandled HVC remain unsupported exits. Optional GIC distributor/redistributor addresses are explicit and must be distinct 64 KiB-aligned values. Factory and MMIO/HVC callbacks run serially on one VM-owned Starlark thread, canceled when the VM stops. Runtime operations reject calls from any other Starlark thread. The runtime supplies bounded guest-memory access, permission-enforcing DMA, memory protection, registers, counter/epoch, IRQs, disk resources, events and native protocol construction. Native device instances cannot be shared across VMs; unused or failed-factory native devices are closed. No host paths, sockets or processes are exposed.",
	"vmm.mmio":                           "Creates a guest MMIO region with explicit address/size, optional read(offset,size)->uint and write(offset,size,value)->None callbacks, and access widths (default 1,2,4,8). Reads/writes must be aligned to their width and fit entirely inside the region; read results must fit the requested width. Add the value to an ARM64 platform factory result. Mutable callback state should be created inside that factory. A missing callback fails explicitly. This primitive does not allocate RAM or select hardware.",
	"vmm.darwin_nvram":                   "Encodes string variable names/values into one volatile 8 KiB CHRP NVRAM bank. Unqualified names select Apple common; explicit Apple common/system GUID prefixes select their corresponding partitions. Returns immutable bytes for a caller-owned backing layout. This is format construction only and never accesses host firmware.",
	"vmm.arm64_boot":                     "Creates a board-independent ARM64 handoff with explicit physical RAM base, image address, entry point and optional X0-X30/system registers. It contains no devices, firmware commands, NVRAM or board selection. Generic validation checks load ranges and instruction alignment; native backends separately enforce host-page constraints. Compose hardware with vmm.arm64_platform and pass it independently to vmm.machine. Native cc currently provides one CPU and headless execution; stopped cc.v1 exposes bounded read_physical and read_virtual until VM close.",
	"vmm.darwin_arm64_boot":              "Constructs a board-independent unslid ARM64 executable/fileset handoff in memory from a decoded Mach-O kernel and flattened Apple device tree. Loads bounded nonoverlapping segments, zeroes BSS, appends ARM64 revision-2 bootargs, chosen DRAM ranges, a random-seed and device-tree memory-map ranges, and enters the original thread PC with X0 pointing to bootargs. Physical and virtual bases are required explicit arguments; RAM bases and size must be 32 MiB aligned. The machine memory must equal the encoded memory value. payloads maps memory-map names to immutable files loaded separately at 16 KiB aligned addresses and reserved through topOfKernelData. TrustCache is placed below the kernel inside its protected range; RAMDisk can contain an original APFS image. nvram optionally maps string variable names to string values in a fresh volatile CHRP bank; the bytes seed the chosen proxy and are returned as boot.nvram for explicit recipe-owned device backing. Unqualified names and the 7C436110-AB2A-4BBB-A880-FE41995C9F82: prefix select common; the 40A0DDD2-77F8-4392-B4A3-1E7304206516: prefix selects system and is needed for system variables such as boot-args. None preserves a supplied proxy with matching bank geometry or creates an empty bank. XNU performs its own fileset rebasing/signing. entropy=None generates 256 fresh cryptographic bytes for modern XNU; an explicit entropy value must be 64 bytes (earlier XNU) or 256 bytes with nonzero data. Returned read_only_address/read_only_size describe the immutable kernel prefix for optional platform protection configuration. No hardware platform is selected. Does not personalize IMG4 or imply a successful kernel boot. Public HVF rejects Apple PAC initialization; the native cc private_apple_isa option selects ISA level 3 with a separately entitled executable.",
	"binary.encode_apple_device_tree":    "Encodes an Apple flattened device tree from recursive records or dictionaries with properties (string-to-bytes), optional property_flags, and children lists. Uses the same bounded layout and validation as binary.apple_device_tree, preserves explicit flags, and creates a portable in-memory file. Template expressions require caller policy before kernel handoff.",
	"binary.arm64_disassemble":           "Decodes 1 through 4096 ARM64 instructions from a portable file or byte view. Returns records with address, bytes, instruction and target for PC-relative operands. Unknown instructions retain their original bytes and report unknown. Offset and caller-supplied address are independent; no host disassembler is used.",
	"binary.apple_device_tree":           "Decodes an Apple flattened device tree into recursive properties, property_flags and children records. Preserves template flag bits without resolving bindings. Bounds input to 1 MiB, depth to 32 and nodes to 4096; rejects malformed names, duplicate properties, truncation and trailing bytes.",
	"scs.create":                         "Creates a new exclusively locked native SCSREPO3 repository file without overwriting existing data. File paths and durability are native backend authority; stored contents use the portable SCS engine. Repositories close at execution teardown or explicit close. See docs/scs.md for publication, Git cloning and limits.",
	"scs.open":                           "Opens a native SCSREPO2/3 repository with exclusive ownership. Paged indexes load lazily and are verified as accessed. Opening may repair an incomplete tail; it is not a forensic read-only operation. Complete corruption fails rather than being silently discarded.",
	"scs.memory":                         "Creates a bounded volatile SCSREPO3 repository, or reopens an existing portable repository file into independent paged memory. Defaults to 512 MiB of addressable storage, not a total-process memory quota. Published roots can be preserved as a standalone file via repository.file(); Sync does not make memory survive process exit.",
	"archive.appx":                       "Reads unencrypted APPX/MSIX packages and bundles through portable files. identity exposes name, publisher, version, architecture and resource_id. full_name and family_name use the Windows SHA-256/UTF-16LE publisher identity hash; these names do not authenticate the publisher. entries/files are lazy ZIP file views; complete reads or entry.verify() check CRC. Entry name/path is the once-decoded UTF-8 OPC path, archive_name is the original ZIP name; do not unescape paths again. Unsafe and colliding decoded paths are rejected. Bundle packages expose name, type, architecture, version, size, is_stub and file. Member names normalize Windows separators to slashes; full installations should skip stub alternatives; call archive.appx(member.file) to read a child. Rejects unsafe paths, duplicate/colliding names, oversized manifests and absent or size-mismatched embedded members. verify_block_map(maximum=8589934592) checks SHA256 decoded blocks, sizes and complete payload coverage under a decoded byte budget; bundle members require separate calls. This checks an unauthenticated map, not publisher trust or ZIP container signature digests. Does not authenticate signatures, decrypt packages, resolve flat bundles, or install/register software. Keep the source open while reading entries.",
	"archive.crx":                        "Verifies a CRX3 envelope and returns its developer ID, format version and ZIP entries as portable files without extraction. Checks all RSA/SHA-256 and P-256 ECDSA/SHA-256 signatures and requires a matching developer key. Header decoding is bounded to 1MiB. Does not pin a Chrome Web Store publisher key or parse legacy CRX2 packages. The source must remain open while reading entries.",
	"emulator.shell.filesystem":          "Creates a caller-owned bounded Unix namespace shared by shell, compiler and Linux commands. write accepts portable files; find returns an immutable portable file snapshot. Paths are canonical absolute guest paths, never host paths.",
	"emulator.s390":                      "Creates an in-process ESA/390 or System/370 (architecture='s370') machine with storage, CPUs, a channel subsystem, CKD DASD, 3270 and 3215 consoles, unit-record devices, AWS tapes and the hardware system console. Attach devices, then ipl() or storage_load() and run(); time is virtual. See docs/s390.md.",
	"emulator.shell.run":                 "Executes POSIX shell source in the supplied Unix filesystem. Copies the recipe-owned absolute-path command table before concurrent workers start; no Starlark callback runs on a worker. Source/stdin accept files, strings or bytes. Returns status, steps, stdout and stderr; emulator failures raise errors. Output limits apply per stream. Runtime cancellation and timeout cancel and join shell jobs; compilation is arena-bounded, not preemptible.",
	"emulator.shell.date":                "Creates a UTC date command with an explicit fixed Unix-second clock. It reads no host timezone or implicit wall clock; unsupported zones and format directives fail explicitly.",
	"emulator.shell.uname":               "Creates a uname command describing the modeled Linux/amd64 environment, not the host kernel or a claim of full Linux compatibility.",
	"emulator.linux":                     "Creates an ELF execution capability for shell.run(executable=...). Recognizes static Linux/amd64 ELF images, preserves argv[0] independently of the resolved guest path, and uses the same virtual filesystem and inherited streams. Non-ELF input falls back to the shell script loader.",
	"renvo.compiler":                     "Creates an in-process C compiler command capability. The recipe installs and binds its guest path. Invocation arguments, working directory, input and output all use the shell-owned Unix filesystem and streams; no host compiler is invoked.",
	"renvo.linker":                       "Creates a portable Renvo linker command capability, without installing an executable or choosing its guest path. Writes outputs into the invoking shell filesystem.",
	"renvo.archiver":                     "Creates an in-process archive command capability. index=True supplies ranlib-style arguments; index=False accepts ar-style arguments. Inputs and outputs remain in the invoking Unix filesystem.",
	"renvo.headers":                      "Exposes bundled libc headers as portable directory files. Requires renvo_bundle; does not install or overwrite files. The recipe chooses where and with which modes to provision them.",
	"windows.coff":                       "Inspects a bounded standard COFF object in memory. Returns machine, sections (index, name, bytes data, size, characteristics, relocations) and primary symbols (index including auxiliary slots, name, value, section, type, storage_class, aux_count). Relocations retain offset, numeric type, symbol_index and resolved symbol. Rejects PE images, bigobj and overflow relocation tables. maximum defaults to 64MiB.",
	"windows.ne_internal_references":     "Expands internal NE relocation chains without loading or modifying the image. Returns source segment/offset/type, resolved target segment/offset, original entry ordinal when used, and additive status. Validates chain bounds, cycles, targets, and maximum_references. Imported and operating-system fixups are outside this view; iterated relocation storage is rejected.",
	"windows.ne_extend":                  "Extends a Windows NE module with fixed code and data segments while preserving original segment storage, imports, resources, and exports. exports maps ordinal to an offset in the new code. relocations contains (source_type, source_offset, target_segment, target_offset) tuples: type 2 is a selector and type 3 a far pointer; target 0 denotes new code, -1 new data, -2 a movable export ordinal in target_offset, and positive values existing segments. Movable export references retain the loader thunk needed when real-mode segments move or are discarded. Each source begins with the 0xffff chain terminator. aliases maps unused ordinals to original ordinals, retaining the original entries and Windows loader prologue patching when wrappers redirect them. Clears stale fast-load metadata. Returns an in-memory file.",
	"windows.ne_exports":                 "Inspects named and ordinal exports of a native Windows NE image through its existing bounded parser. Returns ordinal, names, segment, offset, file_offset, available bytes, entry flags, movable and constant metadata. Constant exports have no code bytes; unbacked or iterated segment storage is identified without inventing a file offset. maximum bounds input to 64MiB by default. Does not load or execute the module.",
	"windows.ne_imports":                 "Inspects original imported relocation heads of a native Windows NE image. Each record identifies module, name or ordinal, source segment, offset, source_type and additive flag. Validates module/name tables, segment storage, relocation counts and import targets; maximum bounds input bytes and maximum_imports bounds returned records. Iterated segment relocations are explicitly unsupported. Does not apply relocations, follow loader-linked chains, load or execute the module.",
	"windows.catalog_sign":               "Builds a native RSA/SHA-256 signed CTL catalog from a dictionary of simple filenames to files or bytes. PE members use the catalog image digest; ordinary files use their full byte digest. Includes File, SIP member information, and optional OSAttr attributes. Creation time is explicit Unix seconds. Supports 1–4096 members, at most 64 MiB each and 256 MiB total. Returns bytes; does not install trust or change guest signing policy.",
	"windows.type_library":               "Reads one standalone MSFT or SLTG type library from a file or bytes, bounded to 16 MiB. Returns the same guid/name/description/version/language/lcid/flags/syskind/types fact dictionary as an element of windows.pe(file).typelibs. Rejects PE containers and unknown signatures; does not register or execute the library.",

	"rfe.backend":                "Creates an experimental in-process software x86 PC backend from Renvo x86.rfe. JIT-compiles hot 16-, 32- and 64-bit code using Renvo native blocks, without KVM or QEMU. Requires a supported 64-bit native-JIT host; unavailable executable memory is an error. Privileged/device/unsupported instructions use precise architectural slow paths. Shares BIOS, IDE, VGA, input and overlays with cc. MS-DOS 6.22 startup, Windows 3.11 standard/enhanced-mode Notepad, and Windows 98 SE Explorer/Notepad are verified. The graphical smokes verify keyboard input, PS/2 mouse-selected Save and changed bytes on the guest disk. Enhanced-mode paging and actual native-tier retirement are explicitly asserted. Implements basic x87, string port I/O, paging and protected/v8086 gates; parts of x87 and other legacy CPU/device features remain incomplete. overlay_limit bounds dirty snapshot memory in bytes. Inspect with vm.extension(\"rfe.v1\").state().",
	"vm.record":                  "Starts continuous background recording and returns after a captured frame and encoder output are ready (30-second startup bound). For boot capture, create the VM with start_paused=True, call record, then resume. QEMU requires FFmpeg with libx264. H.264 MP4 output stays in bounded memory; stop() finalizes to a portable file. Integer-only upscaling on a fixed letterboxed canvas preserves pixel geometry; oversized sources downscale with nearest-neighbor. lossless=True uses libx264rgb without color subsampling and requires a player supporting H.264 RGB/4:4:4; default libx264 CRF10/YUV420 favors compatibility. Handles guest mode changes; missed sampling slots repeat the prior frame to preserve time. Video only, one active recorder per VM. See help(recorder) for bounds, stats and ownership; finalize before VM teardown.",
	"channel.extension":          "Discovers a synchronous v1 device over an exclusively owned byte channel with deadlines. Exposes manifest-declared methods with positional/keyword arguments and type checks. close() closes the channel. No host SDK, sockets, futures, retry or reconnect. Malformed replies, transport errors and timeout close it with outcome uncertain; ordinary device ERR leaves it usable. help(device) shows the manifest. For complete framing, discovery, value grammar, bounds and native input examples: print(channel.extension_protocol()).",
	"channel.extension_protocol": "Returns the complete offline synchronous v1 wire specification, including the Windows 1.01 native input payload. Print it in the REPL; no connection or I/O required.",
	"channel.expose_tcp":         "Native-only IPv4 loopback bridge for one trusted TCP client. Port zero selects a free port, returned as port. Takes exclusive ownership of the byte channel on success; do not read or write it elsewhere. The listener closes after the first connection; disconnect closes both streams without reconnect or replay. Uses bounded copy buffers. close() interrupts and joins workers; runtime teardown also closes it. The channel must unblock reads and writes on close. TCP is plaintext and has no authentication; other local processes can connect.",
	"channel.memory_pair":        "Creates two connected, bounded byte channels entirely in memory, without host sockets or DNS. maximum limits each incoming queue (default 1 MiB, at most 64 MiB). read_available returns None when the peer has no data yet, bytes when data is ready, and empty bytes after the peer closes and its data is drained. write_available returns the byte count, None when capacity is insufficient, or -1 for a closed endpoint; writes are all-or-nothing. write instead raises an error when delivery is unavailable. Emulator checkpoints restore queued data and closed state when endpoints are retained in plugin state.",
	"auto_plan":                  "Like auto, but also proposes named virtual filesystems from directory listings. The plans attribute returns id, title, description and a relative path under $plans. Following that path constructs the selected read-only view on demand; discovery does not read member payloads. Ordinary files remain accessible, plain auto is unchanged, and a real $plans entry takes precedence. Plans preserve input provenance and distinguish variants rather than overwriting duplicate objects. A proposal is a candidate: content validation can report unsupported or malformed inputs when opened.",
	"archive.ibmi_save":          "Reads qualified IBM i optical descriptors, catalogs and stored sections. Supported release/target pairs are 4705/4705, 4705/3905, 4600/4600 and older 3400/3400 font descriptors. The default catalog profile requires a save catalog; profile=boot750 permits only the qualified QGENESIST or QINTAPE catalog-free starts. Auto additionally expands codec-02 LD_TRS into lazy logical-prefix views while retaining unknown LD_LZ1 tails and physical/logical provenance. Logical section views fail on unstored bytes, never zero-fill. Explicit IBM037 text rendering is not detected saved CCSID. Standalone 528-byte SAVF, unqualified generations and full object restoration remain unsupported.",
	"archive.bff":                "Reads single-volume AIX by-name backup archives with extended name records (FS_NAME_X=11), checking every header checksum, record and security bounds, declared sizes and end framing. Stored payloads, raw headers and ACL/PCL bytes remain borrowed file views; packed Huffman bodies decode natively using the enclosing logical size and a cumulative decoded-byte limit. Entries preserve original names, paths, modes, inode/link identity, owners, device numbers and timestamps without applying them. The returned trailer retains final unused distribution-block bytes, which may be nonzero. This initial generation handles exact-end or 1KiB-rounded distribution archives; other record generations, continuation volumes and larger physical tails fail explicitly. It does not run scripts, plan installation, interpret XCOFF or validate uncompressed file contents with a nonexistent payload checksum.",
	"auto":                       "Wraps a byte view, file, bytes value or existing filesystem in a lazy standardized Go node view. Registered format detectors inspect a bounded prefix and confirm the format through native parsers. Native EROFS supports compact/extended inodes, flat/inline data and LZ4 full/compact indexes including 2-byte packs and big physical clusters. EROFS views verify superblock CRC32C and reject unsupported incompatible features; source bytes must remain unchanged while views are used. summary returns current entry metadata without detecting or reading its payload; metadata additionally detects the payload format. Use summary for directory inventories. By default eager decoding is limited to 512 MiB while bounded gzip, bzip2 and XZ streams have no total-length ceiling; an explicit positive maximum bounds both kinds. Container paths resolve recursively while file preserves the original bytes. Detectors receive containing-tree context for companion files across directories. For an isolated file, tree and its tree-relative path attach that context explicitly. Companion lookup reads raw entries within the supplied tree without following links or recursively detecting files; dot segments and host paths are not accepted.",
	"web.application":            "Composes a scripted request handler with optional dynamically registered VM consoles and native session authentication. Public GET/HEAD asset paths are explicit; other requests require a token, and mutations require same-origin checks. The HTTP listener permits loopback or an exact IP assigned to the local Tailscale tunnel; wildcard and LAN binds are rejected. An explicit HTTPS origin supports a trusted reverse proxy without trusting forwarded headers.",
	"web.browse":                 "Resolves a recursive path against an auto view. Optional path mounts a tree under a script-owned route prefix. json=1 returns entry metadata and paginated children; raw responses use the existing file range handling. Returns structured HTTP errors for missing, malformed and bounded-out inputs.",
	"windows.batch_plan":         "Inspects DOS batch commands and COPY source declarations using portable media files. Labels, branches, media selection, and external commands remain explicit runtime dependencies; it does not execute the script.",
	"windows.press_setup_plan":   "Plans Microsoft Press SETUP.INI media trees and retains companion SETUP.CMD actions and shortcut dependencies. It does not run the setup executable.",

	"archive.cfb":                              "Reads an OLE compound file through bounded FAT, DIFAT, and mini-stream chains, exposing member file views without host extraction.",
	"archive.installer_media":                  "Discovers InstallShield packages, including nested packages, from a dictionary mapping portable relative media names to files.",
	"database.msi":                             "Reads a Windows Installer database and its embedded streams. plan resolves static payloads and table effects for supplied target properties, retaining custom actions and runtime dependencies without execution.",
	"windows.acme_table":                       "Parses an ACME setup table into headers, objects, arguments, annotations, and source provenance.",
	"windows.acme_plan":                        "Joins an ACME STF object graph with its INF file catalogue and portable media files. Unknown choices and runtime destinations remain explicit; no installer code is executed.",
	"windows.sdk_inf_plan":                     "Plans Windows 3.x SDK disk catalogues from INF declarations, retaining group and environment actions. Sources are decoded natively from supplied media handles.",
	"windows.setup_inf":                        "Parses command-oriented Microsoft setup INF sections with source locations. plan expands known copy catalogues and branches while retaining runtime queries and unknown loops.",
	"archive.rms_variable":                     "Decodes sequential RMS variable-length (VAR) and variable-with-fixed-control (VFC) records using the 32-byte attributes from an ODS-2 header or BACKUP attribute52. Returns records with original offset and borrowed control/data file views. Removes length words, odd-length alignment bytes and FFFF end-of-block padding from those views, but does not translate text or interpret printer controls. Validates EOF, control size, declared maximum data length and no-span block boundaries; rejects indexed/relative organizations, other record formats and extended record flags. maximum_records bounds returned records. Metadata alone is not proof that arbitrary file bytes actually use RMS framing; mismatches remain errors.",
	"archive.vmsbackup":                        "Reconstructs VMS BACKUP file-data chains after the same block, CRC and redundancy validation as vmsbackup_blocks. Returns entries with raw name bytes, flags, declared logical size, stored_size, attributes (kind/data records), missing_contents and data. Data borrows source ranges, excludes allocation slack, and is None when a nonempty file has metadata but no stored payload bytes. missing_contents reports absence without inferring its cause or accepting a partially stored file. Empty files have an empty data view. Rejects discontinuous virtual block addresses, partial data, missing or duplicate name/RMS fields and unsupported record kinds. Retains file attributes without translating RMS records or applying host path rules. Volume/file-ID record interpretation remains outside this API; inspect them with vmsbackup_blocks. Source bytes must remain unchanged while data views are used.",
	"archive.vmsbackup_blocks":                 "Reads a complete single-volume VMS BACKUP block stream, validating header CRC-16, whole-block CRC-32, sequence numbers, record boundaries and each encountered XOR redundancy payload. Returns blocks with offset, sequence, raw header, parity and records. Records expose offset, kind, flags, address, reserved and a borrowed data file. Unknown record kinds and flags are preserved. Does not repair damaged blocks, require final-group redundancy, join file data records, interpret RMS attributes, or decode nested file contents. The source must remain unchanged while borrowed views are used. Limits independently bound block count, total record count and block size before allocation.",
	"filesystem.ods2":                          "Reads Files-11 ODS-2 version1 home blocks, index headers and versioned directory trees. Verifies home/header checksums, primary and backup index maps, file identities, allocation bounds, EOF fields, directory records and traversal limits. Returns entries, files, exact-path find(path), raw home bytes and padded volume_name. Entries retain raw names, version, file_number, sequence, header, record_attributes, characteristics, data and size. Names include ;version, with unsafe bytes percent-escaped; the root self-reference is a directory_link and is not followed. Data is a borrowed read-only extent view; RMS records are not translated and allocation slack is excluded. Supports retrieval formats1/2/3. Extension chains, placement-control pointers, alternate-volume resolution and other ODS generations remain explicit gaps. Opening an ODS-2 view does not validate any enclosing CD-image trailer or decode nested backup savesets.",
	"archive.hunk_load":                        "Reads one non-overlaid Amiga HUNK_HEADER load module without loading or executing its code. Returns header (raw bytes, padded resident library name views, table_size, first, last and allocation sizes in bytes), one unnamed element in units containing the record stream, and entries for code/data/debug payloads. Uses the same record fields as hunk_objects. Checks header ranges, section count, section payload bounds and declared allocation capacity; BSS remains metadata. maximum_records bounds header items, blocks, symbols and relocation groups. Only ordinary longword RELOC32 load relocations are accepted; compact encodings, overlays and extended memory attributes remain explicit gaps. Resident libraries are listed, not resolved; no relocation is applied and no guest memory is allocated.",
	"archive.hunk_objects":                     "Reads concatenated classic Amiga and EHF HUNK_UNIT object libraries, preserving unit names and complete raw unit views in units. The entries list exposes code, data and debug payloads under unique unit/record paths for nested inspection. Each unit's records expose tag, flags, absolute offset, raw bytes, optional payload, memory_size, symbols and relocations. Names retain longword padding. Code, data, debug bytes and big-endian relocation offset arrays remain borrowed files; BSS has a declared memory_size and no payload. Checks record boundaries, section ordering and END markers. maximum_records bounds blocks, symbols and relocation groups together. Does not link, apply relocations, resolve symbols or allocate BSS; reference targets and offsets are metadata, not a validated linked program. Supports EHF PPC_CODE, RELRELOC26 and EXT_RELREF26 framing without applying relocations. Load modules require hunk_load; indexed libraries and overlays remain rejected rather than silently skipped.",
	"archive.arsenic":                          "Decodes one raw StuffIt method-15 fork, not a StuffIt container. Reconstructs adaptive arithmetic-coded MTF tokens, reverses BWT and optional block randomization, expands final runs, and validates the in-stream IEEE CRC32 for nonempty streams before returning an immutable in-memory file. maximum_decoded_bytes bounds logical output and stored input; maximum_block_bytes bounds each intermediate BWT block. Truncated input, invalid indices and exceeded limits fail explicitly. Use the enclosing container's declared fork length as an additional check; resource maps and nested archives require separate decoders.",
	"archive.lha":                              "Reads level-0 LHA headers and stored lh0 or Huffman/LZSS lh5 payloads. Checks header byte sums, file CRC-16, declared sizes, terminal marker, path components and duplicate paths. Returns entries, files and find(path). Entries preserve raw name/header bytes, the packed DOS timestamp, DOS attributes, method, CRC, decoded data and a borrowed stored view. Backslashes become path separators; parent traversal and empty components are rejected. maximum_entries bounds records; maximum_decoded_bytes bounds total decoded bytes and each stored payload. Other header levels, directory records, compression methods and self-extractors are explicit gaps. Nested containers are not automatically decoded.",
	"archive.bsd_dump":                         "Reads full, single-volume historical BSD dumps with 1024-byte records, magic60012 and old 128-byte UFS inodes, in either byte order. Validates header checksums, tape positions, record identity, address flags, complete inode payloads and directory structure. Returns entries, paths in files, find(path), dump date and raw allocated_map/dumped_map file views. Entries expose the same inode metadata and data views as filesystem.ufs; holes read as zeroes, hard links share data, symlinks are not followed and device nodes are metadata only. maximum_entries bounds both inode records and reachable paths; maximum_decoded_bytes bounds input size and cumulative logical inode bytes. Incremental dumps require a base image and are rejected, as are other dump generations and multi-volume streams. Dumped inode bitmap membership is checked against recovered records and the allocation map. Nested payloads require separate decoders.",
	"filesystem.ultrix_label":                  "Reads the eight-slot DEC/Ultrix disk label at byte16312, validating magic, active-table flag, signed 512-byte sector geometry and nonempty partition bounds. Returns block_size=512 and partitions with index, conventional a-h name, start_block, blocks and borrowed data. Empty slots remain present with data=None. Overlaps are preserved because the whole-disk slot overlaps individual filesystems; no partition is silently selected or treated as a duplicate. The label has no checksum or filesystem-type field, so inspect each selected partition with its own format reader. Does not scan for alternate labels, mount filesystems or execute boot code.",
	"filesystem.ufs":                           "Reads historical and 4.4BSD UFS1, UFS2 and NetBSD UFS2EA in either byte order, checking superblocks at byte8192 or byte65536. UFS2 uses 256-byte inodes and 64-bit pointers; UFS1 uses 128-byte inodes and 32-bit pointers. The declared inode format selects old 16-bit ownership/16-bit directory name lengths or modern 32-bit ownership/typed directories and inline symlinks. Checks fragment/cylinder-group geometry, inode and data ranges, 512-byte directory records, dot entries, directory type/inode agreement, duplicate names and directory cycles. Returns entries, paths, find(path), block_size, fragment_size, groups and inode_format. Entries preserve inode identity, mode, link count, ownership, timestamps, flags, device number and borrowed data views. Reads 12 direct blocks and single/double/triple indirect pointers; zero pointers remain sparse zeroes. maximum_blocks bounds mapping work; maximum_entries bounds reachable paths. Hard-linked files share data, symlinks are not followed and device nodes are metadata only. Unknown inode formats, extended-attribute enumeration and journal replay remain unsupported. filesystem.ffs_build separately constructs FFS1 roots. Nested archives require separate decoders.",
	"filesystem.cms_library":                   "Decodes a CMS LIBPDS library (MACLIB, TXTLIB and other F 80 libraries) from its fixed-format content, such as cms(...).read(index). Returns format, directory_bytes, directory_record, reserved (two header words kept uninterpreted) and entries with name, raw_name, flags, record, records and data. Several entries may name one member (for example TXTLIB entry points); a member ends before the next record beginning with X'61FFFF61'. Member data stays EBCDIC card images. DMSLIB-format TXTLIBs, LOADLIBs and CSLLIBs are rejected.",
	"filesystem.cms_unpack":                    "Decodes CMS packed (COPYFILE PACK) content, such as cms(...).read(index), from F 1024 container records (record_format='F') or variable container records (record_format='V'). Returns record_format (F or V), record_length, records, maximum_record and data: fixed records concatenated, or the 2-byte length-prefixed variable stream used by cms read. Verifies the end marker, whole fixed records, LRECL-bounded variable records and that the final container record's tail is blank or repeats the stream 1024 bytes earlier. Unobserved codes are rejected.",
	"filesystem.cms_module":                    "Decodes CMS MODULE content such as cms(...).read(index). Fixed modules are one headerless image. Variable modules return segments, each with header, entry, origin, end, end2, image, loader_table (name, raw_name, words), relocation_count, relocation_width (0, 4 or 5), relocation_data (raw entries), extra (records after the relocation list such as service history, or trailer bytes when there is none), program_object (raw IEWPLMH trailer or None) and trailer. Core-image records must sum exactly to end-origin; further segments (as in CP's CPLOAD) are found structurally. Loader entry size (20 or 24) is chosen by validation and ambiguity is rejected.",
	"filesystem.cms_loadlib":                   "Decodes a variable-format CMS LOADLIB (OS PDS simulation). Returns directory_bytes, directory_record, header and entries with name, raw_name, alias, record, records, user_data (OS load module attributes) and data (the member's load module records as a 2-byte length-prefixed stream). The directory is read from its own records; record 1's truncated copy is ignored. Members end before the X'61FFFF61' delimiter.",
	"filesystem.cms_csllib":                    "Decodes a CMS callable services library (CSLLIB). Returns name, date, time, identifiers, header and segments (one module segment per routine, in the cms_module segment shape; each Extra holds the routine's descriptor record, and the final one also holds the library index).",
	"filesystem.object_deck":                   "Decodes F 80 object card content (TEXT files, TXTLIB members). Returns symbols (module, name, raw_name, type, id, owner, address, value, flags, extended, kind) from ESD and XSD cards, ends, sections (SD/PC/CM sections assembled from TXT data with a symbol and data file), text_cards, relocations and other_cards (SYM, loader and control cards). TXT and RLD ESDIDs must be defined; ESDIDs restart after each END; XSD long names may span cards.",
	"filesystem.cp_directory":                  "Reads the z/VM CP object directory (DIRECTXA output) of a CKD volume, by default the active one selected by the VOL1 label (root overrides it with a page pointer: 2-byte cylinder, record, zero). Returns root, header, users, profiles (from the directory header; their devices are owned by SYSTEM) and groups (POSIX GID, name). Each user has name, flags, word (POSIX UID), device_count, block, devices (own), configuration (resolved chain: own then INCLUDEd profile devices), iucv (target, resource, message_limit, priority, ident_flags), namesave, distribution, account, acigroup, include, ipl, password_date, autolog, machine (XA, ESA, XC), nolog, classes (bit 31 = class A), storage_kb, maximum_storage_kb, maxconn, share_absolute, share_relative, posix_uid, posix_gid, posix_group and options (only option bits verified on media). Devices have address, type (X'80' MDISK, X'40' LINK, X'08' unit record, X'10' DEDICATE), owner, start, end, volume, mode, mode_code, password_flags, target_user, target_address, link_mode, device_type, spool_class, real_address and raw. Passwords stay encoded. The root's leaf chain, high keys, device counts, block owners and configuration order are verified; a superseded generation whose pages were reused fails. This is the directory CP runs with, which may differ from USER DIRECT source.",
	"filesystem.program_object":                "Validates a stored binder program object (IEWPLMH versions 2-5), such as a CMS module segment's program_object, and returns it as a file. Version 1 objects are rejected.",
	"filesystem.cms":                           "Opens the CMS EDF minidisk whose CMS1 label is record 3 of the given cylinder's first track in a CKD_P370/CKD_C370 image. cylinders, when nonzero, is the authoritative extent (for example from a CP directory MDISK statement); the label must fit it, so stale or overlapping labels fail. directory_origin selects an alternate directory block. Returns id, block_size, blocks, used_blocks, cylinders, maximum_cylinders, blocks_per_track, directory_origin, created (YYMMDDHHMMSS), label, files and the methods read(index, max_bytes=64MiB) -> file and verify(max_bytes=64MiB) -> record(files, bytes, errors). files entries expose name, type, mode, record_format (F or V), record_length, records, data_blocks, origin, levels, flags, date, century and fst. read returns EBCDIC content unchanged: fixed files are records*record_length bytes with unallocated blocks as zeros; variable files are the 2-byte length-prefixed record stream. Pointer-tree record counts and displacements are verified. Minidisk discovery, CP directory parsing and code page conversion stay in Starlark.",
	"filesystem.ckd_scan":                      "Validates every physical CKD track and exact cylinder-aligned EOF through a portable file. Returns tracks, records, data_bytes, key_bytes and image_bytes; max_tracks bounds the scan. Reads free space and record zero too, and propagates source checksum errors. Accepts uncompressed CKD_P370 and compressed CKD_C370 base images. With max_errors > 0 (at most 1M), every track implied by the cylinder-aligned image size is parsed and per-track failures are returned as errors (track, error) instead of stopping, failing only beyond max_errors; tracks counts successful tracks. Does not claim logical dataset decoding.",
	"filesystem.ckd_vvr":                       "Parses an observed primary Z VVDS volume record, returning component, cluster and catalog names, index/data flags, index root RBA, CI size, high-used and allocated byte bounds, control-area geometry, maximum record length and key position/length. Reports ci_addressed and converts qualified extended data-component CI counts to 64-bit byte bounds. Rejects incompatible headers, cell bounds and geometry; extended index roots and multivolume layout remain unsupported.",
	"filesystem.ckd_catalog_record":            "Parses an observed BCS record into kind, name, raw key, ordered type-03 relationship references and nested cells. Data/index component cells expose their names and children; other attributes retain raw bytes. Validates record length, key header, component lengths and subtree bounds.",
	"filesystem.ckd_vsam_records":              "Reads a resolved immutable VSAM data component through portable files. ESDS preserves physical record order; fixed RRDS preserves record numbers across vacant slots; KSDS follows the supplied active index root and verifies strictly increasing keys. Assembles spanned records only within a control area and matching update generation. Returns record number, RBA and unconverted data bytes. Explicit high-used bounds, CI/CA geometry and key metadata are required; allocation size is not substituted for high-used RBA. The optional index is traversed natively using header/section/key compression and active child pointers, excluding free intervals. Enforces interval, record-count, per-record and total-byte budgets. Does not infer catalog metadata, index roots, variable RRDS or extended-address formats.",
	"filesystem.ckd_control_interval":          "Decodes one explicitly selected VSAM data control interval (512 to 32768 bytes, multiple of 512). Returns eof, free_offset, free_length and records in data order. A zero CIDF is a software EOF marker; callers must establish the input is a VSAM data CI. Validates CIDF and backwards RDF boundaries, including repeated equal-length records. Rejects unsupported flags and spanning. Does not interpret indexes, RRDS slots, catalog relationships or high-used RBA; not a complete VSAM dataset reader.",
	"filesystem.ckd_igw_attributes":            "Enumerates the bounded active IGW attribute tree of an allocated-page file through native map-chain and child-link validation. Returns exact 20-byte keys, flags and uninterpreted values. Does not carve inactive pages or decode file payloads.",
	"filesystem.ckd_attribute_page":            "Parses one 4096-byte IGW AD page, returning level and cells with decompressed 20-byte key, uninterpreted flags and value bytes. Observed cell flags, including 0xc0, 0xd5, 0xe5 and 0xec, are preserved, not interpreted as liveness. Validates page framing, live-region bounds, group/cell lengths, anchored prefix compression, trailing zero suppression and leaf key ordering. Internal namespace separators may be shortened; full traversal validates actual leaf order. Does not resolve internal child identifiers, traverse a dataset, or decode PDSE members or HFS files.",
	"filesystem.openbsd_label":                 "Reads a version-1 OpenBSD or legacy version-0 BSD disklabel at label_offset, an explicit byte offset in the complete disk. The default 512 is the common standalone amd64 label location; for an MBR image pass the OpenBSD partition offset plus 512. Validates both magic values, XOR checksum, partition count, 48-bit sector addresses, declared disk/partition bounds and FFS geometry. Legacy labels use 32-bit sector addresses and explicit fragment geometry rather than the OpenBSD packed fields. Sector size is declared by the label, including 4096-byte sectors. Returns sector_size, total_sectors, bound_start, bound_end, uid, type_name, pack_name and partitions. Partitions preserve index, a-z/A-Z name, absolute start_sector, sectors, type, block_size, fragment_size, cylinders_per_group and borrowed read-only data. Empty slots have data=None; overlapping raw c remains present. Requires the complete declared disk; does not guess offsets, mount, repair, construct images or accept other disklabel versions.",
	"archive.adcr":                             "Decodes Aladdin ADCR01 and ADCR03 resources to in-memory files. Both validate the header, 24-bit decoded size, copy ranges and complete compressed-byte consumption. Version1 validates its additional stored-length framing and decodes control words with a 4096-entry phrase table and rolling insertion counter. Its optional dictionary supplies the first 18 bytes as an initial seed phrase; unobserved framing flags are rejected. Version3 validates recursively encoded Huffman tables and uses the last 65535 dictionary bytes as preceding history. Missing referenced history is always an error, never zero-filled. maximum_decoded_bytes bounds output and stored payload bytes. These wrappers have no checksum; verify decoded structures or independent hashes. The decoder never locates or executes loader code or selects an installation variant. Other ADCR versions remain unsupported.",
	"filesystem.apm":                           "Reads an Apple Partition Map as portable partition file views. block_size explicitly selects the 512-, 1024- or 2048-byte logical map; device_block_size and device_blocks retain independent driver-descriptor geometry. This distinction matters for CDs containing overlapping maps. Each partition exposes its effective block_size: a CDvr-tagged Apple_Driver43_CD record with a matching block-zero driver descriptor uses 2048-byte device units even in a 512-byte map; other records use the selected map units. Validates ER/PM signatures, consistent entry counts, allocated-partition bounds and nonoverlap. Returns partitions with index, raw name/type/processor bytes, start_block, blocks, logical data-range fields, status and boot metadata. data is the complete physical partition view; no inner filesystem is inferred. An out-of-image Apple_Free descriptor retains its declared geometry with data=None and complete=False; allocated partitions must fit fully. Boot metadata is not executed and boot checksums are not verified.",
	"archive.tome":                             "Reads classic kc0001 Apple installer Tome catalogs and decodes both forks using chunked InstaCompOne in memory. Validates catalog counts, unique IDs, payload ranges, nonoverlap, exact fork sizes, both decoded-fork checksums and complete compressed-input consumption. Returns entries with ID, raw name bytes, reversible ID-qualified paths, file type/creator, Finder flags, version, Mac-epoch dates, decoded data/resource files and original stored views. maximum_decoded_bytes bounds total decoded output and each stored fork. Catalog checksum values are preserved as data_checksum and resource_checksum; checksums_verified is True after validation. Supports the observed 00010000 binary and 00000000 seven-bit-text chunk framing, plus stored chunks selected by a leading 01 byte, with retained 32KiB history and command-aligned 64KiB blocks; other encodings fail explicitly. Resource maps and nested archives require separate decoders. Installer code never runs. catalog_kind distinguishes file catalogs (1) from individual-resource catalogs (2). Kind2 exposes resource_type and signed resource_id, with the decoded resource payload in data and an empty resource fork; file-only metadata is omitted. Unknown catalog kinds are rejected.",
	"archive.stuffit":                          "Reads the classic 22-byte/112-byte StuffIt archive generation, including self-extractors with an archive data fork. Validates declared archive size, header CRC-16/ARC, nested directory markers and each decoded fork CRC. Classic top-level counts are checked; STi2/ST46/ST50/ST60/ST65 installer counts are preserved separately as declared_count/top_level_count with root_count_verified=False because their selection semantics are not interpreted. STi2 repeated directory records remain in order. ST46 preserves repeated STcp/STde/STal/DIFF records with creator STin, exposing installer_record, one-based occurrence and raw declared fork sizes; copy/delete/alias metadata bytes are retained even when their logical data size is zero. ST60/ST65 additionally preserve STda/STmv metadata, including metadata naming existing directories without replacing them. No installation operations or patches are applied. ST60 alternative ordinary files are also retained as separate occurrences; other variants reject ordinary duplicate files, and ordinary file/directory conflicts still fail. Supports stored forks, method13 LZSS/Huffman with embedded or predefined tables, and method14 installer blocks with recursive Huffman tables and retained 256KiB history. Method14 validates block framing, decoded sizes and final fork CRCs; unavailable history is never invented. Returns version and entries preserving raw name bytes, reversible percent-escaped paths, file type/creator, Finder flags, Mac-epoch dates, resource/data methods and CRCs, separate data/resource files and original stored views. maximum_decoded_bytes bounds total decoded fork bytes and each stored fork. Also reads the StuffIt5 banner container with method15 (Arsenic) forks, validating the global, record and metadata CRC16 fields, parent/previous/file-next links, directory child counts and in-stream fork CRC32. StuffIt5 fork CRC16 attributes are None because integrity comes from the method15 stream. Other StuffIt5 fork methods, other compression methods, encryption and StuffItX remain explicit gaps; executable self-extractor code never runs. Nested containers and resource payloads require their own decoders.",
	"archive.compactpro":                       "Decodes a single-volume CompactPro archive, including self-extractors whose data fork contains the archive. Validates catalog CRC, directory descendant counts, payload ranges and the combined CRC of each file's decoded resource and data forks. Supports RLE and block-based LZH followed by RLE, retaining both forks and their stored views. Returns entries with reversible percent-escaped paths, raw name bytes, file type/creator, Finder flags, Mac-epoch dates, compression flags, CRC, data/resource and their sizes. maximum_decoded_bytes bounds total decoded fork bytes and each stored fork. Encrypted and multi-volume archives fail explicitly; no self-extractor code runs. Nested archives and resource payload formats remain separate layers.",
	"filesystem.hfs":                           "Reads classic HFS, HFS+/HFSX and embedded HFS+ volumes without mounting. Returns raw volume name, entries, paths and exact find(path). Entries expose distinct data/resource forks, xattrs, reversible escaped paths, Finder/catalog metadata, Unix modes/uid/gid and link targets. Hardlink inode aliases share decoded inode contents while raw_data/raw_resource retain physical alias forks. Symlinks are not followed. Inline and fork-backed attributes have independent extent-overflow support. Shared decmpfs types 3/4 (inline/resource zlib with stored-block escape) and 9 (stored inline) decode lazily with exact logical lengths and checksums; resource zlib allocations may have unused trailing bytes. Other compression types fail unless raw_forks=True explicitly requests physical forensic forks. compression_type identifies decoded files. Does not mount, repair or execute filesystem contents.",
	"archive.mac_resource":                     "Parses a classic Macintosh resource fork, checking map, reference, name and payload bounds and repeated type groups. Duplicate IDs retain every record in map order; duplicate_ids reports collisions, occurrence is one-based, and later occurrences have ordinal path suffixes. Returns fork attributes and entries with resource_type (four raw bytes), signed id, name (raw bytes or None), attributes, offset, size and data. Uncompressed data is a borrowed view; supported Apple dcmp 0/1/2/3 compressed resources are decoded and validated in memory. Compression requires both attribute bit0 and the a89f6572 payload tag, matching Resource Manager; raw attributes remain available when the bit is set on ordinary data. compressed reports actual decoding, while stored_data and stored_size preserve the original representation. maximum_decoded_bytes bounds total decoded compressed payload bytes and each stored compressed input. Unknown codecs and unsupported token variants fail explicitly. The synthetic path uses hexadecimal type and signed ID without interpreting names as host paths. Payload-specific formats remain separate layers.",
	"filesystem.xfs":                           "Reads version-4 and checksum-protected version-5 XFS directory trees using borrowed local, extent-list and multi-level bmap-btree views. Checks levels, key ranges, sibling links, cycles and extent counts; sparse and unwritten data reads as zeros. Version-5 superblocks, v3 inodes, BMA3 trees and directory blocks validate CRC32C, metadata UUID, owner and physical address. Supports directory formats 1/2/3 and file-type extensions. realtime_file explicitly supplies the separate realtime device. Returns entries, paths and find(path); symlinks are never followed. Does not mount, replay logs, repair or build filesystems, decode attribute forks, or accept unsupported incompatible features such as bigtime and large-extent-count layouts.",
	"windows.memory_image":                     "Borrows a file as a read-only physical capture. Optional ranges map (physical_start, file_offset, size); omitted ranges cover the whole file, while an empty list captures nothing. Gaps remain unavailable, not zero-filled. Keep the source unchanged while views exist; this does not parse crash dumps or discover RAM ranges.",
	"windows.icon":                             "Selects an icon image from an ICO, PE or NE file using its index and requested dimensions. Returns image bytes with actual dimensions, bit depth and resource identity; it does not launch or render the executable.",
	"archive.ar":                               "Parses Unix ar (including Microsoft COFF-library NUL-terminated long names) or AIX small indexed ar into ordered member metadata and borrowed file views. find(name, occurrence) distinguishes duplicate names. AIX archives follow double-linked member order rather than physical order, validating first/last endpoints, member-table names and offsets, optional symbol references, free-list links and non-overlapping ranges. Deleted members are not active files. maximum_entries and maximum_metadata bound indexing; no linking, object loading or installation is performed. AIX big indexed archives remain unsupported.",
	"archive.aws":                              "Reads an AWS tape image into logical records and tape marks, checking block framing and previous-length links. Each record exposes data, original offset, physical block count and tape_mark; data is None for marks. Does not interpret record payloads such as DDR disk dumps.",
	"archive.compress":                         "Decodes a UNIX compress (.Z) stream with 9–16-bit LZW codes, legacy or block mode and packing realignment at width changes or resets. Enforces maximum_bytes; the format has no checksum, so enclosing size/checksum metadata should be validated separately. zero_padding=True explicitly accepts a final incomplete all-zero code after at least one decoded literal, for fixed-block media packages such as Ultrix setld subsets. It does not trim input bytes, suppress nonzero truncated codes or strip decoded zeroes. Use this option only with independent container and inventory validation; the default retains strict incomplete-code checks.",
	"archive.pack":                             "Decodes a UNIX pack (1f1e) Huffman stream, checking its symbol tree, end marker, padding and declared output size. Enforces maximum_bytes and returns a file; useful for compressed IRIX manual pages nested inside inst images.",
	"archive.irix_image":                       "Opens an IRIX inst software image with its IDB index and explicit image name, such as 4Dwm.sw. Returns files in physical record order, retaining duplicate paths, source names, IDB attributes, modes, owners and groups. Reads unwrap indexed .Z payloads and validate decoded sizes and BSD checksums. Supports headerless tape images and indexes without offsets, matching repeated names by IDB occurrence order; explicit offsets take precedence. Checks all tape padding is zero and block-aligned. Does not select subsystems or execute installation actions.",
	"archive.irix_tape":                        "Reads an SGI standalone tape directory after checking its header checksum and member ranges. Returns entries with path, size, offset and data file views; use filesystem.efs on an mr member to inspect the nested miniroot. This is the standalone archive format, not an inst software image or raw tape-record framing.",
	"archive.irix_idb":                         "Parses an IRIX installation database into ordered items, preserving duplicates and non-file records. Each item exposes kind, path, source, mode, owner, group and attributes. Use the logical subsystem attributes to pair renamed overlay images with archive.irix_image; inspecting an index does not select a machine variant or execute installation actions. Validates quoting, numeric modes, entry limits and zero tape padding.",
	"archive.bru":                              "Reads classic uncompressed BRU backups, validating each 2 KiB record's signed-byte checksum, archive identity, sequence and member name. Returns ordered entries with type, mode, ownership, link target and data file views assembled across record headers. Checks the end record and trailing buffer padding, including padding-block checksums. Compressed/extended members and incomplete multivolume inputs fail explicitly; no restore commands are executed.",
	"filesystem.sgi":                           "Validates an SGI volume-header checksum and returns partition and boot-directory file views. Partitions expose index, partition_type, start_block, blocks, data and complete; addresses are 512-byte basic blocks. A whole-volume descriptor may exceed a CD image: its declared blocks are retained, data covers only available bytes, and complete is false. Filesystem partitions and boot files must fit in full. Does not infer an inner filesystem from a filename.",
	"filesystem.efs":                           "Reads an IRIX EFS volume into entries and case-sensitive paths, resolving direct and indirect extents without mounting. find(path) returns an entry or None; entries expose data, entry_type, size, inode, mode, uid, gid and modified. Accepts a trimmed free tail only when the allocation bitmap proves the omitted region is free and all referenced extents fit the original input. Symlink data remains the link target and is never followed; device entries have no data.",
	"archive.udif":                             "Opens XML-backed, single-segment UDIF v4 disk images. Returns disk (complete logical disk), original xml and trailer, BLKX tables (name, offset, size, raw), and verify(). Raw, zero-fill, ignored, zlib and bzip2 chunks use bounded caches. verify() reads stored data and checks declared data-fork, table and master CRC32s; ignored sectors are excluded from table checksums. Rejects encryption, segmented images, unsupported codecs and overlapping/gapped maps. Does not authenticate signatures or infer filesystems.",
	"archive.xar":                              "Indexes XAR v1 without extraction. Returns entries, files, original decoded toc, raw heap and find(path). Entries preserve path, kind, ID, mode, uid/gid, link target, encoding, raw/data file views, size and verify(). Verifies declared compressed-TOC checksum on open; member verify() checks archived/extracted checksums and exact decoded lengths. Supports stored, zlib and bzip2 members. Safe paths, XML depth/size, heap extents and index sizes are bounded. Signature metadata remains in TOC/heap but signatures are not authenticated.",
	"archive.pbzx":                             "Opens chunked Apple installer payloads as a lazy read-only concatenated file. Validates decoded/stored chunk lengths, XZ indexes and final short-chunk framing; stored chunks are borrowed. Uses a 64 MiB decoded cache and 64 MiB per-chunk bound. Optional nonnegative replay_cache_bytes adds a byte-bounded volatile indexed S2 cache of verified decoded chunks. Replay and decoded retention use independent 64 KiB blocks, avoiding whole-chunk work on small reads; first XZ verification still covers the complete original chunk. No eager reads or host intermediates. The returned file.stats dictionary separates cumulative successful xz_chunks/xz_bytes from replay_blocks/replay_bytes and includes decoded_cache/replay_cache counters; snapshots under concurrent reads are observational, not transactional. The budget charges exact retained encoded allocations; transient decode buffers are additional. Zero disables it. Omitted maximum_bytes has no total decoded ceiling; explicit positive limits are enforced. Read complete contents to force XZ checksums. Parse the resulting CPIO separately.",
	"archive.dcs":                              "Expands a DCS v1 CBS component-store container to an in-memory read-only file using native LZMS decoding. maximum_bytes (1..512MiB) bounds both stored and expanded sizes; framing, block counts, lengths and trailing data are checked before returning content. This is separate from WIM resource compression and does not verify manifest hashes or catalog trust.",
	"archive.lzfse":                            "Opens Apple LZFSE v1/v2, LZVN and stored-block streams as a read-only file. Bounds block size/count and retains a 64 MiB decoded cache plus 256 KiB match history. Backward reads outside cache replay preceding dependent blocks. Validates entropy states, source/output lengths, match distances and end marker; LZFSE has no content checksum. Explicit maximum_bytes bounds total decoded bytes. No host or C decoder is used.",
	"archive.pbze":                             "Opens a bounded Apple pbze chunk stream as a lazy read-only file using native LZFSE decoding. Validates chunk framing and decoded lengths; maximum_bytes bounds total decoded size. Uses a 64 MiB decoded cache and a 64 MiB per-chunk bound. No host decoder or intermediate files are used.",
	"binary.rpm_metadata":                      "Reads RPM package metadata with a version-3/4 lead and v1 headers, without executing scripts, decompressing payloads or authenticating signatures. Returns fields (named stored tags), tags and signature_tags (numeric keys with type/count/value), source_package, payload_offset and a borrowed payload file. STRING is a string; numeric values and STRING_ARRAY/I18NSTRING are lists; BIN is bytes. Missing fields are absent; localized strings are not selected. Unknown tags are retained; unknown types, duplicate tags, unsupported leads, invalid offsets/counts/alignment and unterminated strings fail. Entry/byte limits cover both headers; decoded values are also bounded against alias amplification. Maximum entries is 1M and maximum bytes is 256MiB. No digest, immutable-region or signature verification is performed; this is inspection, not package validation or an RPM database reader.",
	"binary.berkeley_db":                       "Reads BSD Berkeley DB version-3 B-trees with inline keys and values in either byte order. Returns page_size, little_endian and entries with borrowed key/value file views, preserving on-disk comparator order. Validates root traversal, page bounds, overlapping records, cycles and leaf sibling links. Rejects overflow records, recno databases and newer formats explicitly; does not execute or infer a database-specific comparator.",
	"binary.elf_symbols":                       "Reads bounded ELF64 static/dynamic symbols and load segments from a portable file, in either byte order. Returns type, machine, entry, little_endian, symbols (name, address, size, section, binding, type, visibility, dynamic), and segments (address, offset, file_size, memory_size, flags). Addresses are ELF values, not runtime-relocated addresses; callers supply mapping/load bias. Zero-sized/undefined symbols are preserved, not assigned inferred extents. Rejects extended numbering, duplicate symbol-table kinds, compressed symbol/string metadata, malformed extents/names and resource-limit violations. Does not read host symbols or load code.",
	"binary.perf":                              "Reads finalized little-endian Linux PERFILE2 perf.data directly from a file. Returns attributes, samples, mappings, comms, lost, total_lost, unknown_records (record type to count), records and little_endian. Samples expose ip, pid, tid, time, address, id, stream_id, cpu, period, identifier and a present bit mask; absent fields are None. Each record retains its file offset and raw misc bits, so streams can be ordered and CPU modes distinguished. MMAP/MMAP2 records expose address, length, page_offset, filename and available inode/build-ID/protection metadata. Mapping, COMM and loss records include a sample_id trailer record. Attributes retain sample_type, read_format, flags, IDs, sample_id_all, use_clock_id and clock_id (None unless explicitly selected). Times retain the recorded clock domain; no symbols, paths or clock conversion are inferred. Rejects pipe/unfinalized input, unsupported sample layouts, heterogeneous attribute layouts, compression and AUX streams. All limits must be positive. Records and samples are each capped at 10 million; maximum_string_bytes bounds aggregate decoded names and is capped at 64 MiB. Limits and malformed records fail explicitly rather than returning a truncated profile.",
	"archive.apple_archive":                    "Reads bounded AA01 Apple Archive records. Returns path, kind, borrowed data and fields keyed by the original three-letter names. Supports unsigned integer, path, timestamp and blob fields; timestamps contain seconds and nanoseconds. Rejects unknown encodings and malformed extents. Does not unpack to a host directory or infer archive authentication.",
	"archive.im4p":                             "Parses an ASN.1 IM4P firmware envelope with bounded DER depth/node counts, including canonical high-tag-number private properties. Returns payload_type, description, borrowed payload, extras (original optional DER records) and expected_size (LZFSE metadata when present, otherwise zero). Does not decrypt, decompress, authenticate signatures or infer a filesystem; pass payload explicitly to archive.lzfse or the appropriate format reader.",
	"archive.img4":                             "Constructs an IMG4 DER wrapper around a validated complete IM4P file and optional IM4M manifest. Borrows source records without changing payload or metadata; the manifest uses explicit context tag zero. Does not create a ticket, sign or authenticate the image. Omitting the manifest does not bypass firmware authentication.",
	"archive.aea_metadata":                     "Reads bounded AEA1 header metadata without decrypting or authenticating it. Returns profile, scrypt_strength, borrowed auth_data and values (key to binary bytes). Rejects malformed or duplicate records. Metadata may contain encryption keys; inspect names before printing values. Never contacts a key service.",
	"archive.aea":                              "Opens a symmetric profile-1 AEA1 archive natively. key is 32 raw bytes; None selects the explicit hex encryption_key metadata field. Returns a lazy plaintext file, encrypted_size, segment_size, segments_per_cluster, compression, checksum and verify(). Authenticates root/cluster/segment HMACs and optional padding; verifies plaintext SHA256 or Murmur checksums. Supports stored, LZFSE, LZVN and zlib segments with bounded allocations and a one-segment cache. maximum_bytes bounds declared decoded size. verify() reads the entire archive. Rejects other profiles/strengths and unavailable keys; never fetches keys, launches tools or writes intermediate files.",
	"filesystem.apfs_sealed_tree":              "Constructs a packed headerless APFS filesystem metadata B-tree in memory from (key,value) byte tuples already in APFS traversal order. Returns file, root_hash, nodes and records. Supports power-of-two node sizes 4096 through 65536 and SHA256, SHA512/256, SHA384 or SHA512. Reproduces sequential node splitting, relative node identifiers, conservative index preflight, freed separator bytes and child hashes for the observed PCC generation. Bounds records and tree storage; does not install the tree into a container, create integrity metadata or snapshots, supply file extents, or claim authentication without a trusted matching root seal.",
	"filesystem.apfs":                          "Inspects a raw APFS container natively. Returns the selected container superblock byte view, block_size, blocks, transaction, allocation() and volumes with name, canonical UUID string, ID, role, flags, tree metadata, snapshot_count, snapshot_tree, snapshot_tree_type and browse(raw=False), plus the selected volume superblock byte view. snapshots() validates snapshot metadata and name records; snapshot(name) opens that immutable filesystem through its physical superblock and historical object-map versions, exposing the same read APIs. browse reconstructs entries, paths and exact find(path) from ordinary unencrypted filesystem trees; each entry exposes entry_type, ID/private_id, mode/uid/gid, target, size, data, raw_data, compression_type, xattrs and xattr_streams (nonresident attribute stream IDs). allocation() validates the selected checkpoint maps, space manager, chunk bitmaps and free counts, returning bounded free_ranges and metadata summaries; it does not allocate or modify blocks. Supports sparse files, regular hardlinks, embedded/streamed xattrs and shared decmpfs types 3/4/9. raw=True retains compression metadata and physical streams with data=None for compressed files. Checks object checksums, object-map versions, tree and extent bounds. Symlinks remain entries. Sealed volumes support headerless hashed metadata trees and separate file-extent trees. seal_info() returns integrity version/flags/hash type, advertised root hash and original root node. verify_seal(expected=None) validates all metadata node hashes, optionally against external trusted root bytes; it counts file-data hash records but does not read or verify file contents. records() copies bounded (key,value) metadata byte tuples in APFS traversal order. object(id) returns the selected virtual object mapping and borrowed bytes. canonical_records(time_ns, hashes) normalizes inode/directory/stream metadata, omits physical extents and merges APFS file-info hash tuples. seal_image(records, expected, snapshot_name='', snapshot_time_ns=0, companions=[]) constructs an immutable standalone single-checkpoint container with these records, a mandatory matching external SHA-256 root, preserved file data, updated maps and allocation accounting; it requires an unencrypted snapshot-free volume and a single-block internal-pool bitmap. A nonempty snapshot_name and explicit positive snapshot_time_ns additionally construct a named boot snapshot, its physical superblock, object-map snapshot index and extended metadata, while moving the extent-reference tree into the snapshot and starting an empty live extent-reference tree. companions accepts up to 16 dictionaries with name, role, created_ns, optional directories and optional entries. Entry dictionaries supply path, entry_type (directory/file/symlink), mode, optional uid/gid, file data and symlink target. Native construction copies plaintext into new blocks, builds bounded mutable virtual filesystem trees, physical extent references and data-stream refcounts, creates missing directory parents and synthetic root entries, expands the container volume slots, and assigns deterministic UUIDs. Data shares an existing system volume group; ungrouped sources remain ungrouped. Bounds include 128 directories, 1024 explicit entries, 8 MiB per file and 32 MiB aggregate data, with a single bounded extent-reference leaf. Existing source IDs, compression and xattrs are not copied. Native directory, mapping, allocation and seal checks run before returning. The result includes snapshot_name, snapshot_transaction and deterministic snapshot_uuid. It retires historical checkpoints and is not a live-disk journal operation. File-content hash verification remains the caller’s responsibility. Encrypted and fusion volumes and ephemeral filesystem trees are unsupported; no mounting, repair or execution is performed.",
	"archive.bom":                              "Returns every flat Apple BOMStore path-inventory record, including architecture variants sharing a path and trailing leaf-chain nodes missing from branch indexes. Records preserve kind, BOM ID, mode, uid/gid, mtime, declared size, architecture, checksum or link target/device number. BOM describes metadata only and has no file contents. maximum_entries bounds indexing. The auto tree preserves duplicate variants under %00bom-variants with bom_path metadata.",
	"archive.cpio":                             "Indexes ASCII CPIO directly into flat metadata dictionaries using bounded forward metadata reads, without following links or instantiating special files. Supports newc, CRC newc, odc and concatenated archives; maximum_entries bounds original records, with contextual limit errors. Regular data is borrowed. archive identifies the trailer-delimited hardlink namespace. apple_double=True explicitly folds ._ sidecars into finder_info, resource and named xattrs using bounded ATTR decoding; malformed metadata and missing targets fail rather than discarding data.",
	"archive.cpio_build":                       "Constructs a deterministic Linux-compatible newc archive from Unix entry dictionaries. Preserves modes, ownership, timestamps, symbolic links, hardlinks and device numbers. Synthesizes missing parent directories, rejects duplicate or unsafe paths, and borrows payload readers without extracting them.",
	"archive.gzip_members":                     "Validates individual gzip members and returns exact compressed-member file views. CRC and size checks cover every member; maximum_bytes bounds total expanded data and maximum_members bounds indexing. No expanded payload is retained. Trailing junk is rejected.",
	"filesystem.ext4":                          "Reads a clean ext2/3/4 image without mounting. Returns flat entry records with Unix metadata and lazy file data; symbolic links and device nodes are metadata, never followed. Handles multi-level extents, sparse/unwritten ranges, indirect blocks, indexed directories, 64-bit descriptors and metadata checksums. Rejects unsupported incompatible features and images requiring journal replay. Positive entry/depth limits bound traversal.",
	"filesystem.ffs_build":                     "Builds a sparse FFS1 root from Unix entry dictionaries, preserving ownership, special permissions, symlinks, hardlinks and BSD device numbers. Payload readers are borrowed. Raw layout is for FreeBSD/NetBSD; openbsd adds an MBR and disklabel. Size is filesystem capacity excluding the OpenBSD prefix; zero estimates capacity. Capacity and total file payload are bounded to 32 GiB. No host extraction, mounting or mkfs.",
	"filesystem.ext4_build":                    "Builds a sparse ext4 image from Unix entry dictionaries using native 4096-byte blocks, extents and linear directories. Size is explicit and block-aligned; metadata is bounded at 256 MiB. inode_count optionally sets minimum total inode capacity, including reserved inodes, rounded up to block-group geometry; zero sizes from input entries. Larger values reserve free inodes for later guest writes, subject to the metadata and disk-size bounds. Preserves ownership, permissions, links and devices. Images have no journal; file payloads are borrowed immutable readers. UUID is 32 hexadecimal digits; label is at most 16 bytes. ext2=True builds a revision-1 ext2 image instead (block maps, filetype and large_file features only) for kernels without ext4.",
	"binary.ppc_instructions":                  "Inspects a four-byte-aligned POWER instruction range without execution. Accepts source, offset=0, size=256, address=0, byte_order=big and maximum=4096. Reads at most 64KiB, returns offset/address/length/word/opcode/GNU text/typed operands, resolved 64-bit branch target and explicit decode errors. Absolute branch labels are sign-extended for target; upstream GNU display uses 32-bit label spelling. Prefixed instructions consume eight bytes; unknown words consume four and are not proof of executable code. Maximum is at most 16384; source bounds, alignment, address overflow and instruction budget fail explicitly.",
	"binary.macho":                             "Inspects a bounded little-endian Mach-O64 image (architecture x86_64 or arm64), optionally selected from a big-endian fat32/fat64 universal container. cpu_subtype optionally selects an exact uint32 subtype (including arm64e capability bits) and validates the thin header; omitted selection rejects ambiguous CPU matches. Exposes the selected cpu_subtype, initial thread entry PC (zero when absent), borrowed file, segments (name, address, size, file_offset, file_size, flags), original symbols (name, address, type, section, description), dyld exports (name, address, flags, resolver, reexport_ordinal, import_name), indirect imports (name, kind stub/pointer, address, size), and fileset_entries (name, address, file_offset) for MH_FILESET kernel collections. fileset(name) opens an embedded Mach-O through the same reader, preserving collection-relative segment and linkedit offsets. Bound filesets with stripped symbol tables may contain zero indirect-symbol placeholders; these expose no named imports. Fileset offsets refer to the containing image; entries are not independent extracted binaries. at(address, size) returns borrowed file-backed virtual bytes; zero-fill, unmapped, ambiguous and overflowing ranges fail. Header, command, string, symbol and export-trie traversal bounds and aggregate name/prefix allocations are enforced. Export trie support includes ordinary, absolute, weak, thread-local, reexport and stub/resolver terminals; it does not resolve reexport dependencies. Does not relocate, link or execute the image.",
	"binary.software_update_catalog":           "Reads plain or gzip Apple software-update catalogs and returns installer products as id/metadata dictionaries in ID order. Selects OSInstall or SharedSupport InstallAssistant identifiers, preserving original package URLs, int64 sizes, digests, dates and Distribution locations. Does not fetch URLs or filter host compatibility. XML is decoded per product, bounded to 128MiB stored/expanded input, 8MiB per product, 4M XML tokens and depth128; each product retains plist value limits. Binary catalogs use the existing stricter plist limits. Rejects duplicate product/catalog keys, malformed XML/plists and corrupt gzip.",
	"binary.software_update_distribution":      "Reads bounded installer-gui-script XML (8MiB, 4M tokens, depth128). Returns title, raw_title, version, build, auxinfo, options, scripts and localizations. Prefers macOSProductVersion/macOSProductBuildVersion to VERSION/BUILD. Resolves observed double-quoted localization title assignments; unsupported localization grammar remains in raw_title/localizations. Scripts are retained as text, never executed, and external entities are not followed. This is metadata, not an eligibility decision.",
	"binary.plist":                             "Decodes bounded XML 1.0 or bplist00 property lists into dict/list, bytes, string, bool, int64, float and opaque round-trippable plist_date values. Binary null becomes None; unsupported UID and 16-byte integers fail explicitly. Enforces 64MiB input/decoded data, 100000 visited values and depth128, validates reference/offset bounds, rejects cycles, duplicate keys and invalid Unicode. Does not follow XML external entities.",
	"binary.plist_encode":                      "Encodes supported plist values as deterministic XML1.0 in an in-memory file. Preserves plist_date values from decoding. Enforces aggregate conversion/output byte, node and depth limits, including dictionary keys. Rejects None, non-finite floats, non-string keys, invalid XML characters and integers outside int64.",
	"binary.appledouble_metadata":              "Decodes FinderInfo, resource forks and macOS ATTR named attributes as separate borrowed views. Absolute ATTR offsets, 64 KiB header/table bound, names, duplicate attributes and overlapping data extents are checked. Unknown entry IDs are retained in other. Does not apply metadata or follow links.",
	"binary.appledouble":                       "Parses AppleDouble version 2 headers and returns an integer-entry-ID dictionary of borrowed files. Validates table bounds, duplicate IDs and non-overlapping extents. Preserves unknown entries without interpreting them. FinderInfo (9), resource fork (2) and any ATTR extension remain raw bytes; does not apply metadata or modify files.",
	"filesystem.hfs_build":                     "Builds a bounded unjournaled case-sensitive HFSX version-5 volume natively in memory from UNIX entry dictionaries. size is the volume byte length; payloads are borrowed file/string/bytes values. Entries support path, mode, uid, gid, mtime, data, target, hardlink, major, minor, finder_info (32 bytes), resource, xattrs (name to file/bytes), owner_flags and admin_flags. Preserves regular-file hardlinks through native private inodes; directory hardlinks are unsupported. Paths use POSIX components (colon becomes catalog slash); names use HFS canonical decomposition. Allocation, catalog and attribute trees include free growth nodes with bounded metadata. Does not build partitions, journal, execute guest tools or write intermediate host files.",
	"archive.gzip":                             "Opens concatenated gzip streams as a lazy read-only file with a 1 MiB decoded cache. Forward reads reuse the decoder; backward reads outside the cache replay it. Checksums are validated as data is consumed; size scans the complete stream without retaining it. Omitted maximum_bytes has no decoded-length ceiling; an explicit positive maximum is enforced. cache=True requires explicit positive maximum_bytes, eagerly validates every member to EOF, and returns an immutable paged RAM snapshot supporting random access without replay or host files; integrity or overflow failures return no partial result. Does not interpret an archive inside the stream.",
	"archive.bzip2":                            "Opens concatenated bzip2 streams, including legacy randomized blocks, as a lazy read-only file with a bounded decoded block cache. Block and stream checksums are checked during reads; size scans the complete stream. Omitted maximum_bytes has no decoded-length ceiling; an explicit positive maximum is enforced. cache=True requires explicit positive maximum_bytes, eagerly validates every member to EOF, and returns an immutable paged RAM snapshot supporting random access without replay or host files; integrity or overflow failures return no partial result. Does not interpret an archive inside the stream.",
	"archive.burn_containers":                  "Reads WiX v3 Burn attached CAB container boundaries from PE metadata, returning portable file slices in UX-first order. Rejects unsupported, detached, or truncated layouts without executing the bootstrapper.",
	"archive.c2r_database":                     "Decodes a bounded version-0x30 Office UnityDB into frozen literal tables. Checks declared extents, zlib framing and decoded SQL SHA-256; SQL statements are never executed. Supports the emitted integer/string INSERT subset, not arbitrary SQLite. Integrity checks do not authenticate the publisher.",
	"archive.c2r":                              "Indexes an Office Click-to-Run full stream using a schema-5 UnityDB. Entries expose package-relative path, size, packages, data and verify(); find is case-insensitive. Lazy reads verify stored-block SHA256 and bounded zlib decoding. verify() also checks the complete logical-file SHA256. Metadata references, block coverage, source extents and paths are validated before returning. Uses one shared decoded-block cache; keep both sources unchanged. Does not apply deltas, install Office, interpret App-V manifests, activate products, or authenticate catalog signatures. Pin or authenticate metadata separately.",
	"archive.cab":                              "Parses a Microsoft Cabinet file and exposes its members as file views. Folder decompression is shared when caching is enabled, so several entries in one compressed folder need not decode it repeatedly. Optional lzx_checkpoint_bytes (2..64 MiB, requires cache=True; zero disables) uses source-backed LZX block-boundary history checkpoints rather than a retained whole folder. First access decodes a complete folder, retaining compressed histories and SHA-256 hashes that bind future decoded frames to those initial bytes. It is not signature/trust verification. Sources must remain stable for member lifetimes. Checkpoint spacing is a target: a 24-bit LZX block can extend it; interleaved random access may cost more decoding than ordinary caching. Each member exposes frozen cab_metadata containing raw DOS date/time, attributes, folder index, set_id and cabinet index; reading metadata does not decompress the member.",
	"archive.cab_set":                          "Opens related Cabinet files as one set, resolving files and compressed data that span cabinet boundaries. The caller supplies the cabinet files; the parser does not search host directories.",
	"archive.installer":                        "Recognizes a supported installer container, including supported embedded payloads, and returns an inspection object with files and a declarative installation plan. It does not run the installer or apply that plan.",
	"archive.installer_probe":                  "Inspects a candidate installer and returns detection details and diagnostics as a dictionary. Use it to distinguish unsupported packaging from a recognized payload before requesting a full plan.",
	"archive.installscript":                    "Parses compiled InstallShield InstallScript into functions, callbacks, calls and effects. The result supports bounded evaluation of the modeled script semantics; parsing does not execute a host installer.",
	"archive.installshield":                    "Combines an InstallShield header, cabinet files and optional externally supplied files into an archive view. Exposes file groups, components and shortcuts needed for installation planning.",
	"archive.kwaj":                             "Decodes a Microsoft KWAJ-compressed input into a file, enforcing the decoded-size bound. Use kwaj_info when the header's original name and compression method are also needed.",
	"archive.nsis":                             "Decodes NSIS 2 ANSI non-solid stored/DEFLATE metadata and file payloads natively, without running installer code or extracting host files. Stable /files/NNNNNN names identify File instruction indices, preserving duplicate filenames and shared payloads. files lists names; find and indexing return readable files; entries retain name, instruction, source, file and output_directory_hint; instructions, sections and string(offset) expose symbolic metadata for inspection. Directory hints are not resolved destinations. maximum_bytes bounds cumulative unique decoded payload bytes (512 MiB default). Other limits match nsis_list. The same payload is recognized by archive.installer and auto. installer.plan supports selected-section straight-line effects and stops at unresolved control flow; explicit half-open ranges select audited product-policy regions and are reported as explicit_ranges=True. Supported effects include file writes, directories, attributes, string assignments, planned-file copy/deletion, registry values and native WriteUninstaller reconstruction. This is not a general NSIS script VM; callers must reject unresolved plans and supply explicit variables. Solid streams, other codecs, Unicode strings and modified opcode tables are unsupported.",
	"archive.nsis_list":                        "Lists File instructions from the NSIS 2 ANSI non-solid stored/NSIS-DEFLATE metadata layout without executing the installer or reading member contents. Returns format, header_offset, data_offset, header_size, header_compression, instruction_count and ordered entries. Entries retain name, raw_name bytes, instruction index, output_directory_hint, directory_instruction, relative data_offset, absolute offset of the member length word, packed_size, compressed and filetime. Repeated names and payload references are preserved. Directory hints are only the nearest preceding SetOutPath in bytecode order, not resolved installation destinations; variables, shell folders and language references remain symbolic, and literal dollars are doubled. Version-dependent variables remain numeric. This is a listing, not an installation plan or readable file view: no decoded sizes, CRC verification or extraction are claimed. Solid streams, other codecs and Unicode metadata are not supported. Zero limits select defaults; limits bound signature scanning, packed/decoded metadata, rendered text and instruction count.",
	"archive.kwaj_info":                        "Returns the decoded KWAJ file together with its original name, method and compressed/decoded sizes. This preserves wrapper metadata that archive.kwaj omits.",
	"archive.sevenzip":                         "Parses a 7-Zip archive into member file views with bounded entry count, metadata and decoder dictionary size. Unsupported compression methods fail explicitly.",
	"archive.sfp":                              "Parses an SFP installer archive and exposes member paths, timestamps, stored sizes and payload file views. It does not install the contents.",
	"archive.szdd":                             "Decodes the legacy Microsoft SZDD single-file compression format and returns a file. maximum bounds decoded output rather than the compressed input alone.",
	"archive.tar":                              "With a directory, serializes its entries into tar bytes, optionally compressed. With a file, parses a tar archive and preserves ordered entries, metadata and duplicate-name occurrences.",
	"archive.wim":                              "Parses a Windows Imaging Format archive and exposes its image contents through file views. The archive reader handles its supported compression internally without mounting an image. Nonnegative cache_bytes bounds retained buffers shared with reference archives; zero disables retention. Large solid LZMS archives split that same budget between decoded chunks, a small decoded hot-file tier and compressed in-memory blobs (whole small files and at most 32MiB per segment of large files), keyed by the complete WIM SHA-1 and segment ordinal. No decoded data is persisted. cache_stats.bytes/entries/peak_bytes aggregate these tiers; blob_bytes counts encoded retained bytes, blob_hits/blob_loads count compressed-tier accesses, and recent_files is a bounded chronological window of the last 64 cold file reads. prefetch(paths) validates at most 200000 explicit file paths before payload reads, deduplicates content, and prepares eligible solid files in physical chunk order with at most four joined workers; returns the unique source byte count. Prefetch does not pin entries or exceed the cache budget, and ordinary non-solid files are not eagerly read. Large multi-chunk reads use bounded parallel decoding and join all workers before returning. Entry and file metadata expose tagged_items as ordered tag/data records, retaining unknown extensions. apply preserves Windows tag-2 extended attributes (names, flags and value bytes) for supporting filesystem builders; it does not synthesize trust verdicts or apply unknown tags.",
	"archive.rar":                              "Opens RAR4 and RAR5 archives using bounded-memory native readers, including solid compression, PPMd and executable filters. Files are decoded on demand; complete reads verify checksums. Password-protected and split-volume members require capabilities not exposed by this API.",
	"archive.zstd":                             "Opens a Zstandard stream as a portable file with a 64 MiB decoder window limit. Frame headers provide decoded sizes when available; streams without content sizes require a counting pass. Reads and seeks replay decompression without extracting host files.",
	"filesystem.mds":                           "Reads Alcohol MDS v1 descriptor metadata, including sessions, track geometry, pregaps, TOC and companion filenames. Optional images maps declared names (including *.mdf) to portable files. Bound tracks expose raw sectors, logical data or audio, and interleaved P-W subchannels without conversion or copying. auto resolves MDF companions from its source tree. The sessions attribute contains tracks and their byte views.",
	"filesystem.raw_cd":                        "Views a single-track 2352-byte or 2448-byte raw CD as logical 2048-byte sectors. Supports ISO9660 and High Sierra in Mode 1 and Mode 2 Form 1, preserving the source without conversion or mounting.",
	"filesystem.duplicator":                    "Decodes version 1 The Duplicator images, including explicit cylinder filler. Cylinder checksums are not verified.",
	"filesystem.hdcopy":                        "Decodes standard and extended HD-Copy images in memory with strict RLE validation. Omitted tracks remain unreadable.",
	"filesystem.diskdupe":                      "Decodes DiskDupe DDI track maps into a read-only logical disk. Omitted tracks remain unreadable, never zero-filled.",
	"archive.omaha_payload":                    "Decodes an Omaha metainstaller LZMA/BCJ2 payload resource into a portable file, normally a tar archive. Bounds both expanded stages, validates all four stream extents and complete BCJ2 termination. Does not select PE resources, execute installer code, authenticate publishers or apply installation effects.",
	"archive.lzma":                             "Decodes one standalone LZMA stream into a bounded in-memory file. Checks header size, dictionary limit, decoded length, stream termination and trailing data. LZMA has no checksum; callers must authenticate the enclosing payload. This does not interpret the decoded archive.",
	"archive.xz":                               "Decodes an XZ stream into a file, rejecting decoder dictionaries above max_dictionary. This unwraps compression; it does not interpret an archive contained in the decoded bytes.",
	"archive.zip":                              "Parses a ZIP archive and exposes member files through files or entries. Members expose name/path, entry_type, compressed_size and compression_method. Complete reads validate decoded size and CRC; entry.verify() forces complete validation, including empty entries. Each member retains a bounded 8 MiB decoded window; backward reads outside it restart decompression. No host extraction occurs.",
	"filesystem.android_super":                 "Reads raw Android super-partition metadata versions 10.0–10.2 with geometry/header/table SHA256 validation and backup fallback. Exposes groups, physical devices and logical partition files composed from linear/zero extents. slot chooses a metadata slot; devices supplies additional physical files by name. Views borrow their inputs; sparse wrappers and EROFS traversal are separate formats.",
	"binary.android_boot":                      "Reads Android boot and vendor_boot v3/v4 headers, exposing bounded section files, command_line, version and page_size. Vendor v4 includes ramdisk fragments with type and board identifiers, plus a bootconfig section. Validates section/table bounds; does not decompress ramdisks or authenticate AVB signatures.",
	"binary.annotate":                          "Wraps a file with caller-supplied attributes while retaining its bytes. Use this to attach construction metadata without rebuilding the file's contents.",
	"binary.base64":                            "Encodes binary input as a Base64 string. url selects the URL-safe alphabet, padding controls trailing equals signs, and maximum bounds encoded output.",
	"binary.bits":                              "Creates a stateful bit reader over binary input. Its read, peek, drop and align operations consume or inspect bits in the selected bit order.",
	"binary.builder":                           "Creates a bounded mutable byte builder. Append encoded values, reserve or patch regions, then obtain bytes or a file; capacity is an allocation hint and limit bounds growth.",
	"binary.concat":                            "Returns a lazy file formed by concatenating binary parts in order. Unlike bytes_concat, it need not materialize the complete result at construction time.",
	"binary.cursor":                            "Creates a sequential byte reader at the selected offset. Scalar reads and bytes(size) advance its position; seek, skip and align change that position explicitly.",
	"binary.decode":                            "Decodes a hex, Base64 or unpadded URL-safe Base64 string into bytes. This is the inverse of binary.hex/base64, not a character-set decoder; use binary.text for text.",
	"binary.encode":                            "Encodes a string into bytes using the selected character encoding, including explicit IBM037/cp037. Unrepresentable characters fail. nul appends an encoding-appropriate terminator; binary.text performs the reverse conversion.",
	"binary.extents":                           "Constructs a file of the requested logical size from supplied byte extents. Unpopulated ranges read as zero, allowing sparse image layouts without allocating every logical byte.",
	"binary.hex":                               "Encodes binary input as lowercase hexadecimal text, with two characters per input byte. maximum bounds the encoded result; use top-level hex to format an integer.",
	"binary.layout":                            "Compiles a fixed-size binary layout from a format string and optional field names. The returned value encodes records or decodes a source at a byte offset.",
	"binary.replace":                           "Returns bytes with up to count non-overlapping occurrences of old replaced by new; count=-1 replaces all. old must be nonempty and maximum bounds the result.",
	"binary.strings":                           "Scans bounded binary input for printable ASCII, UTF-16LE or explicitly rendered IBM037 strings of at least minimum characters. IBM037 is a selected rendering, not saved-CCSID detection. Returns strings, not offsets or a decoded file.",
	"binary.text":                              "Decodes bounded binary input using the selected character encoding, including explicit IBM037/cp037 (not CCSID detection). nul requests terminator-aware decoding; maximum bounds input size.",
	"binary.snapshot_many":                     "Copies at most 65536 stable files into independent immutable in-memory snapshots in input order. maximum bounds their aggregate size, checked before reading; cache_bytes is one shared decoded-byte budget for the whole batch. Uses compressed 64KiB chunks with deduplication within the batch; returned files retain neither sources nor encoder. Encoded bytes and transient memory are outside the decoded cache budget. Sources must remain unchanged during copying. Preserves bytes, not authenticity; retain source and format validation.",
	"binary.snapshot":                          "Copies a stable source file into an independent immutable in-memory snapshot, using 64KiB compressed chunks (raw when compression would expand them). Reads the complete source sequentially within maximum; no host intermediates or source references remain. cache_bytes bounds the per-snapshot decoded read cache, not encoded storage or transient decoder memory. Source must not change during copying. This preserves bytes, not authenticity: retain source hash and format validation. Returned file supports ordinary random access, not a serialized archive format.",
	"binary.view":                              "Creates a bounded random-access byte view, optionally over a source slice. It supports byte search, comparisons and further slicing without requiring the entire source to be copied.",
	"binary.zlib":                              "Decodes one complete RFC 1950 zlib stream from bytes or a file. Returns bytes; verifies the checksum and rejects truncation, trailing data and output beyond maximum (0..512MiB). No host extraction or intermediate files.",
	"binary.xml_many":                          "Parses a list of at most 4096 XML inputs with 1..8 native workers (default 4), preserving input order and duplicates as independent immutable documents. XML options and per-document validation match binary.xml. Input/file conversion runs on the caller thread before parsing; workers receive owned byte snapshots and never call Starlark. total_maximum bounds aggregate input bytes (default and maximum 256 MiB). Input conversion errors precede parsing; otherwise the lowest failing input index determines the error after all workers join, with no partial result.",
	"binary.xml":                               "Parses XML into namespace-aware immutable document and node values. Size, depth and node-count limits bound parsing; with_root/with_children/with_text create edited values for serialization. Optional discard_whitespace removes whitespace-only XML character-data tokens (space, tab, CR, LF) unless inherited xml:space requests preservation; xml:space=default resets preservation and unknown values preserve conservatively. Other text, attributes, comments and processing instructions remain unchanged. Default parsing retains all whitespace. This option is for structural analysis, not lossless editing or signed serialization. Nodes expose attribute_map(namespace='', strict=False), an independent name-to-value dictionary for one exact attribute namespace. Namespace declarations are ignored, duplicates in the selected namespace are rejected, and strict=True rejects attributes from any other namespace. Default element namespaces do not apply to unprefixed attributes.",
	"block.cache":                              "Adds a bounded read-through chunk cache to a block device. It speeds repeated reads but is not a writable overlay or a persistent image cache.",
	"block.device":                             "Wraps a file as a block device with declared logical and physical block geometry. Writable mode requires a source that supports writes; it does not implicitly add copy-on-write storage.",
	"block.nbd":                                "Creates an NBD protocol server for a block device. serve(channel) processes requests over a supplied byte channel; constructing the server does not open a listening socket.",
	"block.overlay":                            "Creates a bounded writable copy-on-write layer over a base block device. Writes affect the overlay, leaving the base unchanged; max_dirty_bytes bounds retained changes.",
	"block.view":                               "Returns a read-only file view of a live block device. Later device changes remain visible through the view; it is not an immutable snapshot.",
	"block.temporary_overlay":                  "Creates disposable native sparse backing in the explicit host directory, with no changed-data quota or memory fallback. The immutable base remains unchanged. Create it before its VM; runtime cleanup closes users before removing the backing at script exit. Available host disk space bounds writes; backing I/O errors are terminal. It is not a snapshot or reusable disk image.",
	"bytes_concat":                             "Materializes a list of binary values into one bytes value, preserving order. Use binary.concat when a lazy file composition is preferable.",
	"clock.monotonic":                          "Returns seconds from the runtime's monotonic clock. Subtract readings to time work; the value is not a calendar timestamp.",
	"clock.read_profiler":                      "Measures completed reads from labeled file views with bounded per-file aggregates and slowest-call samples. Uses the runtime monotonic clock without executing callbacks on I/O threads.",
	"clock.profiler":                           "Creates a profiler for named spans, counters and measured calls. Its snapshots and reports summarize work explicitly recorded through this profiler.",
	"clock.unix":                               "Returns the runtime clock's current Unix timestamp in seconds. Use clock.monotonic rather than calendar time to measure durations.",
	"clock.utc":                                "Converts an integer Unix timestamp into a dictionary of UTC year, month, weekday, day, hour, minute, second and millisecond fields. Weekday uses Sunday=0; this converts a supplied time rather than reading the current clock.",
	"crypto.aes":                               "Encrypts or decrypts complete 16-byte AES blocks in CBC or ECB mode, returning bytes without adding or removing padding. CBC uses a zero IV when none is supplied; callers implementing a protocol must provide its required IV and authentication.",
	"crypto.checksum":                          "Returns Adler-32, a supported CRC-32, or sum16le for binary input. sum16le adds little-endian 16-bit words with end-around carry and no complement; an odd final byte is a low byte. Its optional initial accumulator must fit 16 bits. Continue using even-sized chunks; nonzero initial is rejected for other algorithms. These checksums are not cryptographic authentication.",
	"crypto.constant_time_equal":               "Compares two binary values using a constant-time comparison for equal-length inputs. Returns a boolean; differing lengths are not hidden.",
	"crypto.des":                               "Encrypts or decrypts complete 8-byte blocks with an 8-byte DES key. Supplying an IV selects CBC; otherwise blocks are processed independently. This legacy compatibility primitive adds no padding or authentication.",
	"crypto.deterministic":                     "Derives a reproducible byte sequence from a seed using domain-separated counter hashes. Equal seed, algorithm and size produce equal output; it is not an entropy source.",
	"crypto.hash":                              "Returns raw digest bytes for binary input using MD4, MD5, SHA-1, SHA-224, SHA-256, SHA-384 or SHA-512. Legacy algorithms are provided for format compatibility, not as recommendations for new security protocols.",
	"crypto.hash_blocks":                       "Advances a supplied SHA-1 or SHA-256 compression state over complete blocks and returns the updated state. It does not perform message padding or finalization; use crypto.hash for a complete digest.",
	"crypto.hasher":                            "Creates a mutable incremental hash state. update(value) feeds input, sum() reads the current digest without resetting, and reset() starts a new message.",
	"crypto.hmac":                              "Computes an HMAC over binary input using the supplied key and hash algorithm. Returns raw authentication bytes rather than hexadecimal text.",
	"crypto.mod_exp":                           "Computes base raised to exponent modulo modulus from byte-encoded nonnegative operands. byte_order applies to inputs and output; the result is padded to the modulus byte width.",
	"crypto.mod_inverse":                       "Returns the multiplicative inverse of an integer modulo a positive modulus, or None when no inverse exists. Operands are bounded to 16384 bits; this arithmetic operation is not a constant-time cryptographic protocol.",
	"crypto.mod_mul":                           "Multiplies byte-encoded nonnegative operands modulo a byte-encoded modulus. The selected byte order is preserved and the result has the modulus's byte width.",
	"crypto.random":                            "Returns the requested number of bytes from the runtime's cryptographic randomness source. Unlike crypto.deterministic, repeated calls are not reproducible.",
	"crypto.rc4":                               "Applies the RC4 stream cipher to binary input using a fresh state initialized from key. Encryption and decryption use the same operation; this unauthenticated legacy primitive is for compatibility.",
	"crypto.xtea":                              "Encrypts or decrypts complete 8-byte XTEA blocks with a 16-byte key and the selected word byte order. Returns bytes without padding or authentication.",
	"database.ese":                             "Parses an Extensible Storage Engine database into inspectable tables and records. It is a file-format reader, not a host ESE engine or SQL connection.",
	"database.ese_build":                       "Builds an ESE database in memory from declarative tables. Supports Windows 2000 revision 2 with page_size=8192 and Windows 8 revision 0x14 with page_size=32768; revision=0 selects the matching default. sort_data accepts base US-English sortkey.nls for Windows 2000 or SortDefault.nls for Windows 8. An empty indexes list creates a heap table. Revision 2 tagged columns accept lists of values; columns may preserve raw catalog defaults with default bytes.",
	"windows.ad_legacy_pek_list":               "Builds an encrypted revision-2 AD password encryption key list containing key zero. boot_key, key and salt are 16-byte values; generated is a Windows FILETIME. The caller supplies key and salt bytes.",
	"windows.ad_legacy_secret":                 "Wraps a nonempty secret with the Windows 2000 AD RC4-with-salt format using PEK zero. key and salt must each contain 16 bytes. Password hashes require RID-based DES protection before this outer layer.",
	"windows.ad_replication_metadata":          "Builds initial version-one attribute replication metadata in attribute-ID order. invocation_id uses Windows GUID byte order; changed uses the target directory generation's persisted timestamp. Local and originating USNs equal usn. Duplicate attribute IDs are rejected.",
	"windows.ad_stored_sid":                    "Converts a wire-format SID to AD's stored form by reversing only the final subauthority's byte order.",
	"windows.ad_ancestors":                     "Encodes a root-to-object path of 1..1024 nonzero, distinct directory name tags as little-endian DWORDs.",
	"windows.ad_legacy_dn_binary":              "Encodes a Windows 2000 non-linked DN-Binary value from a nonzero directory name tag and opaque binary payload.",
	"windows.winsock_catalog_item":             "Builds a Windows NT Winsock 2 Protocol_Catalog9 PackedCatalogItem from an ASCII guest DLL path, 628-byte WSAPROTOCOL_INFOW record, 16-byte Windows-order provider GUID, and nonzero catalog ID. Preserves the transport helper's protocol fields while assigning its provider and catalog identity.",
	"windows.guid_bytes":                       "Parses a textual GUID into the 16-byte mixed-endian representation used by Windows binary formats.",
	"windows.ad_replication_schedule":          "Builds a SCHEDULE_INTERVAL record from 168 hourly bytes, starting Sunday at 00:00 UTC. Each byte contains four quarter-hour availability bits; upper bits must be zero.",
	"windows.object_ace":                       "Builds an allow, deny, audit or alarm object ACE (types 5..8) from a wire SID, access mask, ACE flags and optional object/inherited-object GUID strings. The containing ACL must use revision 4.",
	"database.sqlite":                          "Parses a SQLite database, optionally applying a supplied WAL view, and exposes schema and table rows. It does not execute arbitrary SQL through a host SQLite process.",
	"database.sqlite_build":                    "Builds a SQLite database file from declarative schema and row data. The output can be embedded in an image without invoking a host database engine.",
	"debug.disassemble":                        "Decodes machine-code bytes into instruction records at a supplied base address. Supports the listed 16-, 32- and 64-bit x86 modes; count bounds returned instructions.",
	"debug.gdb":                                "Connects a GDB remote-protocol session over an existing byte channel. The returned session controls the target and reads structured stops, registers and memory without launching a host debugger.",
	"debug.select":                             "Waits until one of the supplied selectable values is ready, returning that value, or None on timeout. Read the event from the returned source separately; selection itself does not consume the event. For byte channels, readiness includes data, stream closure, or a spurious change notification (such as checkpoint restoration): retry the read to distinguish them. A closed debugger/event session still reports EOF.",
	"digest":                                   "Hashes a file, string or bytes value and returns raw digest bytes, not hexadecimal text. The default algorithm is SHA-256. Content-addressed immutable readers may reuse a SHA-256 digest only after complete byte verification; a cached identity is not a backing-store availability check.",
	"digest_many":                              "Hashes up to 65536 file, string or bytes inputs in input order using 1..8 parallel native readers (default 4). Reads and hashes each input (or reuses a previously byte-verified immutable SHA-256 identity), rejects short files and propagates read errors without returning partial results. Optional while_running is called once with no arguments on the caller interpreter thread while native readers run; its return value is ignored and an error aborts the batch only after joining every worker. Input membership is captured before the callback, but it must not mutate underlying bytes. No Starlark callbacks run in workers; source readers must support concurrent reads and remain unchanged until return.",
	"directory":                                "A mutable in-memory directory tree. write adds file content, mkdir creates directories, find retrieves an entry and remove deletes one. list_dir(path) returns sorted absolute paths of immediate files and directories (including empty directories); missing paths return an empty list. Paths are normalized but case-sensitive. Listings reflect mutations and are independent mutable lists. path_names(lower=False, include_empty=True) returns an independent dictionary of all file and directory paths (including root and empty directories), without reading file contents. Values retain exact spelling; lower=True lowercases lookup keys and rejects colliding file names. Directory spelling follows the first sorted descendant file; empty directories fill remaining names in sorted order. include_empty=False returns only files and their ancestors. path_index(lower=False, include_empty=True) provides the same namespace as an immutable native mapping with string keys, get, keys, values and items; enumeration is sorted and returned lists are independent. It avoids dictionary construction for lookup-only callers. Later namespace changes do not alter the snapshot. Attributes and security descriptors are stored as image-construction metadata; fat_short_path reports the FAT-compatible short-name path.\n\n`get_security(name)` returns the existing path's self-relative Windows security descriptor as immutable bytes, or `None` when no descriptor is assigned. Missing paths are errors. It does not synthesize inherited permissions or modify the tree.\n\n`set_security(name, descriptor)` updates the existing file or directory's security. For a hardlinked file, every alias receives the same descriptor; link-local metadata such as short names is preserved.\n\n`set_reparse(name, tag, data)` attaches an opaque Microsoft reparse payload to an existing file or directory for image construction. `tag` is an unsigned 32-bit Microsoft tag (bit 31 set; reserved low values 0 and 1 are rejected). `data` is immutable bytes, at most 16376 bytes, excluding the eight-byte REPARSE_DATA_BUFFER header. The payload is copied; existing metadata is preserved. No target is followed and no host filesystem object is created. Missing paths, non-Microsoft GUID buffers, and multiply-linked files are rejected. NTFS writes both the payload and its reparse index entry.",
	"emulator.processor":                       "Creates a portable in-process ARM64 or AMD64 instruction context independently of executable formats and OS APIs. map(address,size,data=None,access=rw) copies bounded bytes/files into owned sparse memory; read/write, get_register/set_register and plugin expose explicit experiment state. run(maximum_steps,stop_at=[]) stops before address breakpoints and returns reason, detail, steps and PC for breakpoint, halt, budget or fault. ARM64 uses the existing baseline ARMv8-A processor without optional PAuth. No code is implicitly loaded or executed. close() releases the context.",
	"emulator.machine":                         "Creates an in-process machine for the input's supported architecture, using native instruction execution and modeled Windows API callbacks. Instruction and memory limits bound execution; this is not a QEMU VM.",
	"emulator.plugin":                          "Wraps explicit mutable plugin state and its installation callback for emulator use. Keep checkpointed callback state here rather than hiding it in closures.",
	"emulator.uefi":                            "Loads an ARM64 EFI image into a configurable in-process interpreter. Runs stop at budgets, watches, explicit breakpoints, unsupported operations or validated ExitBootServices. CPU, RAM, firmware and disk overlays support checkpoints; digest-checked rewrites accelerate recognized routines. See the [UEFI inspection API](../../../emulator/uefi/README.md) for configuration and limitations. Native HVF can execute directly from the EFI entry point or continue suspended firmware state; see the [native UEFI API](../../../docs/uefi-native.md).",
	"emulator.x86":                             "Creates a bounded 32-bit x86 execution context from a PE image or raw code. Exposes guest registers, memory, hooks and snapshots; unsupported behavior returns structured stops rather than executing host code.",
	"error":                                    "Stops evaluation with the supplied error message. Use testing.attempt when a caller needs to inspect an expected failure.",
	"filesystem.fat":                           "Parses an existing FAT volume into a filesystem view. Use fat12, fat16 or fat32 with a directory to construct a new volume.",
	"filesystem.fat12":                         "Builds a FAT12 volume from a directory with the requested size, boot metadata and file order. The returned file is an in-memory image, not a mounted filesystem.",
	"filesystem.fat16":                         "Builds a FAT16 volume from a directory, preserving supported attributes and requested boot metadata. Geometry and hidden-sector fields must match the surrounding disk layout.",
	"filesystem.fat32":                         "Builds a FAT32 volume from a directory and requested layout options. It produces a volume file for composition into a disk rather than writing or formatting a host device.",
	"filesystem.gpt":                           "With a file, parses GPT partition metadata; with a size, creates a partition-table builder. Populate the builder with partition contents to produce a complete disk layout.",
	"filesystem.host":                          "Exposes a host directory through the native filesystem backend. This is a host-path boundary, not an image parser or a portable replacement for an in-memory directory.",
	"filesystem.iso9660":                       "Parses an ISO 9660 or High Sierra filesystem and exposes directory and lazy file views. High Sierra uses case-insensitive names and validates descriptor addresses, both-endian fields and extent bounds. Validated Rock Ridge takes precedence over Joliet, preserving case-sensitive names, POSIX metadata and symbolic-link targets without following links. Joliet and basic ISO names remain available when Rock Ridge is absent. It does not mount or copy the image.",
	"filesystem.mbr":                           "With a file, parses the MBR and partitions; with a disk size, creates a builder with optional boot code, signature and CHS geometry. Partition payloads remain caller-supplied.",
	"filesystem.ntfs":                          "Parses an NTFS volume when given a file, or builds one from a directory and size. Construction accepts explicit NTFS generation, boot metadata, log and upcase data so older NT layouts need not inherit modern defaults.\n\nRead-only mounts resolve extension attributes through the base record's\n`$ATTRIBUTE_LIST`, matching record sequences, stream names and starting VCNs.\nAn extension backlink alone does not make its data current. Conflicting\nreferenced extents remain errors; unreferenced stale records are not merged.\n\nRead-only `volume.metadata(path)` returns `path`, `directory`, `record_number`,\n`sequence`, `security_id` and `security_descriptor`. Use the record number with\n`filesystem.ntfs_record` to inspect the corresponding base FILE record. Metadata\nreads do not flush guest writes or make a paused live volume coherent.",
	"filesystem.udf":                           "Parses a UDF filesystem into directory and file views. Symbolic links expose decoded targets and metadata rather than encoded payloads; they are not followed. Reads resolve the filesystem's on-disk structures without mounting it.",
	"filesystem.vhdx":                          "Parses a VHDX container and exposes its logical disk contents. This unwraps the virtual-disk format; parse the returned disk's partitions/filesystems separately.",
	"firmware.acpi_compatible_id":              "Builds a complete SSDT file that assigns an ACPI compatible ID to the selected device. The output includes the ACPI header and checksum and can be supplied directly to qemu.acpi_table.",
	"firmware.acpi_fadt_arm64":                 "Builds a hardware-reduced ARM64 FADT with an explicit DSDT guest address and PSCI conduit declarations. HVC requires PSCI; the caller supplies the declared platform.",
	"firmware.acpi_gtdt_arm64":                 "Builds an ARM64 Generic Timer Description Table with distinct physical and virtual timer PPI interrupt IDs.",
	"firmware.acpi_mcfg":                       "Builds a PCI ECAM allocation table for the supplied base address, segment, and inclusive bus range.",
	"firmware.acpi_pci_root":                   "Builds AML for a PCI root with a fixed memory window and INTx routes. Routes are (device, pin, interrupt) tuples; pins use ACPI numbering 0 through 3.",
	"firmware.acpi_madt_arm64":                 "Builds a GICv3 MADT with enabled CPU interfaces for the supplied MPIDRs, a distributor, and an always-on redistributor range with one 128 KiB frame per CPU. Addresses and interrupt IDs are explicit platform inputs.",
	"firmware.acpi_rsdp":                       "Builds an ACPI 2.0+ root pointer with both checksums and an explicit guest XSDT address. Returns a file containing the serialized structure.",
	"firmware.acpi_table":                      "Wraps an ACPI table body with the requested signature, revision and OEM/creator identifiers, computing its checksum. Returns a table file for use by a VM backend.",
	"help":                                     "Prints runtime help for a value, or an overview of available globals when no value is supplied. Returns None and does not modify the inspected value.",
	"hex":                                      "Formats an integer as signed, 0x-prefixed hexadecimal text with optional digit padding. For file, string or bytes input, returns the raw bytes as unprefixed hexadecimal text; width applies only to integers.",
	"html.escape":                              "Escapes special characters in text for HTML output. This is text escaping, not sanitization of an existing HTML document.",
	"html.unescape":                            "Decodes HTML character references in a string. It does not parse or validate markup.",
	"image.compare":                            "Compares decoded images and returns difference measurements using the specified per-pixel threshold. Pixel differences alone are not proof that a guest application launched successfully.",
	"image.decode":                             "Decodes one bounded immutable image snapshot with width, height, pixels, format and pixel(x,y). Repeated snapshot pixel reads never reopen or redecode the source. Useful for glyph and framebuffer probes; encoded input and decoded pixel limits apply.",
	"image.info":                               "Decodes image metadata and returns dimensions and format information within input and pixel-count bounds.",
	"image.pixel":                              "Returns the color of one decoded image pixel at x,y. Coordinates must be inside the image; input size and decoded pixel limits apply.",
	"image.sample":                             "Decodes the source once per call and returns r/g/b/a color records in point order, using the same 8-bit alpha-premultiplied channels as image.pixel. points must be a list or tuple of (x,y) lists or tuples with integer coordinates inside the image; duplicate points are preserved. maximum bounds encoded bytes; max_pixels bounds both decoded image pixels and point count. An empty selection returns an empty list after validating the image. No decoded image is cached between calls.",
	"json.decode":                              "Parses bounded JSON input into Starlark dictionaries, lists and scalar values. Invalid JSON produces an error.",
	"json.encode":                              "Serializes a supported Starlark value as JSON text. Values that have no JSON representation fail rather than being stringified implicitly.",
	"mirror_file":                              "Opens a cached download or tries the supplied mirror URLs, checking the requested size and SHA-256 when supplied. The cache and key identify persistent native-backend storage; cache='' uses the native user cache directory's trex folder. An optional validate(file) callback runs synchronously before publication and on cache hits: it must return None to accept or raise an error to reject. The borrowed read-only file is usable only during the callback; do not retain it or derived views. Failed validation prevents publication; invalid cached objects are downloaded again. The result is a file, not extracted contents.",
	"exists":                                   "Reports whether a host path names an existing regular file. Like open, this is an explicit host-path boundary; recipes use it to choose between a saved explicit output (such as a machine checkpoint) and constructing it again.",
	"open":                                     "Opens a host-backed file for reading through the native storage backend. This is an explicit host-path boundary; portable format APIs consume the returned file.",
	"path.base":                                "Returns the last component of a logical path. It operates on path text and does not access the filesystem.",
	"path.clean":                               "Normalizes a logical path, resolving separators and dot components into the library's absolute-path form. It does not resolve symlinks or check host existence.",
	"path.dir":                                 "Returns the containing directory of a logical path. This is lexical path manipulation, not a filesystem lookup.",
	"path.ext":                                 "Returns the final filename extension, including its leading dot, or an empty string when none exists.",
	"path.from_windows":                        "Converts a Windows-style path into the library's logical slash-separated path representation. It does not translate it into a host path or access a drive.",
	"path.join":                                "Joins logical path components and normalizes the result. The operation is independent of the host operating system's path rules.",
	"qemu.acpi_table":                          "Creates a QEMU backend descriptor for an ACPI table file. The table is supplied through the backend when the VM starts.",
	"cc.kernel_symbol":                         "Read-only nearest original executable-section ELF symbol lookup for a stopped kernel address. Returns name, address, size and offset, or None when no preceding symbol exists. A nonzero offset is a nearest-symbol observation, not an exact call-site assertion. Input is bounded to 256 MiB and symbol/string sections to 32 MiB. Does not relocate, execute or patch bytes.",
	"cc.boot_bsd":                              "Directly boots an original amd64 OpenBSD, FreeBSD or NetBSD kernel in Linux/amd64 KVM with optional borrowed NVMe root backing. Stops at an explicit serial marker, timeout or failure and closes the VM. Returns success, serial, error and a read-only post-boot root snapshot (None without a root). The snapshot is available on failure too; FreeBSD timeout errors include bounded architectural observations. Output is capped at 1 MiB, kernel at 256 MiB, memory at 64..8192 MiB and timeout at 600 seconds. Console is at most 64 ordered (wait, send) string tuples; root writes use a bounded in-memory overlay, never alter the source. This is a bounded serial boot primitive, not a persistent desktop or managed-agent session.",
	"cc.backend":                               "Creates an in-process CrumbleCracker backend for Linux/amd64 KVM or native macOS ARM64 HVF. ARM64 uses explicit CPU/load state and an optional caller-composed vmm.arm64_platform. The native loop dispatches MMIO, optional polling/lifecycle hooks and platform HVC before PSCI; it has no board map or OEM service policy. Native protocol devices receive placement, backing and wiring from the platform factory. run_timeout bounds ARM64 execution in seconds (default 30). private_apple_isa=True selects HVF private ISA level 3 before VM creation and requires com.apple.private.hypervisor on a configured host; this host option is independent of board selection and nested EL2. ARM64 is headless and supports one CPU, named byte channels and snapshot-backed block resources. RAM must align to host pages; memory protection applies to both guest CPU access and emulated DMA. PC devices and Intel symbol/disassembly helpers below apply to x86 guests. The i386 and x86_64 platforms provide Go BIOS services, one ATA disk, VGA capture and PS/2 input. ACPI is automatic for x86_64 and optional for i386. uefi=True selects native Go UEFI for x86_64 GPT disks, enables PCI IDE, and provides a 1280x720 GOP framebuffer. Native UEFI PCs accept cpus=1..4 as one package without SMT; other PC profiles remain single-CPU. All-core pause/stop/capture barriers precede inspection and teardown. cc.v1.cpus() returns per-core register summaries; state/read_virtual use CPU0, and breakpoint/step require one CPU. RAM supports 16 MiB through 8 GiB for x86_64 BIOS/direct Darwin, 3 GiB for i386, or 16 GiB with uefi=True. usb=True on x86_64 enables a UHCI keyboard and absolute pointer with wheel; PS/2 keyboard fallback remains until USB enumeration. RAM beyond 3 GiB is mapped above 4 GiB, leaving the PCI MMIO window unmapped as RAM. hpet=True adds a 100 MHz HPET with three comparators and enables ACPI. pci_display=True exposes the 1234:cc02 TRF1 PCI framebuffer at bus 0 device 2, with an 8 MiB BAR at 0xe0000000; it requires a compatible guest display driver. pci_ide enables ACPI and a generic PCI IDE controller with PIO and bus-master DMA; ide_dma=False selects a PIO-only disk. ide_model='synthetic' preserves the generic compatibility controller; 'ich7-pata' requires PCI IDE and selects the 8086:27df profile with one primary disk, compatibility IRQ14 and native INTA routed to GSI16. The secondary channel has no disk. Timing registers are recorded; electrical cable timing is not simulated. The cc.v1 extension provides bounded read_virtual, original kernel symbol lookup and read-only disassemble observations for direct Darwin boot. overlay_limit bounds dirty snapshot memory in bytes (default 256 MiB). vmm.start creates the guest from portable memory and disk intent.",
	"qemu.audiodev":                            "Creates a QEMU audio-backend descriptor from its name and properties. It is configuration data; creating it does not start audio or launch QEMU.",
	"qemu.backend":                             "Creates a QEMU implementation of the portable VMM backend with selected machine, acceleration, devices and transport policies. A separate vmm.start call launches the guest.",
	"qemu.chardev":                             "Creates a QEMU character-device backend descriptor from a backend name and properties. Use it in qemu.backend configuration.",
	"qemu.device":                              "Creates a QEMU device descriptor from a device model and properties. This is backend-specific configuration, not an already-running device.",
	"qemu.extension":                           "Returns the QEMU-specific control extension for an existing VM, including QMP/HMP and block statistics. Use portable VM methods when backend-specific control is unnecessary.",
	"qemu.netdev":                              "Creates a QEMU network-backend descriptor with the supplied properties. It does not independently create a host network interface.",
	"qemu.option":                              "Creates a supported QEMU command-line option descriptor. The backend validates and translates it at launch; it is not a shell command.",
	"regexp.compile":                           "Compiles a regular expression into a reusable matching object. Invalid patterns fail at compilation rather than at the first match.",
	"renvo.cc":                                 "Compiles virtual C/C++ sources with the in-process Renvo toolchain for the selected target. Returns a compiledModule containing success/diagnostic information and virtual output bytes.",
	"renvo.go":                                 "Compiles a virtual Go source tree with Renvo for the selected target. Returns compilation status and outputs without invoking a host Go compiler.",
	"renvo.make":                               "Evaluates supported Renvo make recipes against a virtual source tree and rebuilds their outputs in memory. output selects the desired binary relative to the Makefile; no host make process is launched.",
	"launcher":                                 "Describes machines a web launcher page can start and stop. systems is a list of dicts with name (lowercase letters, digits, dashes), title, description, script, args and an optional root, the directory its \"//\" module labels resolve in. Each script's main(args) must return machine terminals (emulator machine web_terminals); a state=PATH argument lets Stop save and Start resume the machine, and tn3270=ADDRESS is shown. Returned from main under -serve, the launcher serves the page, each running system's consoles under /s/NAME/ and their tn3270 ports; Ctrl-C saves running systems that have a state file.",
	"repl":                                     "Enters the interactive Starlark prompt using the current runtime. Useful for retaining parsed inputs or a VM while running bounded experiments.",
	"runtime.set_memory_limit":                 "Explicitly sets the process-wide Go soft memory limit and returns the previous limit in bytes. The positive limit applies to Go-managed memory, not guest RAM mappings, other native allocations, or other processes. Set an appropriate budget at construction/boot phase boundaries; restore the returned value when done. This is not a reservation or hard RSS bound, cannot reclaim reachable data, and may cause severe GC overhead below the live working set. Affects concurrent work in the same process; use only when the caller owns process memory policy.",
	"runtime.release_memory":                   "Explicitly runs a process-wide Go garbage collection and requests that unused heap pages be returned to the operating system. Drop unneeded construction references before calling it at a phase boundary. Reachable readers, caches and guest resources are not closed or evicted. Does not change GC policy or the memory limit and does not guarantee an RSS reduction or a hard memory bound. May pause concurrent work; include its elapsed time in end-to-end measurements.",
	"runtime.heap_profile":                     "Returns a bounded read-only snapshot of the running Go allocation sampler, not a per-recipe ownership graph. limit defaults to 20 and must be 1..100. Returns sample_rate_bytes, total sampled_bytes/sampled_objects, stack_count, and entries ordered by retained sampled bytes. Each entry has sampled_bytes, sampled_objects and stack frames (function, line); paths and addresses are omitted. Raw counts are unscaled samples. Additional estimated_bytes/estimated_objects fields on entries and totals apply the inverse sampling probability from mean sampled object size; these are statistical estimates, not exact ownership measurements. order_by accepts sampled_bytes (default) or estimated_bytes. Counts and estimates may lag up to two GC cycles. Does not force collection or change sampling, GC policy, memory limits or guest timing. Bounds stack inventory to 1048576 and retries a changing inventory at most three times; failure returns no partial snapshot.",
	"runtime.host_memory":                      "Read-only native host diagnostic snapshot: platform, pid, files and errors. On Linux reads fixed procfs status/stat/meminfo/vmstat/cgroup paths for the positive 32-bit pid (default this process) and memory counters at the mounted /sys/fs/cgroup root and up to 16 process-membership ancestors, bounded to 64 KiB per file; each failure is explicit. Raw texts permit bounded policy-side inspection without host tools. The mounted cgroup root need not be the process leaf; consult membership before interpreting scope. Samples are not atomic, PIDs may be reused, counters are cumulative, and an OOM increment alone does not identify its victim. Other platforms return empty files/errors. Does not change limits, collect garbage, reserve memory or launch processes.",
	"runtime.stats":                            "Returns a snapshot of runtime resource statistics for diagnostics and benchmarking. Counters describe the running runtime, not just one image recipe. Includes heap allocation/goal, in-use/idle/released heap pages, stack and runtime metadata reservations, the configured soft memory limit, cumulative GC cycles and stop-the-world pause nanoseconds, and cumulative GC/runtime available CPU seconds. CPU seconds are runtime-wide capacity-accounting counters, not elapsed time or per-recipe work; compare snapshots rather than interpreting totals as a critical path. Observes configuration only; does not change GC policy, memory limits or guest clocks.",
	"stdout":                                   "Writes the supplied value to the runtime's standard output and returns None. It does not create a named file.",
	"testing.attempt":                          "Calls a function with supplied positional and keyword arguments and captures success or failure in an ok/value/error record. It does not roll back side effects performed before an error.",
	"testing.module":                           "Loads a module label and returns its globals as a dictionary. Used by the portable test runner to discover tests through the normal module loader.",
	"url.path_escape":                          "Percent-encodes text for use as one URL path segment. It is not query-string encoding or whole-URL normalization.",
	"url.path_unescape":                        "Decodes percent escapes in URL path text. Malformed escape sequences produce an error.",
	"vmm.backends":                             "Lists the VMM backends registered in this runtime and their advertised capabilities. Availability depends on the runtime's backend implementations.",
	"vmm.channel":                              "Describes a named VM byte channel of the requested kind. required determines whether lack of backend support is a validation failure.",
	"vmm.disk":                                 "Describes a guest disk or optical medium backed by a file or block device, including bus, unit and write/snapshot policy. It does not boot or materialize the source.",
	"vmm.display":                              "Describes the requested guest display mode and whether support is mandatory. This is part of a portable machine specification.",
	"vmm.darwin_boot":                          "Creates an unslid amd64 XNU v2 direct handoff from an immutable original kernelcache or Mach-O executable. Supports amd64 fat slices and Apple comp/lzss caches with checksum validation. Native cc requires Linux/amd64 KVM, x86_64 and no UEFI image execution; QEMU rejects this intent. No guest bootloader is executed or kernel instructions patched. RAM disks are not supported. Optional smc_osk must be empty or exactly 64 bytes; it is explicit caller-supplied SMC material, never bundled or logged. Native cc exposes SMC APP0001/IRQ6 and omits OSK0/OSK1 when no material is supplied. Verified scope is Lion HFS-root boot to launchd and a visible single-user shell, not keyboard input, a desktop or a complete installation.",
	"vmm.linux_boot":                           "Creates portable direct Linux boot intent from borrowed immutable kernel and optional initramfs files plus a command line. Requires backend boot.linux capability; QEMU supports Linux hosts and x86 guests using anonymous inherited descriptors.",
	"vmm.machine":                              "Creates a portable machine specification from architecture, memory, CPUs, storage, networking and channels. It does not start a VM.",
	"vmm.network":                              "Describes a named guest network connection and whether its requested kind is required. The selected backend implements the transport.",
	"vmm.switch":                               "Creates an isolated in-memory Ethernet switch. internet=True explicitly requests a single-guest IPv4 TCP/DNS gateway and requires trex -allow-internet; mac defaults to 02:54:52:00:00:01 and must match the guest. Private/LAN destinations, IPv6 and general UDP are not forwarded. Attach guests with vmm.network('ethernet', switch=lan, mac='02:00:00:00:00:01'), using a distinct unicast MAC for each NIC. Ports have bounded receive queues and disconnect when their VM closes.",
	"vmm.workspace":                            "Groups a name-to-VM dictionary for the browser frontend; it may start empty. add(name, vm) registers a display and returns a never-reused ID; remove(id) unregisters without closing the VM. The optional zero-argument create callback returns (name, vm); calls are serialized and successful creations appear as new tabs. Switching tabs leaves every VM running. Guest construction and provisioning remain recipe-owned.",
	"vmm.start":                                "Validates a machine specification against a backend and starts a VM. Returns the live VM handle used for input, screenshots, events and lifecycle control.",
	"vmm.validate":                             "Checks whether a backend can implement a machine specification and its required capabilities without launching it. Use this before committing to a boot experiment.",
	"web.file":                                 "Builds an HTTP response that serves a file, optionally with a download name, status and headers. Constructing the response does not start a web server.",
	"web.redirect":                             "Builds an HTTP redirect response with a Location header and selected redirect status. It does not follow the redirect.",
	"web.response":                             "Builds an HTTP response from body, status and headers for the web runtime to send.",
	"web.zip":                                  "Builds a downloadable ZIP response for a directory in a supplied virtual filesystem. Packaging is handled in-process rather than by a host archiver.",
	"windows.appx_package_acl":                 "Constructs identity-scoped WindowsApps package DACLs from a native parent DACL. family is a package family name; kind is root, directory or file. By default adds conditional Users execute access bound to WIN://SYSAPPID, the derived family capability SID and Users read access. framework=True instead adds native shared-framework read/execute grants for both AppContainer groups and Users. Both policies inherit parent grants. Unsupported inheritable ACE types/flags and missing inherited grants fail closed. Does not construct an owner/SACL or register packages. No host security APIs.",
	"windows.map_file_security_rights":         "Maps generic rights in a revision-1 self-relative security descriptor to file-specific masks, preserving all other bytes, ACL states and ordering. Accepts bytes or a file up to 1 MiB. Supports simple allow/deny/audit/alarm and callback-allow ACE layouts; unknown layouts and malformed or overlapping components fail closed. This does not default or inherit security, clear propagation flags, apply filesystem changes, or decide access.",
	"windows.copy_explicit_file_acl":           "Constructs effective and propagation ACEs from an explicit file ACL under auto-inheritance. Source is a bounded ACL; owner and group are binary SIDs. Maps and clips file rights, substitutes creator owner/group, preserves callback data, and splits directory propagation when needed. Supports allow/deny/audit/alarm and callback-allow layouts; inherited ACEs, other creator identities, reserved flags and unsupported layouts fail closed. Does not select or merge parent ACLs, supply descriptor defaults, represent absent/NULL ACLs, or change a filesystem.",
	"windows.inherit_file_security":            "Constructs a bounded self-relative file descriptor with explicit owner/group and auto-inherited ACLs. Selects protected versus unprotected explicit entries, drops old unprotected ID entries, and appends parent inheritance. Preserves absent versus empty ACLs. Matches the token-free RTL flags-0x101b construction path, not access validation or WCP defaulting/final control policy. Source owner/group are required; child NULL ACLs, defaulted controls, unsupported controls and non-revision-2 ACLs fail closed. Parent NULL ACLs contribute no entries and do not create a child NULL ACL. Parent defaults to absent.",
	"windows.event_template_providers":         "Reads a CRIM 5.1 WEVT_TEMPLATE resource (bytes/file, at most 16 MiB) and returns canonical lowercase braced provider GUIDs in directory order. Validates bounded directory counts, GUID uniqueness, WEVT block extents/non-overlap and up to three trailing zero bytes. Event schemas, message/channel metadata, resource trust and registration policy are not interpreted.",
	"windows.assign_registry_security":         "Constructs the bounded registry setter result from current/request descriptor bytes and explicit security_information. Requires owner, group, DACL and audit SACL with matching protection flags; optional LABEL replaces mandatory labels, otherwise current labels survive. Maps effective key rights (kernel execute 0x20039), preserves inherit-only generics and duplicate ACEs, consumes AR, and emits a present-NULL empty SACL. Unsupported controls, ACE categories and multiple labels fail closed. Does not create keys, inherit from parents, authorize the subject, propagate to descendants or write hives.",
	"windows.inherit_registry_security":        "Constructs bounded registry-key security with explicit owner/group and container auto-inheritance. Uses key generic rights (read/execute 0x20019, write 0x20006, all 0xf003f), retains generic propagation ACEs, substitutes creator principals, and selects protected versus inherited entries. Shares the file-construction extent and syntax checks; child NULL ACLs, missing principals and unsupported layouts fail closed. Does not select WCP defaults, adjust final request controls, authorize ownership, or assign security to a hive. Parent defaults to absent.",
	"windows.inherit_file_acl":                 "Derives auto-inherited ACEs from a bounded parent file ACL and binary owner/group SIDs. Honors OI/CI/NP, adds ID, substitutes creator principals, and prunes ordinary ACEs covered by earlier inherited masks in each effective/object/container domain; callback ACEs are not deduplicated. Supports the layouts of copy_explicit_file_acl plus one mandatory-label ACE with an integrity SID and mask 0..7, preserving even zero policy masks. This does not authorize explicit label assignment or select token-default labels; whole-descriptor inheritance still rejects labels. An empty result means no inherited ACEs, not an instruction to install an empty ACL. Does not select defaults, apply protection, merge explicit ACLs, or construct a security descriptor.",
	"windows.compile_sddl":                     "Compiles a bounded SDDL subset to revision-1 self-relative descriptor bytes, without host identity lookup, defaulting, generic-right mapping or inheritance. Supports O/G/D/S sections; fixed SY, BA, BU, WD, AC, LU, AU, BO, CO, IU, LS, LW, ME, NO, NS, RC, SU, UD, AN, BG, CG, OW, SO and numeric revision-1 SIDs; P/AR/AI ACL flags; absent, empty and NO_ACCESS_CONTROL ACLs; A/D/AU ACEs, SACL mandatory-label ACEs with integrity SIDs and masks 0..7 (including NW/NR/NX policy tokens), and XA local-attribute Contains-string, exists, or parenthesized local-attribute NOT conditions. Optional account_domain_sid must be a canonical S-1-5-21-a-b-c SID and enables LA (RID 500); no account domain is guessed. Symbolic and base-zero numeric rights may be concatenated and are ORed; numeric overflow is rejected. Unsupported syntax (including CSI (inherit), other domain-relative aliases, object GUIDs, compound conditions and escaped literals) fails closed. This is format construction, not an access decision or a complete file-security policy.",
	"windows.security_descriptor_parts":        "Validates a self-relative Windows security descriptor (at most 1 MiB), including aligned, non-overlapping component extents and ACL entries. Returns `control`, `resource_manager_control`, and binary `owner`, `group`, `sacl`, `dacl` components. Missing components are `None`; ACL presence bits distinguish absent from NULL. Exact same-kind aliases are allowed and unknown ACE bodies remain opaque. This is format inspection, not access checking, inheritance, default selection or a security equivalence predicate.",
	"windows.acl_entries":                      "Validates a complete revision-2/4 Windows ACL (at most 65,535 bytes), returning `revision` and `entries`. Every entry contains `type`, `flags` and its original `data` bytes. Simple allow, deny, audit, alarm, mandatory-label and callback-allow entries also contain a checked binary `sid` and numeric `mask`. Callback application bytes remain in `data` after the SID; other entry layouts retain opaque bytes with `sid` and `mask` set to `None`. Unused ACL allocation space is allowed. This inspects the format; it neither evaluates access nor resolves principals through the host.",
	"windows.image_digest":                     "Computes the CBS HashTransforms.ImageDigest hash, distinct from Authenticode/catalog hashing. Normalizes PE resource/debug layout and hashes the DOS prefix, fixed NT header, rewritten section table and section payloads in table order, excluding resource-directory bytes and .debug payloads. Non-images are hashed verbatim; recognizable malformed or unsupported images fail closed. maximum_bytes (1..512MiB) bounds input, and the transformed stream is capped at 512MiB. No signature or catalog trust is implied.",
	"windows.csi_file_attributes":              "Parses the native manifest file attribute mask. None retains the 0xa0 constructor default; an explicit value replaces it, including an empty string yielding zero. Accepts case-insensitive readOnly, hidden, system, archive, temporary, offline and notContentIndexed separated by XML whitespace. Rejects unsupported tokens. This is not the final commit-time filesystem mask.",
	"windows.csi_file_directory_macro":         "Returns the WCP IRtlUserProfile directory identifier for an unwrapped runtime macro name. Only the observed amd64 target policy is supported. MSIL/data/absent component architecture use native directories; x86/wow64/amd64.x86 use redirected directories. ASCII macro names are case-insensitive. Unknown macros and unsupported architectures fail closed. The identifier is not a path: image registry/profile lookup, environment expansion and offline drive mapping remain caller responsibilities. This read-only lookup does not authorize or apply file effects.",
	"windows.csi_file_destination_name":        "Selects the shallow CSI destination name: an explicitly supplied destination_name (including an empty string) overrides name; otherwise selects the suffix after the last backslash, or the preceding path when the last backslash is trailing. Forward slashes are not separators here. None means no override. Does not expand macros, validate a final path or authorize a projection.",
	"windows.csi_mof_equivalent":               "Compares ordered CSI MOF declarations and referenced primary-file hashes using the observed WCP MofDiffer predicate. Inputs are XML file/string/bytes, at most 16 MiB each. Supports ASCII file lookup, SHA1/SHA256 nested hashes with zero or one Identity transform and legacy SHA1; unsupported policy and ambiguous file names fail closed. Declaration strings are case-sensitive, file lookup is case-insensitive, and either comparable hash mismatch vetoes equality. The native uninstallmof branch compares its declaration string but rechecks the primary file hash. True is only predicate equivalence: it does not derive installer queue/inspection policy, execute MOF registration, verify payload authenticity or apply state.",
	"windows.assembly_manifest":                "Parses an assembly manifest XML value into identity attribute records and declared file records, including hashes and hash algorithms. Each file also has digest=None or the declared nested XMLDSIG record (algorithm, transforms, value); unsupported algorithms/transforms are retained for inspection, not treated as verified. The registry_keys list retains every declared key path, including empty CBS index keys, independently of values. References also retain dependency_type, resource_type, qualified attributes and binding_redirects. deployment is the root deployment attribute list, or None when absent; impact is preserved without inferring installation. top_level and file_elements are deduplicated local element names for conservative presence filtering (assembly children and direct file children respectively); namespace and attribute validation still belongs to the consumer. It does not install a side-by-side assembly or verify those hashes. Returns a lazy assembly_manifest view over an owned parse. Each call owns its projected mutable lists; fields materialize on first access. Freezing the view also freezes fields materialized later.",
	"windows.catalog_hash":                     "Computes the Windows catalog membership hash of a file. For PE inputs this uses the format's Authenticode hashing rules rather than a plain whole-file digest.",
	"windows.catalog_members":                  "Reads a PKCS#7 catalog and returns its member digest byte strings. Extracting membership does not verify the catalog signature or establish certificate trust.",
	"windows.certificate":                      "Parses one DER X.509 certificate and returns its DER bytes, subject, issuer, SHA-1 and SHA-256 fingerprints and a subject/issuer-equality flag named self_signed. Also exposes exact subject_public_key_info DER, public_key_algorithm OID, public_key_parameters DER, public_key_bits and public_key_bit_length without re-encoding the signed key material. These fields and self_signed do not establish signature verification or trust.",
	"windows.signing_identity":                 "Loads a DER or PEM X.509 certificate and matching unencrypted PKCS#1/PKCS#8 RSA private key for in-memory PE signing. Optional chain certificates are embedded without establishing trust. The identity exposes only its public certificate; private key material is not printable or exportable.",
	"windows.test_signing_identity":            "Generates an ephemeral RSA-2048 key and self-signed code-signing certificate. not_before and not_after are explicit Unix seconds supplied by the caller. Returns an opaque identity with a public certificate attribute; does not install trust or change driver-signing policy.",
	"windows.pe_sign":                          "Signs a PE32 or PE32+ file with RSA/SHA-256 Authenticode entirely in memory and returns new bytes with an aligned WIN_CERTIFICATE and updated checksum. Preserves sections and overlay data. Existing certificate tables require replace=True. Optional page_hashes=True embeds SHA-256 page hashes. No timestamp, nested signatures, or trust-store changes are generated. See docs/pe-signing.md.",
	"windows.clone_file_entries":               "Copies a path-to-metadata dictionary and each distinct entry dictionary, preserving insertion order and alias groups: names sharing one entry share one new entry in the clone. The cloned dictionaries are independently mutable from the source; equal-valued but distinct entries remain distinct. File sources and other field values remain shared and are not read.",
	"windows.creg_compare":                     "Compares two Windows 9x CREG registry files structurally and returns a difference report. This avoids treating different physical record layouts as necessarily different registry contents.",
	"windows.creg_from_patches":                "Constructs a Windows 9x CREG registry file from a name, value patches and optional key records. generation selects windows95, windows95_rtm, windows_nashville, windows98_fe, windows98 (SE), or windowsme, including their navigation page and allocator layouts; this is not an NT REGF hive writer.",
	"windows.creg_keys":                        "Returns the key paths represented by a Windows 9x CREG registry file as lists of path components. Empty keys can therefore be preserved independently of value patches.",
	"windows.creg_patches":                     "Converts values in a Windows 9x CREG registry file into declarative registry patch dictionaries. Use creg_keys as well when reconstructing empty keys.",
	"windows.csp_registrations":                "Derives CryptoAPI provider-registration arguments from a 32-bit provider DLL without assigning registry paths or defaults. strict=False returns an empty list for unrecognized registration code; malformed input still fails. The provider is not executed.",
	"windows.empty_event_log":                  "Constructs a valid empty legacy Windows event-log file of the requested size. This creates an EVT-format starting state, not an EVTX log.",
	"windows.event_log":                        "Parses legacy EVT or supported EVTX event-log input into event records. The format is detected from the file; this does not query the host's event-log service.",
	"windows.font_names":                       "Extracts full font-name strings from supported OpenType/TrueType files, including collections. Returns a list of names without installing or rendering the font.",
	"windows.bitmap_info":                      "Parses the first 40 bytes of an uncompressed 24/32-bit RGB Windows BITMAPINFOHEADER. Returns width, absolute height, depth, DWORD-aligned stride, pixel size, and top_down. Does not allocate pixels or access a display.",
	"windows.hive":                             "Parses an NT REGF registry hive and returns a read-only key/value inspection object. Keys expose security as the bounded raw self-relative descriptor from their SK record, without walking descendants or inferring permissions. path_component_patches(replacements, exclude_keys=[]) returns planned key_parts/name/type/value/before records for REG_SZ, REG_EXPAND_SZ and REG_MULTI_SZ values. Matches complete backslash/NUL-delimited components case-insensitively, preserving all unmatched raw bytes. Excluded key subtrees use literal key parts. Traversal is bounded to 1,048,576 keys, 4,194,304 values, 1 GiB of string data and 65,536 output patches; errors return no partial plan. This never edits the hive. Use hive_from_patches to construct a hive and patch_hive to produce an edited copy.\n\nLarge registry values in hive format 1.4 and later use native `db` descriptors\nand segment lists above 16,344 bytes. Every segment, including the final one,\nhas at least 16,344 bytes of capacity; unused capacity and alignment padding\nare excluded from decoded values. Complete hive construction and mutable patches preserve\nthis representation; older hive formats retain direct large-value cells.\nMalformed segment counts and truncated segments are rejected. snapshot=True captures at most 512 MiB in an immutable owned reader and lazily indexes decoded keys and case-insensitive children. Native hive_from_patches and patch_hive outputs already own immutable versions: their bytes, lazy indexes and verified SHA-256 are shared by subsequent hive views, without another copy. Patching creates a distinct version. Borrowed inputs are copied for snapshots; default False keeps their live-read behavior and never caches mutable-source metadata.",
	"windows.hive_from_patches":                "Builds an NT registry hive from declarative value patches and optional key records. With source, imports its raw value types/data, empty keys, security, class and flags natively before applying keys and patches; the source format is inherited unless format overrides it. Optional source_drive/target_drive (both ASCII drive letters with colons) relocate upper/lower drive spellings in key/value names and raw UTF-16 REG_SZ/REG_EXPAND_SZ/REG_MULTI_SZ data only. exclude_keys removes case-insensitive subtrees from the relocated source: legacy strings accept slash/backslash separators, while key/key_parts dictionaries support lossless literal components. exclude_values contains exact key/key_parts and name dictionaries. Component lists never split literal separators; matching occurs after relocation. Additions are applied after exclusions and are never relocated or filtered. Source options require source. Without source, format controls the generation and metadata is caller-supplied.",
	"windows.hive_keys":                        "Enumerates an NT hive's key paths, optionally including key metadata. This preserves keys that have no values when exporting a hive for reconstruction.",
	"windows.hive_log":                         "Constructs the transaction-log companion for the supplied NT hive. The result is a new file; it does not replay a host registry log.",
	"windows.hive_patches":                     "Exports an NT hive's values as declarative patches. raw requests raw value representations, useful when preserving types or bytes that should not be interpreted as text.",
	"windows.hives_from_inf":                   "Builds registry hives from setup INF data with optional TXTSETUP context, extra inputs and patches. format selects the hive generation; the function does not boot Windows or execute setup.",
	"windows.inf":                              "Parses a Windows INF file into section and entry data, with helpers for install sections and registry patches. directives preserves ordered (section, key, fields) logical rows before string expansion or duplicate grouping, including repeated directives; section markers have key/fields None. json remains the expanded section projection. This is declarative inspection, not execution of every installer directive.",
	"windows.inf_patches":                      "Converts a selected INF registry section into patches for the named hive. Returns modifications for later construction rather than changing an existing hive.",
	"windows.internet_shortcut":                "Builds InternetShortcut (.url) bytes for a URL with optional icon location and index. It creates the shortcut contents but does not fetch or open the URL.",
	"windows.kd":                               "Creates a Windows kernel-debugging protocol session over an existing byte channel. The session exposes packets, events, context and memory operations, bounded by the configured protocol limits.",
	"windows.minidump":                         "Parses a Windows minidump into inspectable dump streams and associated metadata. It operates on the supplied file and does not attach to a live process.",
	"windows.module_sources":                   "Indexes a path-to-source dictionary by normalized DLL basename without reading sources. Matching is case-insensitive, both slash styles are accepted, extensionless basenames gain .dll, and the first non-excluded match wins.",
	"windows.mof":                              "Parses Managed Object Format source into a structured document containing its declarations. Pass documents to wmi_repository to construct repository files; parsing alone does not register classes with a running service.",
	"windows.msc_snapins":                      "Extracts snap-in identifiers referenced by an MMC console file. Use the identifiers to select registration metadata; the function does not launch MMC.",
	"windows.ne_fastboot":                      "Constructs the Windows NE fast-boot binary and overlay from supplied modules, returning bin and overlay files. overlay_path is the guest-visible location to encode, not a host output path.",
	"windows.patch_hive":                       "Applies declarative registry patches to an NT hive and returns a new file, optionally replacing the root name. `keys` lists registry paths to create without adding values; existing keys and their values remain intact. This preserves the source hive's layout, including legacy REGF 1.1 cells. The input hive is not edited in place.\n\nEach value patch has\n`key` (normalized path) or `key_parts` (literal component list), plus `name`, `type`, and `value`. Literal components preserve slash and dot names and take precedence over `key`. `type` accepts a registry type name or a raw non-negative 32-bit integer for formats such as SAM indexes that encode counts in the type field. Optional `keys` contains registry key paths\nto create, including missing parents; existing keys are preserved. Empty keys\nremain truly empty, so registry indexes represented by child-key names do not\nneed placeholder values. New keys inherit their parent's security descriptor. keys also accepts key/key_parts dictionaries with optional raw self-relative security bytes; other key metadata is rejected. delete_keys removes strict existing subtrees; delete_values removes strict existing key/key_parts/name values. Alternatively, exclude_keys and exclude_values resolve a single source-epoch union before edits: duplicate and absent exclusions are harmless, ancestor removals subsume descendants, and recreated keys retain no excluded contents. Exclusions cannot be combined with ordered delete_keys/delete_values; excluding the root is rejected. Exclusion paths are bounded to 512 levels and 1,048,576 distinct trie nodes. Empty value names and (default) identify the default value. Deletions precede key creation and value patches. Optional key_flags records contain key/key_parts and user_flags and/or virtualization_flags (integers 0..15), applied last to strictly existing keys. Each replaces only its persistent nibble; virtualization_flags bit 2 also clears NK KEY_VIRTUAL_STORE, as native class-2 SetInformationKey does. No live cache or timestamp is synthesized. Child-index updates and native source imports preserve packed metadata. Hive keys expose raw NK flags, user_flags and virtualization_flags separately. Missing deletions, deleting the root, cyclic metadata or invalid security fail without changing the input. Each edit list is bounded to 1,048,576 entries. Deletion traversals are bounded to 512 levels and 1,048,576 keys. Updates maintain child indexes, segmented value cells and shared SK references; unrelated class/flags and value bytes are retained.",
	"windows.patch_ini":                        "Merges literal section/key string settings into a Windows INI file, matching section and key names case-insensitively. Preserves unrelated lines, commas, and quotes; returns a new file. source=None creates a new INI file.",
	"windows.registry31":                       "Constructs a Windows 3.1 SHCC3.10 registry file from class-path string entries and optional REGEDIT text source files. Explicit entries override sources; keys match case-insensitively. Uses Windows-1252 strings and rejects output exceeding 64 KiB. Does not launch REGEDIT or modify a live registry.",
	"windows.pdb":                              "Parses a PDB symbol file with bounded stream sizes. Exposes identity and symbols, including lookup of the nearest symbol to an RVA; symbol parsing does not download files automatically.\n\n`guid` and `age` describe the PDB Info stream;\n`dbi_age` is the independently stored symbol-record age in the DBI stream.\nPost-link processing can advance `age` without advancing `dbi_age`.",
	"windows.pe":                               "Creates a lazy inspection object for a PE32 or PE32+ file. Metadata and data share an owned immutable snapshot; read uses RVAs, while patch returns a separate modified file. The explicit resource_files() method instead indexes the ORIGINAL input without copying payload bodies, returning frozen rows with type, name, lang, size and a read-only file view. Unlike resources/data it borrows the input, which must stay stable for all returned file lifetimes; it does not use a previously materialized snapshot or verify hashes/trust. Resource metadata is bounded to 65536 visited entries and 16 nested directories; cyclic directories and out-of-file or out-of-section ranges fail.",
	"windows.pe32_executable":                  "Links a labeled section, fixups and optional imports into a minimal PE32 executable. The caller supplies instruction bytes and policy; the builder lays out headers, RVAs and imports and checks fixup bounds.",
	"windows.pe32_link":                        "Links an in-memory ELF32/i386 cdecl object into a PE32 DLL, executable, or native driver. executable=True requires an entry point; subsystem=2 selects a GUI executable, subsystem=3 a console executable, and subsystem=1 a native driver. native_dll=True marks a subsystem=1 module as a DLL. Imports map DLLs to symbol/argument-word dictionaries; exports and callbacks map symbols to stack-word counts. import_abi optionally selects fastcall instead of stdcall for named imports. indirect_calls maps synthetic symbols to stdcall argument counts: the cdecl caller supplies a function pointer followed by those arguments. Supports R_386_32 and R_386_PC32 relocations; rejects unresolved symbols and unsupported relocations. No OS runtime is linked.",
	"windows.pkcs7_certificates":               "Extracts distinct embedded DER certificates from PKCS#7/CMS input, including legacy catalogs. Returns certificate records; extraction is deliberately separate from signature or trust verification.",
	"windows.reactos_record":                   "Encodes a named ReactOS binary-record kind from declarative fields. Layout and field validation stay in Go while values and operating-system policy remain with the caller.",
	"windows.registration_expand":              "Expands percent-delimited registration variables in replacement-dictionary order, uppercase token then lowercase token, for at most four passes. Unknown tokens remain unchanged and non-string inputs pass through; this is not general case-insensitive environment expansion.",
	"windows.registry_children":                "Selects direct children from a self-registration registry-state dictionary. values=True selects direct values; otherwise it derives immediate subkeys, without changing the input or recursively returning descendants.",
	"windows.registry_partition":               "Splits a self-registration registry-state dictionary into selected and retained dictionaries for a hive subtree. values selects the value-identity layout; ordering and payload identity are preserved, and neither result aliases the input dictionary.",
	"windows.selfreg_patches":                  "Derives registry patches from supported self-registration resources in a PE file or windows.pe object, using module for path substitutions. It does not emulate DllRegisterServer; use the self-registration policy/runner for runtime effects.",
	"windows.setver":                           "Returns a SETVER driver image with the named executable's reported DOS version added or updated. The original source remains unchanged and maximum bounds the processed image.",
	"windows.shortcut":                         "Builds Windows Shell Link (.lnk) bytes for a target with optional arguments, working directory, description and icon metadata. It serializes a shortcut; it does not resolve or launch its target on the host.",
	"windows.symbol_server":                    "Retrieves a bounded symbol file from a symbol-server layout using its name and identity key, optionally validating against image debug metadata. The GUID must match exactly. Following Microsoft's `PDB1::OpenValidate4`, the Info age must be at least the requested image age, and DBI age must equal the image age (legacy DBI age zero is accepted). A newer Info age alone does not permit a different set of symbol records. Limits and timeout are validated.",
	"windows.utf16_strings":                    "Scans a file for printable UTF-16LE strings meeting the minimum length. Returns extracted strings rather than decoding the entire file as text.",
	"windows.win9x_vxd_library":                "Builds a Windows 9x VxD library from a base and supplied members, excluding requested names. Returns the constructed file rather than installing drivers.",
	"windows.win9x_vxd_library_members":        "Lists the member names contained in a Windows 9x VxD library. It provides inventory without writing member files to the host.",
	"windows.win9x_vxd_unpack":                 "Decodes the supported compressed Windows 9x VxD container into an unpacked file. The result can be inspected or rebuilt without a host conversion tool.",
	"windows.wmi_repository":                   "Parses supplied repository files, builds an NT5 repository from MOF documents alone, or appends documents to supplied modern repository files. Modern append preserves the original object store, imports existing class layouts and defaults, rejects replacement identities, and rebuilds a checksummed index with three ordered mapping checkpoints. No WMI service or compiler is executed. Modern repositories expose class_layout(namespace, name) and instances(namespace, name); intrinsic classes resolve through __SYSTEMCLASS. Creating a namespace still requires explicit policy for its __NAMESPACE entry, __thisNAMESPACE security descriptor and __SystemSecurity singleton; parsing or appending MOF does not infer namespace security.",
	"write":                                    "Writes a binary value to the named host output file, subject to max_bytes. This materializes an explicit output; use in-memory files to connect construction stages.",
	"filesystem.ntfs_record":                   "Inspect one FILE record in a volume file without enumerating its namespace.\nThe native reader validates volume geometry, MFT bounds, sector fixups and\nattribute headers. It does not repair malformed attribute values or follow\nextension records. The result exposes `number`, `sequence`, `base_reference`,\n`flags`, `cluster_size`, `record_size`, fixed-up `raw` bytes, and `attributes`.\nEach attribute has `type`, `name`, `instance`, `flags`, `nonresident`, `size`,\n`first_vcn` and `header` bytes. Resident attributes expose `value`; nonresident\nones expose `allocated_size`, `initialized_size` and `runs` containing\n`cluster`, `count` and `sparse`. These are individual attribute extents, not\nautomatically reconstructed streams. This inspection remains usable when an\nunrelated invalid attribute list prevents a complete namespace read.",
	"http_post_json":                           "Sends a UTF-8 JSON string (or empty body) as application/json, returning a bounded in-memory file. Request body is limited to 1MiB; response maximum is 1..128MiB after transfer decompression. By default requires HTTP 200 and rejects oversized responses. response=True instead returns a frozen dictionary for any HTTP status with status, status_code, protocol, headers, trailers, content_length, transfer_encoding, uncompressed, body (file), body_truncated, read_error and complete. Header/trailer values are lists, preserving repeated fields. It reads at most maximum+1 decoded body bytes and retains a maximum-byte prefix; complete is false on truncation or read failure. Trailers may be incomplete unless complete is true. TLS responses additionally expose tls_alpn, tls_version and tls_server_name. http_version defaults to auto; 1.1 creates an isolated HTTP/1-only transport with HTTP/2 disabled and hostname/certificate verification unchanged. The returned protocol and TLS ALPN expose what was actually used. Transport/TLS failures still raise. Never follows redirects or retries; honors lifecycle cancellation and a 1..3600 second deadline. Response bodies may be gzip-decoded by HTTP; uncompressed identifies that transformation. headers optionally supplies at most 64 string-to-string fields, bounded to 64KiB. Names/control characters and case-insensitive duplicates are checked; Host, Content-Length, Transfer-Encoding, Connection, Content-Type and idempotency-key overrides are rejected. Empty User-Agent suppresses the generated header entirely rather than sending an empty value. No token acquisition or credential lookup is performed. tls_roots optionally supplies 1..16 DER/PEM CA files or bytes (up to 1MiB each); an isolated per-request root pool replaces system roots while preserving hostname, validity and chain verification. No global trust changes, implicit certificate fetching or insecure fallback. Intended for metadata; payloads should use file APIs.",
	"http_get":                                 "Performs one sequential HTTP(S) GET into an in-memory file, with normal redirect handling and HTTP gzip decoding. No HEAD/range requests, retries, disk cache or host intermediate files. Requires status200 and bounds response bytes after HTTP decompression to maximum (1..128MiB); timeout is 1..3600 seconds and lifecycle cancellation is honored. This is for bounded metadata; use http_file for large lazy payloads. It does not validate package signatures or digests.",
	"http_file":                                "Creates a lazy random-access file over HTTP byte ranges with a bounded in-memory chunk cache. urls supplies alternative source locations; omitted size uses HEAD discovery. This does not extract or convert the remote content.",
	"windows.registry_key_plan":                "Reduces immutable compact registry_key_records into separate system/user dictionaries of immutable mapping-like windows.registry_key_plan_node values. families is a list indexed by transition owner, with (nonnegative family bucket, before identity, after identity) tuples; identities are strings/bytes or None for absent sides. Every recorded side requires an identity. Processes stable (bucket, transition, declaration) order, rejects unequal descriptors at the same path/side/bucket, retains first ownership and collision count separately from last nonempty security, and builds implicit ancestors. value_changes maps (scope, lowercase NT path) to booleans for explicit keys; omitted keys mean unchanged and unknown/implicit keys fail. compile_security(SDDL) must return bytes using the target identity context; security_matches(before bytes or None, after bytes or None) must return a boolean with the asymmetric registry semantics. Both callbacks must be pure; compilation is interned by exact text and comparisons are memoized by ordered text pair per call. Identical nonempty texts bypass comparison. Computes operation/security/auxiliary flags and bottom-up subtree skips; does not assign security or mutate hives. Nodes support string-key indexing for path, parent, before_present, after_present, explicit, hive_boundary, owner, owned_addition_collisions, operation, security_flags, auxiliary_flags, descriptor, old_descriptor, subtree_changed. No per-contribution history or copied dictionaries are retained; use the noncompact collector plus inspection reducers when history is needed. Input families cap at 65536; expanded hierarchy caps at 1048576 nodes and 256 MiB of path/parent text. Errors return no partial plan. All collection validation and full value comparison remain caller prerequisites.",
	"windows.registry_contributions":           "Collects ordinary registry contributions from up to 65536 explicitly admitted manifest rows (dict(root=binary.xml_node, architecture=string, side=before/after, owner=integer 0..2^30)). Traverses asm.v1/v3 registryKeys and validates strict key/value attribute namespaces, keyName, value content, and local security definition/reference uniqueness and presence. Unknown key attributes and unrelated namespaces/children retain the inspection path semantics; this is not a complete manifest schema validator. Supports exact REG_NONE/SZ/EXPAND_SZ/BINARY/DWORD/MULTI_SZ/QWORD spellings, validates mutable/operationHint, ignores IEDownlevelValue data, and preserves raw UTF-16 and unquoted-empty MULTI_SZ provenance. resolve_key(key, architecture) supplies dict(scope, path); expand_string(text, architecture) is called only for macro-bearing names/string data. Both callbacks must be pure for one call, execute on the caller thread, and are memoized only within the call; input rows are captured before callbacks. Architecture policy is checked by the resolver when keys are encountered, not for empty managed assemblies. Returns dict(groups, keys), plus key_records when requested: groups maps (scope, lowercase NT path, lowercase name) to [before, after] lists of (owner, type, raw bytes, mutable, operationHint, unquoted_empty) tuples. Every contributor, duplicate and unchanged value survives in input order; no winner selection. key_prefix=None omits key capture; a string selects a case-insensitive literal NT prefix (empty selects all), with keys mapping (scope, lowercase path) to [first-spelled path, before_present, after_present]. The prefix filters only keys capture, never validation or values. capture_key_records=True separately retains all empty/nonempty key contributions grouped by owner, in input order, as (side, scope, resolved path, key-attribute dict, selected local SDDL or None) tuples. It does not select competing descriptors or interpret their policy. The default omits key_records. compact_key_records=True requires capture_key_records=True and returns immutable windows.registry_key_records instead of owner dictionaries; it validates supported key owner/virtualization/access attributes immediately and retains decoded path/side/owner/security fields without XML or attribute dictionaries. Use registry_key_plan for this representation; the default detailed inspection result is unchanged. max_records defaults/caps at 1048576 processed roots/keys/values/security-path records; max_bytes defaults/caps at 256 MiB of observed attribute/security text, resolved paths and decoded data (including expanded macro text). Each may be lowered, not disabled. Errors return no partial result. No hive/image writes, package admission, profile mounting, security assignment, trust verification, or CBS completion is implied.",
	"windows.registry_path_mapper":             "Compiles an owned, immutable registry redirection table supplied by the recipe. `rules` contains at most 4096 `(canonical_nt_machine_prefix, flags)` tuples; prefixes are at most 32768 bytes, matched case-insensitively at component boundaries. Flags 1 selects redirection and 2 marks an insertion anchor; 0 selects sharing. Longest matching prefix wins, and later equal prefixes replace earlier entries. The returned callable `(key, architecture)` yields a fresh dict with `scope` and canonical NT `path`. Architectures are amd64, x86, wow64. HKLM/HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE and HKCR/HKEY_CLASSES_ROOT use the table; amd64 preserves their physical paths. HKEY_CURRENT_USER is abstract user scope, not .DEFAULT; explicit HKEY_USERS/.DEFAULT is system scope. Other user roots, macros, NULs, empty interior components, and unsupported explicit WOW placements fail. Trailing declaration separators are removed. No host state or default OS rule table is used; hive mounting, CurrentControlSet resolution, virtualization, security and value expansion remain separate.",
	"windows.assembly_registry_data":           "Decodes CBS manifest text into native hive-writer values. Multi-strings use strict comma-separated quoted fields when the first character\nis a quote; otherwise the entire text is one string, including commas and whitespace.\nThere is no CSV quote escaping or separator whitespace trimming. Backslashes\nare literal and empty quoted entries contribute no strings. This decodes data,\nnot registry contribution/merge operations. QWORDs accept either\neight little-endian hexadecimal bytes or a `0x` numeric literal. Binary and resource-list data remain bytes. REG_NONE declaration text is ignored\nand decodes to empty bytes (including macro-like text). Eight-hex-digit numeric types (for example\n`FFFF0012` or `00040007`) decode hexadecimal data to raw bytes; the native hive\nwriter accepts these type spellings and preserves all 32 type bits unchanged.\nOptional string `variables` expand named\n`$(name)` macros case-insensitively in one pass; unknown used macros are errors.\nEnvironment-variable strings are unchanged. Runtime path policy is supplied\nby the image recipe, not inferred from the host.",
	"windows.container_index":                  "Returns compact CIX statistics and explicitly selected file records. Names and\nsource type select their union; optional `target_hash` (64 hexadecimal SHA-256\ndigits) narrows that selection, or selects equal-content aliases by itself.\nAt most `limit` records are returned (0–1000); input remains in memory.",
	"windows.delta_apply":                      "Reconstructs native PA30/PA31 bytes with mandatory source and target hash\nverification. `maximum` bounds each input and the decoded target; an explicit\nvalue up to 128 MiB supports large Defender definition records without changing\nthe default 64 MiB bound.",
	"windows.delta_info":                       "Inspects PA30/PA31 headers and bounded preprocessing data. `pe_preprocess`\ncontains native address maps and, when present, `managed`: CLI metadata extent,\nstreams (`#Strings`, `#US`, `#Blob`, `#GUID`, `#~`), table row counts, and\nheap/table maps. Inspection does not imply that every recorded transform is\nsupported by apply. Managed apply supports metadata RVA/index normalization,\nsignature-token remapping and heap/table copy-coordinate maps. Nonidentity\nmethod-body tokens are normalized in bounded tiny/fat IL bodies; metadata\ncolumn-width changes use source-cell copy mappings. `maximum` bounds the record\nread and may be raised explicitly to 128 MiB for large records; it does not bypass\nPSF framing checks.",
	"windows.delta_trace":                      "Inspects the selected reconstructed byte range before PE restoration, without\nenforcing the target digest. Source identity and input bounds remain checked.\nThis is a diagnostic API, not an image-construction replacement for\n`windows.delta_apply`. LZMS-backed direct payloads report literal spans; nested binary deltas report\nclipped `add` spans with signed source offsets and literal insertion spans.\nAdditive spans may reference bytes outside the source, which contribute zero.\nThese are binary-delta spans, not LZMS compression matches. Set\n`pe_transforms=False` to omit PE normalization events for broad byte comparisons.\nSupply both prepared-source bounds to also return `prepared_source`, a copy\nof that source window after preprocessing and before patch matching, plus\n`prepared_source_start`. The window must be nonempty and within the input\nsource; it is `None` when omitted. These bytes are diagnostic, not a verified\ntarget.\nWith `inspect_signatures=True`, a prepared-source range is required and\n`source_signatures` lists overlapping original CLI signature consumers in\nnormalization order, including shared blobs. Each record contains `table`,\none-based `row`, `blob_index`, and payload `offset`/`size` excluding the blob\nlength prefix. Without this option the list is empty.",
	"windows.dmr_alternate_path":               "Encodes an alternate package path in a DMR section, optionally using the first-package-family form. Paths describe guest package state, not host output files.",
	"windows.dmr_application_resources":        "Serializes one indexed application's display, description, logo and optional start-page reference files into a DMR resource section. It does not infer resource choices.",
	"windows.dmr_applications":                 "Encodes declared application records, capabilities and a source flag into a DMR applications section. This serializes the supported application metadata without executing registration callbacks.",
	"windows.dmr_container":                    "Combines ordered section files into a bounded DMR envelope. Format and extent validation do not establish that the caller supplied every section required for a valid Windows package graph.",
	"windows.dmr_globalization":                "Encodes an application's UTF-8 and Windows-display-language settings into a DMR globalization section. The application identity and policy are supplied by the caller.",
	"windows.dmr_graph":                        "Serializes an already selected, ordered package dependency graph into a DMR section with bounded node count. Package identity, paths and flags remain caller-supplied; it does not perform dependency resolution.",
	"windows.dmr_mutable_paths":                "Encodes an ordered, bounded nonempty list of mutable package paths into a DMR section. It does not create directories or change permissions.",
	"windows.dmr_package_resources":            "Serializes package resource references into a DMR section. Inputs are bounded encoded reference files; description=None preserves an absent description rather than an empty reference.",
	"windows.dmr_package_security":             "Serializes declared package SID, ARI flags, inbox status and capabilities into a DMR security section. Encoding these fields neither verifies signatures nor establishes package trust.",
	"windows.dmr_target_platform":              "Encodes a target-platform DMR section from a native device-family enum and version bounds. The family value is not a StateRepository database row ID; -1 represents the native unknown enum.",
	"windows.dmr_trailer":                      "Returns the fixed native DMR trailer section as an in-memory file for container composition.",
	"windows.kernel_triage_dump":               "Reads completed AMD64 PAGE/DU64 type-4 kernel triage dumps without a host debugger. Returns header, context (1232 AMD64 CONTEXT bytes), pc/sp/fp, stack (borrowed file), stack_address, modules (name/base/size/checksum/timestamp), and regions (virtual address plus borrowed file). Regions preserve captured ranges independently, including overlaps; this does not synthesize absent RAM or walk page tables. The triage directory and TRGD completion marker must fit the declared triage extent, itself bounded to 64 MiB and contained in RequiredDumpSpace and the input. Bounds counts before reading tables: at most 4096 drivers, 65536 memory blocks, 1 MiB stack and 32767 UTF-16 code units per name. Secondary streams after the triage extent are not interpreted. No symbol download, automatic stack unwinding or driver attribution is implied; stack words alone are not frames. Borrowed files require the input lifetime. Other dump types and malformed/incomplete ranges fail.",
	"windows.kernel_dump_header":               "Reads only the fixed 8 KiB Windows PAGE/DU64 kernel dump header, including\none still stored at the start of a guest pagefile. Returns `bugcheck_code`,\n`bugcheck_parameters`, `dump_type`, `required_size`, `writer_status`,\n`major_version`, `minor_version`, `machine`, `processor_count`,\n`directory_table_base`, `pfn_database`, `loaded_module_list`,\n`active_process_head`, and `debugger_data_block`.\n`extent_available` means the header-declared extent is at least 8 KiB and\nfits inside the input; it does not validate the body or prove that the writer\ncompleted. Other dump signatures are rejected. No guest execution or host\ndebugger is used. This is distinct from the MDMP user-mode minidump reader.",
	"windows.mrm_index_reference":              "Encodes a supplied resource index as an MRM resource-reference file. The caller must resolve the correct index from package metadata.",
	"windows.mrm_literal_reference":            "Encodes a literal string as a native MRM resource-reference file for composition into DMR resource sections.",
	"windows.mrm_missing_file_reference":       "Encodes the native missing-file resource-reference form using a package path and value. It represents that condition without inventing or creating the referenced file.",
	"windows.pri_resource_candidates":          "Returns structural candidate counts and schema information for selected resource indexes in a PRI resource-map section. It does not choose candidates or decode their values.",
	"windows.pri_schema":                       "Parses a selected PRI schema section into its hierarchy and resource item names. The section index is explicit; parsing does not select locale-specific resource candidates.",
	"windows.pri_sections":                     "Parses a bounded PRI container envelope and returns section identities, metadata and payload files. Section discovery alone does not resolve or render resources.",
	"windows.servicing_graph":                  "Builds a native cumulative-update dependency graph from PSF and history indexes and a supplied PSF payload. Optional targets restrict inspected nodes; the result describes dependencies rather than applying an update to a running system.",
	"windows.servicing_plan":                   "Plans cumulative-update assembly stages against a supplied WIM image or declared installed package/component inventory. Returns bounded stage summaries without running Windows servicing or installing the updates.",
	"windows.sam_alias_members":                "Decodes the binary SID vector in an NT5 SAM alias C value supplied as bytes or a portable file, bounded to 16 MiB. Validates header and payload extents, SID encodings and member counts. Does not resolve host accounts or select membership policy.",
	"windows.sam_alias_with_members":           "Returns an NT5 SAM alias C value with its member SID vector replaced, preserving unrelated record fields. Accepts a source value bounded to 16 MiB and a list of checked binary SIDs; rejects duplicates. The caller must update the SAM reverse-membership indexes and counts in the same hive construction operation.",
	"windows.sid_string":                       "Validates one complete revision-1 binary SID (at most 15 subauthorities) and returns its Windows string form, with a hexadecimal authority when it is at least 2^32. Accepts bytes or a file; no host identity resolution is performed.",
	"windows.state_repository_dictionary_read": "Decodes a file or bytes containing a complete SRD1 dictionary into ordered Starlark dictionaries, booleans, strings and lists of dictionaries. Uses the strict native decoder; rejects unknown types, duplicate keys, truncated extents and excessive depth. Input is limited to 64 MiB before allocation. Property identifiers are preserved, not interpreted.",
	"windows.state_repository_dictionary":      "Encodes an ordered dictionary as native SRD1 bytes, returned as an immutable\nfile. Values may be strings, nested dictionaries, or lists of dictionaries;\nother types are rejected. These are wire primitives for AppX XML properties,\nnot an automatic manifest registration operation. For example,\n`{\"LaunchPolicy\": {\"#text\": \"1\"}}` retains the nested text property.\n\nLimits: 1024 entries per dictionary/list, 32 nesting levels, 65536 aggregate\nnodes/entries during input conversion, 65534 UTF-16 key bytes, 1 MiB per\nstring/nested-map payload, and 64 MiB per complete dictionary. Strings are\nterminated UTF-16LE; embedded NULs, invalid Unicode and cycles are rejected.",
	"windows.state_repository_hash":            "Computes native StateRepository hash_base32 for a list of NULL, int64, string or bytes values. SHA-256 consumes concatenated LE64 integers, ASCII-lowercased UTF-16LE strings up to their first NUL (without a terminator), and raw bytes; NULL and empty values contribute nothing. Returns 52 unpadded characters using Windows' base32 alphabet. At most 1024 values and 64 MiB of input; floats, booleans and invalid Unicode are rejected.",
	"windows.update_catalog":                   "Defaults to local-only replay of cached raw catalog responses for the exact caller profile. network=True explicitly synchronizes Windows Update and atomically refreshes this source cache. Missing, damaged or mismatched cached metadata fails without network fallback; cached offers are not a current/latest-offer guarantee. Payload readers inherit the catalog network policy, including lazy reads after boot. Uses explicit device_attributes and caller_attributes string dictionaries, products string, locales list, user_agent, and assume_non_leaf_installed policy. No OS, build, architecture, ring, locale, eligibility or product defaults are supplied. Empty attribute dictionaries are allowed; all arguments are required. Returns an immutable windows_update_catalog with offers (all revisions, including non-leaves), rounds, revision_count and leaf_count. Each offer exposes id, revision, server_id, is_leaf, title, product_release, deployment, update_type, files (name, size, sha1, sha256), prerequisites and bundled_updates. Relationship clauses expose alternatives (id/revision) and is_category. There is no hidden result truncation, preview/client filter or ranking. Select an offer in Starlark and pass it with this catalog to update_media. Catalog discovery downloads metadata only, not payloads or disk images. Optional background=True starts exactly one local replay or explicit synchronization and returns a windows_update_catalog_request; result() waits for the same immutable catalog or original failure without retrying. close() cancels and joins unfinished work; completed results remain readable. Execution cleanup cancels and joins requests automatically. Profile data is copied before launching; no Starlark callbacks run concurrently.",
	"windows.update_media":                     "Prepares an explicitly selected catalog offer as referenced UUP media, not a complete disk image. catalog must come from update_catalog, and offer must be an original offer from that same catalog. architecture, edition, language, metadata_name (exact aggregated metadata payload name) and target_compdb (composition database name/source) are required caller choices. There is no rescan, fallback offer, ranking or client/preview filter. Dependency closure and signed-location renewal retain the original catalog, request profile, selected revision, declared hashes and sizes. Inspection modes expose metadata or plans without full image construction; servicing uses mandatory reconstructed target hashes. TinyRangeX recipes apply those effects and construct the filesystem, registry and bootable disk.\n\nAn optional `base` supplies an existing `archive.wim` instead of downloading a\nnew base image. It requires an explicit `/imageN` root and a nonempty `bootsect`\nfile; the selected image must contain SOFTWARE, SYSTEM and COMPONENTS hives.\nThe returned `image` preserves that selection. This path skips only full-media\nhydration, not update dependency or reconstructed-content validation. It can\nrecover pruned checkpoint bases from the installed component's reverse delta,\nexpand DCS/LZMS component-store files, and reuse unchanged files from the same\ncomponent family only when their declared length and SHA-256 match.\n\n**External-base support is currently inspection-only.** Supply `index_only`,\n`plan_only`, `metadata_only`, or `state_only`; ordinary construction is rejected.\nArchitecture/release compatibility must still be checked by the caller. File\nreconstruction and registry-effect enumeration do not implement the complete\nCBS ownership/installed-state transaction or all component-manifest actions.\nNeither `validate_servicing` nor `servicing_effects` proves that an update is\ninstalled, that a resulting image boots, or that all security updates are\npresent. Do not substitute a build-number patch or first-boot servicing for\nthose missing native construction semantics.\n\nThe optional `cache` selects the persistent source-range directory for this call (relative paths resolve against the working directory). An empty value preserves the native default. No process-wide environment is changed. Only original source bytes are cached, not constructed images.\n\nThe native backend retains downloaded source-media ranges across runs in\n`\u003cos.UserCacheDir()\u003e/tinyrangex/uup` (normally\n`~/.cache/tinyrangex/uup` on Linux; honors `XDG_CACHE_HOME`). This disk cache\nhas no automatic capacity eviction and grows only as source ranges are read.\nThe separate 256 MiB RAM hot cache may evict entries without repeating HTTP.\nDecoded source-WIM chunks share a separate lazy 2 GiB RAM ceiling across the\nreference ESDs. Windows 11 uses 64 MiB solid chunks; even its initial font set\nexceeds the generic archive cache. This is independent of the 6 GiB compressed\nserviced-file cache. Initially verified servicing outputs enter that bounded\ncache as immutable compressed bytes; cache hits reuse them, while evicted files\nare reconstructed and hash-verified again on demand.\nBudget these ceilings together with guest memory when\nlimiting a native smoke run; unused cache capacity is not allocated eagerly.\nCache identity includes the declared SHA-256, payload size and byte range,\nnot the signed URL or filename. Atomic publication and cross-process locks\nprevent partial cache hits and duplicate simultaneous downloads. Cached ranges\nare checksummed on disk reads; invalid entries or cache I/O failures stop with\nan error instead of silently fetching again. Range checksums do not replace\nmandatory reconstructed-file hash verification. New source ranges, failed\ndownloads and update discovery/location renewal can still require network\nrequests. Only source bytes are persisted, not parsed or constructed stages.\n\nThe returned source exposes `validate_servicing(collect_errors=False,\nerror_limit=16, feature_id='', on_error=None, source_names=None)`. It reconstructs and fully reads each planned installed-OS\nservicing file, retaining strict delta hash verification. By default the first\nfailure aborts validation. With `collect_errors=True`, validation continues and\nreturns per-stage `ok`, `verified_count`, `failed_count`, and a bounded `errors`\nlist of `{feature_id, name, error}` records, alongside `feature_id`, `file_count`, and\nsuccessfully verified `bytes`. `error_limit` must be between 1 and 1000; it\nlimits reported examples, not the number of files checked. Collection is for\ninspection and does not permit failed bytes into image construction.\nAn optional `feature_id` restricts inspection to one installed-OS stage; an\nunknown feature is an error.\nOptional nonempty `source_names` requires `feature_id`, selects exact logical\nsource names case-insensitively, and rejects unmatched names. All matching plan\neffects remain in their original order; counts cover only the selected effects.\nWith collection enabled, `on_error(record)` optionally receives each retained\nfailure immediately (at most `error_limit` calls per stage); callback errors\nabort inspection. It is useful for bounded REPL observations during a long scan.\n`servicing_only=True` with index_only and an explicit base returns only the full servicing_metadata, open_resident_component and open_resident_components interfaces plus source identity fields. The batch `open_resident_components(identities, workers=4)` accepts a list of at most 65536 exact identity bytes, preserves order and unavailable None entries, and validates each manifest exactly as the single-reader method. Workers are bounded to 1..8; errors join all native workers and return no partial list. No Starlark callbacks run on workers. `source.servicing_metadata(feature_id).open_packages(packages, workers=4)` opens at most 4096 exact package names on 1..8 native workers, preserving order and duplicates as fresh owned parsed snapshots. Every MUM receives the same identity and package-declaration validation as open_package. All workers join before an error is returned; the lowest failing input determines the error and no partial list escapes. It does not infer admission or prune unavailable references. `source.servicing_metadata(feature_id).plan_components(identities, manifests=None)` plans exact store-only components. Optional `manifests` is an equally sized list of immutable files previously returned by that same metadata index's `open_component`; it reuses their validated snapshots rather than reopening/reparsing source metadata. A foreign index, ordinary equal-byte file, mismatched identity, duplicate component, or wrong length fails without a partial plan. This carries source provenance, not admission, catalog trust or installation authorization. No additional parsed-manifest cache is retained. The returned staging plan exposes `cache_stats()` for its bounded decoded-payload cache: hits, misses, loads, loaded_bytes, load_nanoseconds, evictions, retained_bytes, peak_retained_bytes and entries. load_nanoseconds sums loader wall time (including failures); concurrent loads overlap, while hits and single-flight waits are excluded. Each call returns an independent read-only snapshot; observing it does not reconstruct files, evict content or authorize installation. Retaining only this callable does not retain the staging inventories. It binds the same ordered reconstruction stages but omits preliminary CSI ownership/effect plans that authoritative recipe admission replaces. It cannot combine with other inspection modes or a stage limit, does not prune metadata, and does not provide installed-state or boot-media proof. Optional `while_preparing` (servicing_only only) runs once with no arguments on the caller interpreter thread beside native source preparation. It must not mutate the shared immutable WIM, source readers, or catalog. Its return is ignored; callback errors and native failures return no source, after joining the worker. Progress is bounded and delivered on the caller thread after joining. This is overlapping construction, not deferred integrity verification.\n\n`index_only=True` skips canonical CAB resource hydration. Missing bases in\nthat mode are not by themselves decoder failures; complete image validation\nrequires fully hydrated media (`index_only=False`). An external WIM already\ncontains its own base resources, but its inspection result is still not\nproduction boot media.\n\n`source.servicing_effects(include_files=False)` inspects planned registry values and key declarations\nwithout reconstructing or retaining servicing files. Its `files` list is empty;\nthe default `include_files=True` also returns verified file effects. These are\nconstruction primitives, not a completed CBS transaction.\n`registry_keys` contains ordered records with `key`, `architecture`, and\n`feature_id`, independently of `registry_values`. Empty CBS index keys must\nbe created as keys, not as invented default values.\nEach registry effect includes `operation_hint` from the manifest (empty when\nabsent). Preserve this field when translating effects to hive patches:\n`operationHint=\"append\"` contributes members to an existing `REG_MULTI_SZ`\nthrough the native patch writer's `append=True`; it must not replace the value.\n`operationHint=\"prepend\"` uses `prepend=True`: contribution members precede\nexisting members, preserving contribution order and removing case-insensitive\nduplicates. Reapplying the contribution is idempotent. Both values must be\n`REG_MULTI_SZ`; prepend cannot be combined with another patch operation.\n\n`source.inspect_servicing_files(name=\"\", destination_prefix=\"\", limit=100)`\nreturns bounded planned file metadata without opening payloads. Supply an exact\nsource/store basename or a destination prefix; both selectors are case-insensitive,\nnormalize slash direction, and form a union. Results preserve stage/plan order.\n`files` contains feature ID, architecture, source name/mode, store path and\ndestinations; `total` counts all matches and `truncated` reports omitted matches.\n`limit` is 0–1000. This is placement inspection, not reconstruction or hash proof.\n\n`source.inspect_servicing_packages(feature_id=\"\", offset=0, limit=100)`\nreturns selected package manifest metadata in stage/file-plan order. Records in\n`packages` contain feature_id, package (canonical CBS identity), source_name and\nstore_path; the latter opens with `open_servicing_file`. Aggregate update.mum\ntransport names are not CBS identities. Optional feature matching is\ncase-insensitive; unknown features are errors. `total`, `next_offset` and\n`has_more` support pagination, with nonnegative offset and limit 0–1000. This\nlists the selected closure, not final CBS states or all transport MUMs.\n\n`source.inspect_servicing_components(feature_id=\"\", name_prefix=\"\", offset=0, limit=100)`\nreturns selected component metadata without opening payloads. Optional feature\nand component-name prefix filters are case-insensitive and intersect. Unknown\nfeatures are errors. `components` records contain feature_id, name, version,\narchitecture, language, stem, previous_stem, store_path, staged_only and selected_by.\n`store_path` is usable with `open_servicing_file`. A nonempty `previous_stem`\nidentifies the planner's latest known predecessor, possibly from an earlier\nstage; it does not by itself prove that predecessor is the active runtime winner.\nResults preserve stage/plan order. `total` counts matches, `next_offset` and\n`has_more` support pagination. Offset must be nonnegative; limit is 0–1000.\nA zero limit counts matches without advancing. This is inspection only, not\nproof of installed ownership, completed actions or successful servicing.\n\n`source.inspect_servicing_ownership(feature_id=\"\", deployment_name=\"\", offset=0, limit=100)`\nreturns planned deployment/component ownership edges, including unchanged resident\ncomponents. Optional exact deployment-name and feature filters intersect and are\ncase-insensitive. Unknown features are errors. `edges` records contain feature_id,\ndeployment, deployment_version, deployment_architecture, active, source_count,\ncomponent and component_stem. `active` describes the deployment's sources, not\nwhether a shared component is active through another deployment. Pagination is\nover edges, so large deployments remain bounded; `total`, `next_offset`,\n`has_more`, and offset/limit semantics match component inspection. This is an\nownership plan, not proof that CBS registry changes or component actions ran.\n\n`source.servicing_plan` also reports csi_component_count, csi_deployment_count and\ncsi_owner_count. These count exact component/owner records and catalog-backed\ncanonical deployment descriptions. Every deployment root hash must occur in\nevery source package catalog. This verifies membership, not certificate trust,\nand does not apply the planned CBS records or complete servicing.",
	"windows.wow64_registry_key":               "Maps a 32-bit logical key to its physical AMD64 registry path using Windows 7\nand later shared/redirected-key rules. Explicit WOW6432Node paths are not\nredirected again. It does not implement older registry reflection semantics.",
}

